/* ÇEVİRİ en · ekokuma — arac/ceviri.py ile YAZILDI, elle düzenleme: py arac/ceviri.py uygula.
   Kayıt: id → [metin, kaynak_ozet] (olayda alan başına). Tasarım: denetim/CEVIRI-1103/TASARIM.md */
Ceviri.ekle("en","ekokuma",{
"dunya4-rusyanin-denize-cikisi-sicak-denizler":{"baslik": ["Russia had three seas — all three either froze or were locked. The Black Sea was not a fourth option but the only one.", "caf3b94b"], "metin": ["The Baltic — it was there, but inadequate. Russia's outlet to the Baltic Sea by way of Petersburg was settled by the 1721 Treaty of Nystad (the end of the Great Northern War against Sweden) — so there really was an obstacle, but it was political, not geographical: the road was open, but Sweden was closing it, and when Sweden was defeated the road opened. Nevertheless, the Baltic itself is not ideal either: the port of Petersburg freezes for a few months in winter, and the way out has to pass through the Danish Straits (even after Sweden) — that is, even with the political obstacle removed, the Baltic was not a geographically perfect solution.\n\nThe WHITE Sea/the northern Arctic Ocean — open but useless. The outlet to the Arctic Ocean through Arkhangelsk had in fact been open historically (since the 16th century), and no enemy held it — but the fact that it was covered with ice for much of the year and lay very far from the commercial centres of Europe (the Mediterranean and Atlantic ports) made it strategically worthless. Therefore the answer here to the question posed by the entry text, \"was there an obstacle\", is 'no, but it was useless anyway' — that is, the problem was not access but the worthlessness of that access.\n\nKAMCHATKA/the Pacific — there, but very far away. Russia had already reached the Pacific Ocean in 1639 (including the Kamchatka region, consolidated by the exploration of the Bering Strait in 1728) — so an outlet to the Pacific was never a real problem; geographically it was already there. But it was a region thousands of kilometres from European Russia (Moscow, Petersburg) and almost unreachable in winter; it could never become Russia's main commercial or military sea (even the sale of Alaska to the USA in 1867 is an indication of the strategic unimportance of this remote region).\n\nWHY a warm sea in particular. The TDV article `rusya` explicitly frames the matter not as an obsession but as a geographical necessity: 'a requirement of its geography, shut off from the warm seas.' None of the three seas Russia had (the Baltic — freezes + politically obstructed; the White Sea — freezes + remote; the Pacific — does not freeze but extremely remote) offered a commercial and naval base that was usable all year round and close to Europe. The Black Sea (and from there the Mediterranean) was the only option that met both conditions (freedom from ice + proximity to Europe) — this is why Russia kept up systematic pressure against the Ottomans for 75 years (from Azov in 1696 to Küçük Kaynarca in 1774).\n\nThe point of DEBATE. This card's own assessment: the answer to the question posed by the entry text, 'was there an obstacle on the Petersburg side', is nuanced — before 1721, yes (Sweden); after 1721, no, but geography (freezing + the Danish Straits) was still limiting. That is, Russia's turn to the Black Sea arose not merely 'because it had been blocked', but from the fact that even after the Baltic was opened, the Black Sea was objectively a better option — this distinction is not given in TDV in an explicit sentence; it is this card's own synthesis.", "a42daac6"], "kisa": ["Even Petersburg's own harbour is closed by ice for a few months of the year; Kamchatka is at the other end of the world; the Black Sea, by contrast, was both warm and the gateway closest to Europe — this is the geographical root of Russia's 75-year obsession.", "3917a55f"], "bag": ["When the facts given by TDV (the removal of the political obstacle on the Baltic in 1721, yet the continuation of the search for the Black Sea still as 'a geographical necessity') are read together, it emerges that the question was not only 'is there an obstacle or not' but 'which sea is better' — this interpretation is not a direct sentence of TDV but the synthesis offered by this card.", "a2c6720b"]},
"dunya4-vahran-mersalkebir-1708":{"baslik": ["Oran and Mers el-Kebir (1708) — two ports in Spanish hands", "61f18825"], "metin": ["Importance — why these two cities mattered. According to the TDV article `vehran`, Oran (Vahran) was one of the most important port cities on the Mediterranean coast of North Africa and the natural gateway of Tlemcen, the great commercial centre of the interior. Its geographical position was also strategic: a point protected by mountains, between Morocco and Spain, right on the trade routes of the western Mediterranean. Adjoining it, Mers el-Kebir was Oran's natural harbour and naval base — together the two were one of the rare points in the western Mediterranean where a fleet could anchor and resupply.\n\nWHY they could not be taken UNTIL 1708. Spain took these two cities on 17 May 1509 (with Castilian forces) — in the striking detail given by TDV, a large part of the population was massacred and the rest enslaved; the Muslim and Jewish population was in effect eliminated. When Algiers itself came under Ottoman rule in 1516 (at the hands of the Barbarossa brothers), Oran was already in Spanish hands and remained so — for nearly two centuries. The reason is the same pattern seen again and again in this project: Oran was a fortress fed from the sea (like the last fortresses of Candia, Corfu and Malta) — as long as Spain had a strong fleet in the western Mediterranean, it could regularly send troops and provisions there; the strength of the Regency of Algiers, on the other hand, lay mainly in corsairing and sea raids, and devoting sustained resources to a great combined land and sea siege operation was not among its priorities. TDV also adds that throughout these two centuries the Spanish garrison struggled with Berber (native Algerian) attacks, famine and epidemic disease — that is, the city was never at ease, but neither did it fall.\n\nIts CAPTURE (4 April 1708). After a long siege, the Ottoman forces of Algiers under the command of Mustafa b. Yusuf Bey took the city; TDV specifically notes that this caused 'great joy' in Istanbul — that is, it was a victory noticed even in the capital of the day and perceived as the closing of a two-century-old question.\n\nThe AFTERMATH — it was not permanent. Ottoman rule did not prove permanent either: during this period of twenty-four years (in TDV's words) the trade of the port revived, but in 1732 Spain retook the city. Oran did not again remain fully in Algerian hands (until the moment in 1792 when Spain evacuated it of its own accord and handed it over to Algiers); and in 1831 it was occupied again, this time by the French. That is, the fate of these two cities did not end with a single victory in 1708 — it followed a history with five changes of hands: 1509 (Spain) → 1708 (Ottomans/Algiers) → 1732 (Spain) → 1792 (handover to Algiers) → 1831 (France).", "6ce6728d"], "kisa": ["The Ottomans established themselves in Algiers in 1516; although these two port cities stood right on the doorstep, they went on remaining Spanish for 192 years — because they too, just like Candia and Corfu, were fortresses fed from the sea.", "303edf6f"]},
"dunya5-demirbas-sarl-bender-kalabalik":{"baslik": ["Demirbaş Charles at Bender — the Swedish king who was the guest of the Ottomans", "2bb20820"], "metin": ["Who he was. Charles XII (\"Demirbaş Şarl\" in Turkish sources — a nickname arising from his obstinacy and refusal to budge from his place) was the king who came to the throne at the age of fifteen in 1697, made Sweden for a short time the greatest military power in northern Europe, but in the end caused it to lose that power entirely. In the summary of the TDV article `isvec`, after his early victories against Russia in the Great Northern War (1700-1721), his reign became, with the heavy defeat he suffered against Tsar Peter I at Poltava (in present-day Ukraine) in 1709, a turning point marking the end of Sweden's status as a great power.\n\nTaking refuge on OTTOMAN soil. Fleeing from Poltava, Charles made first for Perevolochna and from there (the Tatars having left him on his own) headed for Ochakov (Özi) instead of the Crimea, and came under Ottoman protection. The body of Swedish refugees, which began as a single group, grew from about 1,000 people to 10,000 as they poured into Bender in the six months following Poltava — that is, Charles did not come alone; he brought with him a small army and a court.\n\nThree years AT BENDER. Charles had a house built for himself at a place called Varnitsa, outside the fortress of Bender, and lived there from 1709 to 1711 — according to the TDV article `bender-kalesi`, he officially lived not in the fortress but in this small settlement that he himself had founded. Throughout this time he ceaselessly sent envoys to Istanbul to press for a new Ottoman campaign against Russia; this pressure played a part, to some extent, in the Ottoman declaration of war on Russia in 1710 (see the card `savas-prut-1711`) and in the Pruth campaign of 1711. But Baltacı Mehmed Pasha's making peace at the Pruth without destroying the Russian army (the crushing of Russia was precisely what Charles wanted most) disappointed the king and strained Ottoman-Swedish relations.\n\nThe KALABALIK (Skirmish) Incident (1-12 February 1713). After the peace of the Pruth, the Ottomans wanted to rid themselves of this 'long-staying and expensive guest', who no longer had any political value, and asked Charles to return to his country — the king refused. An armed clash broke out between the men of İsmail Pasha, the warden of Bender, and Charles's small guard; TDV confirms this but gives no details. According to the standard historical account (citing Akdes Nimet Kurat — outside TDV, verified with an academic source), Charles, with a guard of about forty men, held out for seven days against a far more numerous Ottoman-Tatar force that surrounded him; the fire arrows shot by the Ottoman archers set the house ablaze, a few Janissaries who tried to get in through the windows were killed, and in the end the king was forced to come out. The most famous detail told is this: seeing that his men had surrendered, Charles tried to go back into the house, caught his foot on the threshold and fell, with half his body inside and half outside — and was captured in this state. The outcome confirmed by TDV is certain: after being held in custody for a few days at the Pasha's mansion, the king was sent to Edirne.\n\nThe AFTERMATH. When it became clear that Ottoman support would no longer be forthcoming, Charles was allowed to return to Sweden in 1714, and he crossed Europe on horseback, alone and concealing his identity, and reached his country — when he came he had brought a small army and a court; when he left he was all alone. The debts he accumulated in Ottoman territory (the expenses of himself and his retinue) could not be paid for years; TDV records this explicitly, saying that 'the debts he incurred in Ottoman lands had to be repaid over many years'.", "f6a5a14b"], "kisa": ["For three years a European king, as the guest of the Ottomans, set up a small 'state within a state' at Bender — and when he refused to leave, he was besieged along with his own house.", "ac69d389"]},
"dunya6-iran-turk-hanedanlari-afsar-zend-ayrimi":{"baslik": ["The Turkic dynasties that ruled Iran — and the single exception wedged among them", "1193cc47"], "metin": ["Who the Afshars were. The Afshar (Avşar) are one of the Oghuz tribes, also considered ancestors of the Anatolian Turks; they were a Turkmen community that spread over a wide geography including Khuzistan, Anatolia, northern Syria and Urmia, and left their trace in place names in the Ottoman tax registers of the fifteenth to seventeenth centuries. The founder of the dynasty, Nader (originally Tahmasp Qoli Khan), was a soldier and commander from this tribe: he first tried to revive the collapsing Safavid dynasty (in the name of Tahmasp II and Abbas III), defeated the Afghans and the Ottomans, and in 1736 declared himself independent shah, founding the Afsharid dynasty. His siege of Mosul, repulsed by the Ottoman defense (1743), and his reconfirmation of the Qasr-e Shirin frontier in the 1746 Treaty of Kerden are his most concrete traces in Ottoman history. Afsharid rule lasted from 1736 to 1796 (in practice breaking apart after Nader was killed in 1747).\n\nThe dynasties of Turkic/Turkmen origin that ruled IRAN — the full list. Following the chronological account in TDV's article `iran`: ① the Ghaznavids (963-1186) — rule stretching from Persian Iraq (Irak-ı Acem) to India; ② the Seljuks — defeated the Ghaznavids at Dandanakan (1040) and united all of Iran, from the Jazira to Transoxiana, under one roof; ③ the Ilkhanids — a dynasty of Mongol origin but rapidly Turkified, founded by Genghis Khan's grandson Hülegü; it ruled most of present-day Iran, Azerbaijan, Anatolia and Iraq for about a century; ④ the Jalayirids (1340-1431) and ⑤ the Qara Qoyunlu — a rivalry over the Ilkhanid legacy, centered essentially on Azerbaijan; ⑥ the Timurids — Timur's empire, which after his death continued under successors such as Shahrukh and Abu Said Mirza; ⑦ the Aq Qoyunlu — under Uzun Hasan, they added Persian Iraq and Fars and came to dominate the region; ⑧ the Safavids (1501-1736) — the dynasty founded by Shah Ismail, relying on its Kizilbash Turkmen supporters of Anatolian origin; ⑨ the Afsharids (1736-1796) — described above; ⑩ the Qajars (1785/1794-1925) — the last dynasty of Turkic origin, founded by Agha Mohammad Khan of the Turkmen tribes, which made Tehran the capital.\n\nThe ZAND were not Turkic — and this exception matters. The Zand dynasty (1751-1794), wedged between the Afsharids and the Qajars, stands outside this chain: according to TDV's article `zendler`, the Zand were a tribe attached to the Lak, a sub-branch of the Lur — settled in the part of the Zagros Mountains between Hamadan and Isfahan. The text makes no reference to any Turkic or Kurdish origin for this tribe; the source's own classification is explicitly Lur. The dynasty's best-known ruler, Karim Khan Zand, seized power in 1751 with the title of 'regent' (vakil) in the name of the last Safavid shadow ruler, Ismail III, made Shiraz the capital in 1766 and maintained a relatively just rule (in TDV's words) until 1779. After his death four successors in turn (Abu'l-Fath, Sadiq, Ali Murad, Jafar Khan) had short reigns; when the last ruler, Lotf Ali Khan, was killed in December 1794, the Qajar dynasty came to power. Thus in the eight-century chain of dynasties of Turkic/Turkmen origin, the Zand are the single clear interruption, lasting forty-three years (1751-1794).\n\nThe Turkic population in IRAN. According to TDV's article `iran`, about 20% of Iran's present-day population speaks Turkic (those of Persian origin are the largest group at about 50%, and the rest are Kurds, Armenians, Baluch, Afghans and other minorities); the Turkic population is concentrated mainly in the northwest of the country (the Azerbaijan region) and in border areas far from the center. TDV also gives the historical origin of this population: when the last Mongol remnants in Azerbaijan also became Turkified at the end of the 8th (Hijri) century, the ethnic balance of western Iran shifted permanently in favor of the Turks — in other words, today's Azeri Turkic population is the direct demographic legacy of the list of dynasties above (especially of the post-Ilkhanid period).", "adbde52b"], "kisa": ["From the Ghaznavids to the Qajars, for eight centuries almost all the dynasties that sat on the throne of Iran were of Turkic or Turkmen origin — the one striking exception is the forty-three-year Zand period, wedged between the Afsharids and the Qajars.", "5b3d77ad"]},
"dunya6-vehhabiler-kimdir-ve-arabistan-sinirlari":{"baslik": ["Who were the Wahhabis, and which corners of Arabia did the Ottomans never enter?", "031ec1a8"], "metin": ["Who the Wahhabis were. The founder of the movement, Muhammad b. Abd al-Wahhab (1703-1792), was the son of a Hanbali qadi in the town of Uyayna in Najd; when the opposition around him intensified, he migrated to Diriyah in 1745. The alliance he formed there with the local emir Muhammad b. Saud was the movement's decisive moment: the emir provided the political and military power, and Ibn Abd al-Wahhab the religious legitimacy — this partnership is the foundation of the Saudi dynasty's power, which lasts to this day. The core of the doctrine is a strict emphasis on tawhid (the oneness of God); its followers regard practices such as Sufism, the Sufi orders, visits to tombs and the celebration of the Prophet's birthday (mawlid) as polytheism (shirk) and innovation (bid'a), and they adhere closely to the tradition of the Qur'an, the Sunna and the pious forebears (salaf).\n\nHow they differed from the settled Arabs of the HEJAZ. The Wahhabi movement at first gained strength in Bedouin (nomadic and semi-nomadic) circles — in the tribal society of inner Najd. By contrast, the settled population of Mecca and Medina and the families of the Sharifs, who had governed these cities for centuries, were attached to traditional Islamic practices (including visits to tombs and Sufi practices) and saw Wahhabi doctrine as a direct threat. This opposition took concrete form in 1803-1805: Wahhabi forces seized Mecca and Medina and put an end to the established religious practices, which drove the Ottomans to intervene militarily (through the Governor of Egypt, Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala) — with the destruction of Diriyah in 1818, the First Saudi State came to an end.\n\nWhy there was no lasting rule in NAJD. In the Hejaz (from 1517 onward), Ottoman rule was established essentially along the coast and the pilgrimage route; at no period did a real administration reach into the interior of Najd. Three structural reasons explain this: ① Geography — Najd is a broad plateau surrounded by desert on three sides, ill-suited to agriculture and settled life; ② Social structure — the tribal system prevailed, and it did not allow a full state administration (tax collection, regular garrisons, fixed administrative units) to be established; ③ Nomadic life — constant movement based on herding made attachment to a fixed administrative center practically impossible. After 1517, Ottoman 'sovereignty' here amounted in practice to exerting influence over the tribal sheikhs; the traditional structure was left untouched — and this loose bond lasted until the First World War (1918).\n\nSOUTHERN Arabia (Yemen) — another face of the same pattern. In Yemen too (see the separate card, `dunya-yemende-osmanli-hakimiyeti`) the pattern was similar: the coastal strip and the port cities could be held, but the mountainous interior (the stronghold of the Zaydi imams) never submitted permanently — in 1635 the Ottomans were in effect forced to withdraw.\n\nOMAN and the Gulf — because of a rival sea power. In Oman the problem was not geography but a rival European sea power: from 1507 onward the Portuguese seized the coastal cities (including Muscat, where they built the Jalali and Mirani forts); the Ottoman navy tried twice to break this hold (Hadım Süleyman Pasha in 1538, Piri Reis in 1552) and failed both times. The Portuguese occupation ended only through the resistance of the local Ya'rubi dynasty (led by Nasir b. Murshid in the period 1615-1640) — the Ottomans had no share in this victory. The Al Bu Said dynasty that followed (after 1743) maintained friendly but independent relations with the Ottomans and expanded on its own into East Africa (Zanzibar, Mombasa); when England had it sign the first treaty in 1798, the region passed definitively outside the Ottoman sphere of influence. In short: Najd was closed to the Ottomans by geography and tribal structure, and Oman first by Portuguese and then by British sea power — two separate obstacles, the same result.", "f717afd4"], "kisa": ["A qadi's son formed an alliance with an emir in a small oasis in the middle of the desert — and ninety years later the empire found that in three separate regions reaching out from the coast (the inner desert, the southern coast, Oman) it could not hold on for the same reason: distance, a nomadic social structure and a rival sea power.", "c0575b95"], "bag": ["The reasons TDV gives for the question 'why could they not be held?' differ among these three regions (Najd, the interior of Yemen, Oman) — tribes and geography in Najd, a rival sea power in Oman. This card brings the two together under one heading but does not reduce them to a single cause; that is the card's own synthesis.", "86ec315d"]},
"dunya7-istanbul-sel-zemheri-bogazin-donmasi":{"baslik": ["The winter the Bosphorus froze over — and the hunger of a city encircled by water", "557dfef9"], "metin": ["The freezing of the Bosphorus (January-February 1621). This is the most frequently cited extreme winter event of the Ottoman period. The historian of the age, Tûğî, recorded this winter as an eyewitness in his work Musîbetnâme: after about two weeks of uninterrupted snowfall, the Bosphorus froze completely; the sea between Üsküdar and Beşiktaş turned into dry land, and people walked from Üsküdar to Istanbul. The Golden Horn froze in the same way, and Galata too could be reached on foot. This was not merely a strange natural phenomenon but a real disaster: Istanbul's food supply depended largely on provisions arriving by sea; when the frozen waters completely stopped ships from reaching the city, prices rose many times over and the city faced a serious threat of famine — a striking example of how a capital's geographical advantage (sea on three sides) could at the same time become its greatest vulnerability.\n\nThe GÖKSU flood (1909). A concrete example given by TDV's article `bogazici` (the Bosphorus): during a flood in 1909 the Göksu stream overflowed, partly silted up and caused damage in the surrounding area — a record that even the small streams of the Bosphorus could turn into a local disaster in sudden, heavy rain.\n\nTHE PATTERN — the two faces of the sea. Istanbul's position (two seas, a strait, many small streams) both made it strategically and commercially privileged throughout history and left it vulnerable under certain conditions: in extreme cold the freezing waters cut the supply line, and in sudden rains the narrow stream beds overflowed. This third type of disaster (climatic in origin), although not as frequent as earthquakes and fires, was a separate category of risk that directly threatened the city's daily life (especially its food supply).", "d44b65a7"], "kisa": ["A city was surrounded by the sea on three sides and could still go short of food — because those seas sometimes froze, and bread came by ship.", "a42eba03"]},
"dunya7-istanbulun-buyuk-depremleri":{"baslik": ["Istanbul's great earthquakes — four doomsdays in four centuries", "1661eb22"], "metin": ["A recurring pattern. TDV's article `zelzele` (earthquake) describes Istanbul (before and under the Ottomans) as a city exposed to 'many earthquakes, large and small', and singles out four in particular for their great loss of life and property: 1509, 1719, 1766 and 1894. This shows that Istanbul's seismicity is not a one-off disaster but a structural reality recurring roughly every century or two.\n\n1509 — the 'LESSER doomsday'. The violent earthquake that struck the city became known among the people by this name; according to TDV's article `istanbul`, the city walls, mosques and many buildings suffered heavy damage, and there was great loss of life. Immediately after the earthquake, Bayezid II launched a large-scale campaign of repair and reconstruction in the city — one of the earliest examples of Ottoman response to an urban disaster. TDV's article `zelzele` also reports an interpretation linking this earthquake to the injustices committed by the officials of the time — in other words, for the people of the age the disaster had not only a physical but also a moral meaning.\n\n1766 — the collapse of the Conqueror's mosque. In the great earthquake of May 1766, the main dome of the mosque bearing the name of Mehmed the Conqueror collapsed completely, and its walls were damaged beyond repair. Rebuilding began in 1767 on the orders of Mustafa III under the direction of the architects Sarım İbrahim Efendi and İzzet Mehmed Bey, and was completed in 1771 to a new design entirely different from the old plan (classical silhouette, baroque details) — so the city's most symbolic mosque, as we see it today, is a direct result of this earthquake.\n\n1894 — the first great earthquake to be photographed. The earthquake of 10 July 1894, off the Gulf of Izmit, struck Istanbul severely; many mosques and historic structures, including the city walls, were damaged. According to academic sources (TDV does not give the details of this earthquake), its magnitude has been calculated at about 7.0; it caused more than 1,300 deaths in Istanbul and around the Gulf of Izmit (including Yalova, Sapanca and Adapazarı), and more than 20,000 civilian buildings were damaged. Abdülhamid II invited an expert from the Athens Observatory (Eginitis) to Istanbul to study the earthquake scientifically — one of the first Ottoman attempts to investigate a natural disaster by modern, scientific methods.\n\nRELIGIOUS/juridical interpretation. TDV's article `zelzele` sets two approaches side by side: while most Muslim scholars regarded earthquakes as physical events 'occurring within the framework of the laws of nature laid down by God', another tradition considers them a divine warning against social and moral decay; in this second reading, turning to prayer and worship after an earthquake was recommended. The very name 'Lesser Doomsday' of 1509 is a trace of how these two interpretations (scientific observation and religious meaning) were intertwined in the same event.", "a3544c3b"], "kisa": ["The earthquake that devastated the city in 1509 was popularly called the 'Lesser Doomsday'; two and a half centuries later, in 1766, the dome of the Conqueror's mosque collapsed — and each time the men of religion asked the same question: is this a natural event or a warning?", "6c83643c"]},
"dunya7-istanbulun-buyuk-yanginlari":{"baslik": ["Istanbul's great fires — the price of a wooden city", "31a80c50"], "metin": ["Why so often. The houses of Ottoman-era Istanbul were almost entirely of wood — narrow streets, buildings built wall to wall and a windy setting could turn a single spark into a citywide disaster. For this reason TDV's article `istanbul` mentions 'fire disasters' as one of the city's recurring structural risks.\n\nThe CİBALİ fire (September 1633). A fire that broke out in Cibali spread rapidly, driven by the wind, and reduced a large part of the city to ashes; contemporary sources report that thousands of households burned. According to the standard historical account (TDV does not give this detail), the fire began when the oakum being used by a caulker caught light, and it engulfed a wide area from Unkapanı to Fatih and from Hocapaşa to Samatya; according to the historian of the period, Kâtib Çelebi, about a fifth of the city was destroyed in this three-day fire. Its consequence was directly political: the linking of the fire to the wooden housing and to tobacco smoking became the justification for the harsh ban on tobacco and coffeehouses that Murad IV proclaimed immediately afterwards — an urban disaster turned directly into a public policy.\n\nThe AĞAKAPISI fire (July 1660). In this fire, which broke out in the reign of Mehmed IV, Ağakapısı, the official headquarters of the agha of the Janissaries, burned down completely; TDV's article `agakapisi` describes it as 'one of the great city fires that struck the Istanbul of the period again and again'. According to the standard historical account (TDV gives no details), this was a disaster remembered in the sources as the 'harîk-i kebîr' (great fire), which affected three quarters of Istanbul within the walls and caused about 4,000 deaths — it is counted among the greatest fires Ottoman Istanbul ever saw.\n\nTHE PATTERN. Only twenty-seven years separate the two fires; each struck a different part of the city (the Cibali/Fatih axis, the Süleymaniye/Hocapaşa axis), and each directly triggered the next public decision (the tobacco ban, reconstruction). This shows that in Ottoman Istanbul fire was a type of disaster that recurred as often as earthquakes, probably even more often — as long as the stock of wooden buildings remained unchanged.", "33002531"], "kisa": ["A caulker's flame, starting in Cibali, reduced a fifth of the city to ashes; twenty-seven years later another fire swept through and burned the headquarters of the agha of the Janissaries — in wooden Istanbul, fire was a disaster as frequent as earthquakes.", "98c0d182"]},
"edebiyat-idam-edilen-sair-alim":{"baslik": ["Those who died for their pen or their beliefs: poets, scholars and Sufis executed in the Ottoman realm", "f7114bd6"], "metin": ["In the Ottoman realm, the accusations that cost a poet, a scholar or the sheikh of a Sufi order his life most often fall under three headings: rebellion against the state, the charge of \"zandaqa\" (zındıklık) or \"ilhad\", meaning deviation from the faith, and a satire that had wounded someone powerful. The seven examples below are presented as they are told in the relevant articles of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam; the dates of some of them are disputed among the sources.\n\nThe earliest example is the jurist and Sufi Sheikh Bedreddin. When the movement that grew up around him turned into a rebellion, Sultan Mehmed Çelebi sent forces against him; the sheikh was captured and brought before the sultan at Serres. The council of scholars that was convened ruled that his activities and views were incompatible with religious law, that this counted as rebellion, and that he should be executed on condition that his property and family be left untouched; the decision was announced on behalf of the council by Mevlânâ Haydar Acemî. Bedreddin was executed at Serres in 823 (1420).\n\nUnder Bayezid II, the well-known scholar Molla Lutfî was accused of heresy (zındıklık). After an investigation carried out on the sultan's orders, a panel of five scholars, having held two sessions and heard some two hundred witnesses, sentenced him to death; Lutfî was beheaded at the Atmeydanı (Hippodrome) on 25 Rabi al-Akhir 900 (23 January 1495). TDV states that the sources generally attribute this accusation to the jealousy of his rivals and that most modern researchers consider the charge unfounded.\n\nIn the early years of Sultan Suleiman the Lawgiver, when Molla Kābız openly argued that Jesus was superior to the Prophet Muhammad, he was brought before the Imperial Council (Dîvân-ı Hümâyun) (8 Safar 934 / 3 November 1527). When the military judges (kazaskers) could not persuade him, the sultan, who was following the session from behind a lattice, asked for a new court to be convened. The next day the Shaykh al-Islam Kemalpaşazâde refuted his views and issued a fatwa declaring that this was heresy, and the qadi of Istanbul sentenced him to death (9 Safar 934 / 4 November 1527).\n\nIn the same period Sufi sheikhs too were tried on this charge. The Bayrami-Melami sheikh İsmail Maşûkī (Oğlan Şeyh) was executed at the Atmeydanı together with twelve of his disciples on charges of heresy and ilhad; although the traditional sources give the year 935 (1529), TDV, relying on archival records, places the event in 1539 and says that the fatwa must have been that of Çivizâde Muhyiddin Mehmed Efendi. Hamza Bâlî, for his part, was killed in Istanbul after the fatwa of the Shaykh al-Islam Ebussuud Efendi to the effect that \"if he is a heretic, killing him is lawful\". According to TDV, the state was disturbed by the fact that his beliefs did not conform to Sunni principles, that his spiritual lineage reached back to the Safavids, and by his influence among the Janissaries and palace circles; according to imperial decrees in the archives, the execution must have taken place between the spring and summer of 1573.\n\nThe fate of poets, however, was mostly determined by satire. Figānî was reported to Grand Vizier İbrahim Pasha for a couplet that appeared to mock the statues the pasha had had erected at the Atmeydanı; TDV records that the couplet was in fact an old saying dating from the time of Mahmud of Ghazni and was falsely attributed to Figānî. The poet was arrested, tortured and then hanged, most probably in the spring of 1532. A century later Nef'î was handed over by Grand Vizier Bayram Pasha in Shaban 1044 (January 1635) because of his collection of satires, Sihâm-ı Kazâ, and was strangled in the palace woodshed, his body thrown into the sea.", "d0a79009"], "alinti.metin": ["If Sheikh Hamza too is a heretic on that path, killing him is lawful.", "2985836c"]},
"ermeni-1915-soykirim-tehcir-mukatele-tartisma":{"ad": ["1915: genocide, relocation (tehcir) or mutual slaughter (mukatele)? — the opposing claims of the sides", "ed9a9631"], "metin": ["Common ground. All sides agree on this core: during the First World War the Ottoman government moved the Armenians from their homelands in the east southwards, and a great many Armenians lost their lives during and after this transfer. The temporary law regulating the transfer came into force with the sultan's approval on 27 May 1915 (13 Rajab 1333); the TDV article on tehcir points out that the word “tehcir” (forced relocation) never occurs in the text of the law, and that the official language was “dispatch and settlement”.\n\nThe FIRST reading — genocide. The encyclopaedia of the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum summarises the academic literature that upholds the genocide thesis (listing in its bibliography authors such as Akçam, Bloxham, Kaiser, Suny and Üngör) as follows: the government of the Committee of Union and Progress aimed to eliminate the Armenian presence in order to consolidate Muslim Turkish dominance in Central and Eastern Anatolia; the deportations began in the border region, but in the following months they also spread to provinces far from the front; the deaths occurred in massacres, through hunger, disease and ill-treatment on the road, and upon arrival in the Syrian desert; tens of thousands of children were torn from their families and converted to Islam. The same source also states that the jurist Raphael Lemkin, who coined the word “genocide”, was influenced by these events. The central claim of this thesis is intent: the transfer was the instrument of destruction.\n\nThe SECOND reading — compulsory transfer (tehcir). The official view of the Republic of Turkey (Ministry of Foreign Affairs) states that it does not deny that the Armenians suffered greatly in the war and that many innocent people died; but it defines the transfer as a measure to secure the area behind the front against armed Armenian groups acting together with the invading Russian army. According to the same text, the deaths were the combined result of wartime conditions, internal conflict, local groups seeking revenge, banditry, famine, epidemics and a collapsing state apparatus; officials who committed crimes were tried by court martial by the Ottoman government in 1916, before the war had even ended, and sentenced to death; there is no “authentic evidence” showing a premeditated decision to exterminate. The TDV article on tehcir (Kemal Beydilli) goes further in the same direction: it stresses the share of the German military advisers behind the decision on relocation, and writes that the gendarmerie that was to ensure road security had been withdrawn to the front and that a considerable proportion of the attacks on the convoys came from tribal and Bedouin gangs.\n\nThe THIRD reading — mukatele (mutual slaughter). This reading places Muslim losses at the centre of the account. The TDV article on tehcir, relying on J. McCarthy's calculations, reports that in the regions under Russian-Armenian occupation the fighting behind the front turned into a “civil war”, that at least 2.5 million Muslims died in Anatolia between 1914 and 1922, and that in the first years this figure reached 1.5 million in the occupied regions. The mukatele thesis therefore sees 1915 not as a one-sided extermination but as a wartime reckoning in which two communities slaughtered each other. The word “mukatele” itself does not occur in any of the sources consulted; the concept is represented by the expressions “civil war” and “bloody reckoning” in the TDV article.\n\nMUTUAL objections. According to the genocide thesis, the Turkish thesis dissolves Armenian deaths into the general sufferings of the war. According to the Turkish official view, the genocide thesis selects and magnifies only Armenian suffering, ignores Muslim losses and has a legally defined crime decided not by a court but by parliaments. The TDV article on tehcir criticises Dadrian, a leading name in the genocide literature, for “one-sided claims of innocence and numerical exaggeration”. Turkey states that it has proposed that a joint commission of Turkish and Armenian historians examine 1915 in all the archives, and that the Armenian side has not responded to this proposal.", "32276b25"], "kisa": ["That the event happened is not in dispute: in 1915 the Armenians were displaced by law and a great many people died. The dispute turns on three points — were the killings the product of a premeditated intent to exterminate, or of wartime conditions; how many were lost; and where do the hundreds of thousands of Muslims who died in the same years stand in this account?", "4ba07a0e"], "not": ["There is also a tension within the TDV itself: the article on the Teşkilât-ı Mahsusa (Special Organisation) writes that the organisation took “an active role in the relocation of the Armenians” and that Dr Bahaeddin Şakir, as chief for the Eastern Provinces, was one of those who carried it out; the article on tehcir, by contrast, attributes the failures of road security to the inadequacy of the gendarmerie and to tribal gangs. This card reports both TDV articles as they are and does not rule between them. Armenia's official text (Genocide Museum-Institute, Ministry of Foreign Affairs) could not be retrieved; the thesis is presented here through a third, institutional source (USHMM). The day on which the implementation ended: the date printed in the TDV (“15 March 1915”) falls before the approval of the law and must therefore be a misprint; the correct day was not found in the sources consulted. The USHMM dates the events from spring 1915 to autumn 1916.", "51c322c4"], "bag": ["For why the figures diverge so widely, see the card 'The Armenian population: before and after the war'; for the revolts of the thirty years before the transfer, the card 'Armenian committees and revolts'; and for the post-war period, the card '1918-1923'.", "d325112a"]},
"ermeni-bagimsizlik-neden-olmadi-tartisma":{"ad": ["The Balkan nations won their independence; why could the Ottoman Armenians not do so?", "28a649e3"], "metin": ["First, a correction. According to the TDV article on Sèvres, a Republic of Armenia was founded in Yerevan in May 1918; Britain, France and Italy recognised it on 19 January 1920, and the United States on 23 April 1920. So it is not correct to say that 'the Armenians never attained independence'; what they did not attain was a state encompassing Eastern Anatolia.\n\n① Population — not being a majority. This is the reason on which the sources consulted lean most often. The TDV articles on the deportation and on the Ottomans state that the Armenians did not form a majority of the population in the six provinces, and that the reform plan of 1914 gave them equal and superior rights although they were not a majority; according to what TDV reports, Abdülhamid II had also resisted Article 61 on this ground. Because the sources consulted do not give comparative population ratios for the Balkan provinces, the comparison could not be made here in figures; but the figures for the eastern provinces in TDV's city articles (Van in 1914: 179 thousand Muslims, 68 thousand Armenians; Erzurum in the 1890s: 500 thousand Muslims against 110-135 thousand Armenians) show that the picture in the east was different. The genocide thesis reads this reason from the opposite direction: according to the USHMM summary, the aim of the Committee of Union and Progress was to consolidate Muslim Turkish dominance in Central and Eastern Anatolia by eliminating the Armenian presence — that is, in this reading, the population picture at the end of the war was not a cause but partly a result of 1915.\n\n② The great powers — the limits of support. The TDV article on Sèvres relates that the great powers made promises to the Armenians but refrained from establishing a Greater Armenia: Britain withdrew from the Caucasus on 15 August 1919; the mandate for Armenia was offered to the United States, and the US Senate rejected it on 1 June 1920; the League of Nations, for its part, stated that it had no military or financial power. The article delivers the judgement that the great powers used the Armenians 'as a pawn' in war and diplomacy — this is the article's own assessment. In the cases of independence in the Balkans, by contrast, as the TDV articles on San Stefano and Berlin show, the Russian army was actually in the field and the outcome was confirmed by treaty; for the Armenians, those same two treaties promised only 'reforms'.\n\n③ The military outcome. The Treaty of Sèvres (10 August 1920) required Turkey to recognise the independence of Armenia and left the drawing of the border to the arbitration of President Wilson. According to the TDV article on Kâzım Karabekir, in response to Sèvres giving Erzurum, Trabzon, Van, Bitlis and Bingöl to the Armenians, the government of the Grand National Assembly authorised a military operation on 20 September 1920; the army took Gyumri (Gümrü) on 7 November, and by the Treaty of Gyumri of 3 December 1920 Armenia renounced all the rights recognised to it at Sèvres.\n\nWHAT was not found. The answer that the Armenian side gives to this question in its own historiography (the differences between the programmes of the Dashnaks and the Hunchaks, the divergence between Russian Armenians and Ottoman Armenians) does not appear in the sources consulted.", "2ae29ba7"], "kisa": ["The question itself is half right: a Republic of Armenia was founded in Yerevan in 1918 — but on former Russian territory. No separate Armenian state could be established in the eastern provinces of the Ottoman Empire; the sources consulted look for the reason in three places.", "79df4547"], "not": ["This question is not treated as a separate article in TDV; the card has compiled the reasons from what each of the articles consulted says, and it does not present the compilation as a source. The classification into 'three reasons' is this card's own arrangement, not the classification of any source.", "20b2133c"], "bag": ["For the post-war process see the '1918-1923' card; for population figures see the 'Armenian population' card.", "f0f7a6d9"]},
"ermeni-komitalari-isyanlar-1887-1909-tartisma":{"ad": ["Armenian committees and the events of the 1890s: revolt or massacre?", "3935bbcf"], "metin": ["The committees. According to the TDV article on tehcir, after the Armenian question became an international problem through Article 61 of the Treaty of Berlin, organisations (“committees”) aiming to establish an Armenia in Eastern Anatolia appeared. The most important were the Hunchak (1887) and the Dashnaktsutyun (1890). The article writes that the committees, following the example of the Balkans, aimed to bring about the intervention of the great powers.\n\nTHE EVENTS. The TDV article on tehcir lists the places where events occurred from 1890 onwards as follows: Erzurum, Merzifon, Sasun, Zeytun, Van, Trebizond, Gümüşhane, Erzincan, Diyarbekir, Aleppo, Urfa, Muş, Bitlis, Harput, Ayıntab, Kayseri, Sivas and Birecik; and after the Second Constitutional Era also Adana (Yumurtalık, Dörtyol, İskenderun). According to the TDV article on Muş, the uprising in Sasun began in 1894; in 1895 the government formed a commission, and the French, Russian and British consuls in Erzurum also came to Muş; guerrilla band activity recurred in 1901 and 1905. According to the article on Van, the first revolts in Van occurred in 1896-1897. In Istanbul the TDV mentions two actions: the raid on the Ottoman Bank in 1896, and the bomb attempt to assassinate Abdülhamid II at the Friday procession on 21 July 1905.\n\nTWO names. The TDV article on tehcir states explicitly that writers such as Dadrian present these events as 'the Armenian massacres of the reign of Abdülhamid II', and it does not accept this presentation; the article's reading is that these were revolts raised by the committees that caused loss of life. The own arguments and casualty figures of the side that defends the name 'massacre' were not found in the sources consulted (the USHMM summary does not deal with the events of 1894-96); for this reason that reading is mentioned here by name only to the extent that the TDV reports it.\n\nAdana 1909. The TDV article on Abdülhamid II writes that one day after the 31 March Incident began in Istanbul, the Armenians in Adana raised a great uprising and killed many Turks. The Armenian side's account of the Adana events and the casualty figures of the two sides were not found in the sources consulted.\n\nWHY it matters for 1915. The TDV article on tehcir argues that the Armenian question can only be written about in connection with the actions of these organisations, and that 1915 cannot be detached from them. The counter-thesis rejects precisely this connection: as the TDV reports, the texts published by Lepsius deem even the Armenian uprisings “imaginary”.", "4a6b15d4"], "kisa": ["The same events are known by two different names. In Turkish historiography they are the 'Armenian revolts'; in the genocide literature, the 'Armenian massacres of the Abdülhamid period'. The TDV itself records this second name and rejects it.", "90abd5ab"], "not": ["The TDV articles consulted do not give a day-by-day chronology of the events (the month of Sasun 1894, the date of Zeytun, the Kumkapı demonstration); the separate articles 'sason', 'zeytun', 'hincak', 'tasnak' and 'osmanli-bankasi' are dead in the TDV (302). The card therefore remains at year precision.", "fb6e4b0d"], "bag": ["For the connection of the events with the Hamidiye Regiments, see the card 'The Hamidiye Regiments'; for Article 61 itself, see the card 'Chronology of the Armenian question'.", "3f48fdd8"]},
"ermeni-meselesi-1918-1923-sevr-gumru-tartisma":{"ad": ["After the war: the Republic of Armenia, Sèvres, the eastern campaign and Gyumri (Gümrü) (1918-1923)", "2e28335d"], "metin": ["Mudros. According to the TDV article on the Armistice of Mudros, one article of the armistice gave the Entente powers the right of occupation if disorder broke out in the six provinces (Erzurum, Van, Harput, Diyarbakır, Sivas, Bitlis), referred to in the English text as the 'Armenian vilayets'.\n\nArmenia IN THE CAUCASUS. According to the TDV article on Kâzım Karabekir, after Russia withdrew from the war, Karabekir retook Erzincan, Erzurum and Hasankale in 1918, and entered Kars on 25 April 1918 and Gyumri (Gümrü) on 15 May. In the same month the Republic of Armenia was established in Yerevan (TDV sevr). When the Turkish army withdrew after the armistice, the British left the Kars-Ardahan-Batum region to Armenia and Georgia in April 1919.\n\nSÈVRES. According to the TDV article on Sèvres, under the treaty signed on 10 August 1920 Turkey was to recognise the independence of Armenia, leave the drawing of the Armenian-Turkish frontier to the arbitration of President Wilson, and renounce its rights over the territories that were to join Armenia. The same article describes in detail how the great powers avoided giving Armenia the necessary financial and military support.\n\nThe EASTERN campaign and Gyumri. The TDV article on Kâzım Karabekir gives the reasons for the campaign as the increase of 'Armenian atrocities' in the region and Sèvres giving Erzurum, Trebizond, Van, Bitlis and Bingöl to the Armenians; the Grand National Assembly gave its authorisation on 20 September 1920, and the army took Gyumri (Gümrü) on 7 November and Iğdır on 12 November; by the Treaty of Gyumri (Gümrü), signed on 3 December 1920, Armenia renounced all the rights recognised to it at Sèvres. The article also records that Karabekir took thousands of orphaned children in the east under his care. The Armenian side's account of this campaign and the losses of the two sides were not found in the sources consulted.\n\nTHE RECKONING. According to the TDV article on Talat Pasha, the government of Damad Ferid Pasha referred Talat Pasha to the Court Martial (Divan-ı Harb-i Örfi); his rank of vizier was withdrawn on 12 March 1919, before the trial in absentia began. Talat Pasha was killed in Berlin on 15 March 1921 by Soghomon Tehlirian, who, in the article's words, was carrying out the Dashnaktsutyun's decision to kill the leading members of the Committee of Union and Progress; the jury of the court acquitted the killer, who was put on trial, on 3 June 1921. The other defendants in the trials, the verdicts handed down and the assessment of these cases in the historiography of the two sides were not found in the sources consulted.\n\nLAUSANNE. According to the TDV article on Lausanne, in the debates in the Assembly the 'Armenian question' was among the principal topics, together with the borders and full independence; in the treaty, minority rights were settled in Articles 37-44; the article mentions no provision envisaging an Armenian homeland in Eastern Anatolia.", "943f56a0"], "kisa": ["After Mudros the question passed from the front to the diplomatic table, and from there back to the front. Sèvres envisaged an Armenian state in Eastern Anatolia; two years later Gyumri and Lausanne closed off this scheme.", "54424bbf"], "not": ["The Treaty of Kars (13 October 1921) is in the atlas chronology, but the article 'kars-antlasmasi' in the TDV is dead (302); it is therefore not treated in the card. The statement that 'no provision for an Armenian homeland was included at Lausanne' rests on the fact that the section on minorities in the TDV article on Lausanne mentions no such provision; the text of the treaty itself was not read.", "690425c5"], "bag": ["For a comparison with the Balkans, see the card 'The Balkan nations won their independence; why could the Ottoman Armenians not?'.", "708c185b"]},
"ermeni-meselesi-kronolojisi-1878-1923":{"sebep.b": ["The imposition on the Ottoman Empire, through Article 16 of San Stefano and Article 61 of Berlin, of a requirement of 'reform' in the provinces where Armenians lived", "442f56b2"], "sonuc.b": ["The signing of the Treaty of Lausanne and the settlement of the rights of the non-Muslim minorities in Articles 37-44", "60abeee6"], "bag": ["The dates are given with the precision of the sources consulted: where a day is given, the day is written; where only a year is given, the year. The source of each line is in parentheses; where two sources conflict, both are written.", "4221e02e"], "metin": ["■ 1461 — The Conqueror appointed Hovakim, metropolitan of Bursa, as Armenian Patriarch of Istanbul (TDV millet — 'generally accepted'; the date is disputed).\n\n■ January 1830 — The Catholic Armenians were recognised as a separate millet; the Protestant Armenians in 1850 (TDV millet, tehcir).\n\n■ 1862 — Regulations for the Armenian community: a national assembly of 400 members (TDV Reform Edict).\n\n■ 3 March 1878 — Treaty of San Stefano: reform under Russian supervision in the places in Eastern Anatolia where Armenians lived (TDV ayastefanos; the article number 16 is from the TDV article on tehcir).\n\n■ 13 July 1878 — Treaty of Berlin: the Sublime Porte was to carry out reforms in the places where Armenians lived and protect them against the Kurds (Article 61; TDV berlin, tehcir). According to the TDV, Abdülhamid II resisted this article despite all pressure.\n\n■ 1887 Hunchak · 1890 Dashnaktsutyun founded (TDV tehcir).\n\n■ 1890 — Work on founding the Hamidiye Regiments began; the law is dated 13 May 1896 (TDV hamidiye-alaylari).\n\n■ 1894 — Uprising in Sasun; in 1895 a government commission and consuls in Muş (TDV muş).\n\n■ 11 May 1895 — Joint reform project of Britain, France and Russia (TDV hamidiye-alaylari).\n\n■ 1896 — Raid on the Ottoman Bank in Istanbul; first revolts in Van in 1896-1897 (TDV tehcir, van).\n\n■ 21 July 1905 — Bomb attempt to assassinate Abdülhamid II (TDV tehcir).\n\n■ April 1909 — The Adana events; the TDV article on Abdülhamid II places the event one day after the beginning of the 31 March Incident (13 April) and describes it as an Armenian uprising.\n\n■ 8 February 1914 — The Eastern Anatolian reform agreement with Russia at Yeniköy: two autonomous regions and two foreign inspectors-general (Westenenk, Hoff) (TDV tehcir, osmanlilar).\n\n■ February-April 1915 — Armed Armenian movement in Van; on 7 April 1915 the city was besieged (TDV van — the article's account; the Armenian side's account of the event was not found in the sources consulted).\n\n■ 27 May 1915 — The Law on Dispatch and Settlement came into force with the sultan's approval (TDV tehcir).\n\n■ Spring 1915 - autumn 1916 — The period that the USHMM dates as genocide.\n\n■ 1916 — According to Turkey's official view, officials who committed crimes during the transfer were tried by court martial by the Ottoman government (Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the Republic of Turkey).\n\n■ 7 December 1916 — Interior Ministry report: more than 702,000 Muslim refugees fleeing before the Russian-Armenian units (TDV tehcir).\n\n■ May 1918 — The Republic of Armenia was established in Yerevan (TDV sevr).\n\n■ 1919 — Talat Pasha was referred to the Court Martial (Divan-ı Harb-i Örfi); his rank of vizier was withdrawn on 12 March 1919 (TDV talat-pasa).\n\n■ 1 June 1920 — The US Senate rejected the Armenian mandate (TDV sevr).\n\n■ 10 August 1920 — Sèvres: the independence of Armenia and Wilson's arbitration (TDV sevr).\n\n■ 20 September 1920 — The Grand National Assembly authorised the eastern campaign; Gyumri (Gümrü) was taken on 7 November (TDV kazim-karabekir).\n\n■ 3 December 1920 — Treaty of Gyumri (Gümrü): Armenia renounced its rights under Sèvres (TDV kazim-karabekir).\n\n■ 15 March 1921 — Talat Pasha was killed in Berlin by Soghomon Tehlirian, who was carrying out a decision of the Dashnaktsutyun; on 3 June 1921 the jury of the court acquitted the killer (TDV talat-pasa).\n\n■ 5 January 1922 — The French left Adana together with the Armenians who had helped them (TDV adana).\n\n■ 24 July 1923 — Lausanne: rights of the non-Muslim minorities, Arts. 37-44 (TDV lozan).\n\n■ 1970s and after — According to Turkey's official view, 31 Turkish diplomats and members of their families and 43 people of other nationalities died in attacks by Armenian terrorist organisations (Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the Republic of Turkey).", "c0cc857d"]},
"ermeni-nufusu-savas-oncesi-sonrasi-tartisma":{"ad": ["The Ottoman Armenian population: how large was it before the war, and what happened afterwards?", "62d4b07d"], "metin": ["Before the war — the empire as a whole. The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum writes that about 1.5 million Armenians lived in the Ottoman Empire in 1915. The empire-wide total of the official Ottoman statistics of 1914 and the pre-war census of the Armenian Patriarchate were not found in the sources consulted; for this reason the two sides' overall totals could not be set side by side.\n\nBEFORE the war — at provincial level (from the TDV's city articles). Van: according to the official statistics of 1914, 179,380 Muslims and 67,792 Armenians in the province. Erzurum: according to V. Cuinet, 500,782 Muslims and 134,967 Armenians in the 1890s; the Kamus al-a'lam, describing the same years, gives 464,129 Muslims and 109,835 Armenians — there is a difference of about 25,000 Armenians between the two sources. Adana: according to the yearbook (salname) of 1890-91, 341,376 Muslims and 32,989 Armenians, Greeks and Syriacs in the province; Cuinet counts 346,308 Turks and 51,982 Greeks and Armenians in 1891. Muş: a Russian report dated 1899 shows 1,500 Muslim and 1,200 Armenian houses in the town. These figures show that in the eastern provinces the Armenians were an important but minority population; the TDV articles on tehcir and on the Ottomans also stress that the Armenians did not form the majority of the population in the six provinces.\n\nThose subjected to TRANSFER. The TDV article on tehcir, relying on Lewy's breakdown, writes that in various sources the number of those moved is given as between 438,000 and 1 million. It adds that the figure of 924,158 in the notebook attributed to Talat Pasha has been rejected both as to the authenticity of the notebook and as to the validity of the figure (Halaçoğlu).\n\nLOSSES — a contradiction. The TDV article on tehcir (relying on Lewy): between 56,000 and 2 million — the article describes the figures at the upper end as numbers inflated over time by “partisan people”. The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum: at least 664,000, perhaps as many as 1.2 million. This card neither merges the two ranges nor takes their average.\n\nMUSLIM losses — the other side of the same account. The TDV article on tehcir, relying on McCarthy, writes that at least 2.5 million Muslims (18% of the total population) died in Anatolia between 1914 and 1922, and that in Bitlis, Van and Erzurum this proportion reached 62% (612,610); according to the Interior Ministry report of 7 December 1916, the number of Muslim refugees fleeing before the Russian-Armenian units had exceeded 702,000. The same article notes that Kâmuran Gürün mistakenly gave this figure of 702,000 as the number of Armenians moved — a good example of how figures can change places between the sides.\n\nAFTER the war. According to the TDV article on population, the Greek and Armenian population of Anatolia was greatly reduced through forced migration and the population exchange, and at the first census of the Republic (1927) Turkey's total population was 13,648,270. According to the TDV article on Van, the population of the city of Van, which had been about 70,000 before the war, had fallen to 10,000-15,000 in 1919 (another paragraph of the same article estimates the pre-war population of the city at 30,000-35,000 — the article itself carries two figures). According to the TDV, the non-Muslim population of Adana left the region together with the French in January 1922. The number of Armenians in the 1927 census was not found in the sources consulted.", "2c1de355"], "kisa": ["There is no single correct number; each side counts from its own source. Estimates of losses range from 56,000 to 2 million — this range itself shows how deep the dispute is.", "fd120201"], "not": ["The common answer of the sources consulted as to why the numbers diverge so widely: population counts were kept in terms of 'households' or the 'male population' as units for taxation and military service; the tax registers (tahrir) are incomplete (the TDV article on Van says that no detailed register of Van has ever been found); and each side trusts its own count and regards the other's as inflated. The USHMM bibliography also includes a book dealing directly with this subject (Fuat Dündar, Crime of Numbers, 2010); this card has not read it.", "d2381c23"], "bag": ["For the main debate, see the card '1915: genocide, relocation (tehcir) or mutual slaughter (mukatele)?'.", "5e009600"]},
"ermeni-orgutleri-kurulus-sirasi":{"baslik": ["Armenian organisations — how were the Armenakan, the Hunchak and the Dashnak founded?", "6dfc2e74"], "metin": ["■ Getting the order right\nThese organisations are often spoken of as though they were a single structure; in fact there are three separate foundations, three separate places and three separate worlds of ideas.\n\n■ ① ARMENAKAN (1885, Van)\nThe first Armenian political party to take revolutionary activity as its aim was the Armenakan, founded in Van in 1885 by the students of Mkrtich Portukalian. Portukalian had taught at the school he opened in Van and had raised a revolutionary generation of young people there; when arrests began and he was forbidden to reside in Van, he went to Marseilles with some of his supporters and in 1885 began publishing a newspaper there called Armenia. He had explained the newspaper's purpose as the development of commercial relations between Armenian merchants and various countries; but its content soon changed, and in August 1885 the newspaper was banned from entering Ottoman territory. The party's centre was Van.\n\n■ ② HUNCHAK (1887, Geneva)\nThe Hunchak Committee was founded in August 1887 in the city of Geneva, Switzerland, by Armenian students WHO WERE RUSSIAN SUBJECTS and had gone to study at Western European universities. The founders first prepared the programme of the society; the first issue of the organ bearing the same name appeared in November 1887 and the second in January 1888, and the programme was announced in 1888.\n\n■ WHAT THE HUNCHAK PROGRAMME SAID\nThe programme had five parts. The first part set out the situation of the working and producing class, that is, the socialist framework. The second part dealt with the situation of the Armenians under absolutist rule and the structure that was envisaged. In the third part Ottoman territory was chosen as the field of revolution and the forms of activity were determined. The fourth part described the society's immediate goals and the fifth its ultimate aim.\n\n■ ENTRY INTO OTTOMAN TERRITORY\nIn the first days of January 1890 the society began to organise in Ottoman territory; members came from Geneva, Trebizond and other centres. It is reported that within seven months the membership reached 700; most members were people working in foreign consulates and similar jobs. In later years the society split: one wing defended the line of its founder, the other the reformist line.\n\n■ ③ DASHNAKTSUTYUN (1890, Tiflis)\nIn Tiflis, under Russian rule, many political groups and organisations had appeared. These groups came together in the summer of 1890 and, after discussing at length the programme of the new organisation to be established, founded an organisation called the \"Union of Armenian Revolutionaries\". Its first aim was to unite all revolutionary Armenians and Armenian political groups under a single roof; it aimed to bring together the Young Armenia, Armenakan and Hunchak societies. This union did not last: the Armenakan broke away completely in 1896 and continued to act independently.\n\n■ WHAT THEY HAD IN COMMON\nNote where all three were born: Van is an Ottoman city, but the founding cadre had been expelled from it; Geneva and Tiflis were outside Ottoman territory, and most of the founders were Russian subjects. In other words, the organisation was not formed from within the Ottoman lands but was organised outside them and carried in — this is the factual basis of the Ottoman government's perception at the time of a foreign-sourced conspiracy; whether that perception was exaggerated is a separate debate.\n\n■ A NOTE TO THE READER — BALANCE OF SOURCES\nThe literature on which this card is based belongs to the Turkish academic tradition and defines these organisations predominantly by their methods of terror and revolution. Armenian historiography narrates the same organisations within a framework of national liberation and self-defence. Passing judgement without comparing the two frameworks would be incomplete; the opposing narrative is given on a separate card.", "0898c1ff"], "kisa": ["Three organisations were founded in three separate cities within five years: Van, Geneva, Tiflis — and all three were born outside or on the edge of Ottoman territory.", "b343442a"]},
"ermeni-orgutleri-ne-yaptilar":{"baslik": ["The method and goal of the Armenian organisations", "8fc7f4ec"], "metin": ["■ The logic of the method\nBecause these organisations were very few in number, their line of action could not be built on the logic of a classic uprising. The literature that examines their programmes states that they adopted as their principle the methods of revolution, revolt and terror to achieve their aims, and that while carrying out actions they also tried to incite the Armenian community living in Ottoman territory to mass uprisings.\n\n■ THE CHAIN OF ACTIONS\nThe same literature counts the events of the 1890s as a single chain: the Kumkapı demonstration, the Sasun events, the Sublime Porte demonstration, and the Zeytun and Van movements. What these actions had in common was that they aimed not at a military gain but at international VISIBILITY: to draw the attention of the great powers and make them insist on the implementation of the reform provisions contained in the Treaty of Berlin.\n\n■ THE BEST-KNOWN LINK IN THE CHAIN — 26 AUGUST 1896\nAt noon that day, 26 Dashnak members led by Babken Suni entered the Ottoman Bank, seized the building and took about 150 people inside hostage. According to the report written two days after the event by the Russian naval attaché, first twenty well-dressed people entered the bank accompanied by several porters and began to argue with the clerks; after shouting broke out, a large group that had been prepared in advance forced its way in, all the doors were shut and the clerks were ordered to stay where they were. At the door a sentry was killed and a kavass (guard) was wounded. At the same time a proclamation was sent to all the embassies asking for the support of the great powers; it stated that if the demands were not met, the bank would be blown up together with those inside it. Babken Suni died as a result of an explosion, and others took over the direction of the action.\n\n■ WHY A BANK\nThe choice of target was no coincidence: the bank was the most visible institution of European capital in the Ottoman Empire and served as the central bank of the state. Indeed, the ambassadors in Istanbul began to mediate because they were worried about the fate of their own countries' investments in the bank. Exactly as calculated, the action became the most effective event in carrying the Armenian question onto the world agenda.\n\n■ WHAT WAS THE OUTCOME\nThe part of the calculation that did not work out was this: the action drew Europe's attention but did not bring its intervention. What happened during and after the raid contributed to the polarisation of Ottoman society, especially in Istanbul, and large-scale violence occurred in the city. In other words, the price of the strategy was paid largely by the very community it aimed to protect.\n\n■ TWO NARRATIVES\nAt this point historiography splits in two, and the place where it splits needs to be shown. Turkish academic literature narrates the events as an attempt by foreign-backed organisations to break up the state, and the measures taken against it. Armenian historiography narrates the same events as the self-defence of a community under pressure and the mass violence that followed. These two frameworks are not merely different interpretations of the same events; they also diverge over which event is to be counted as the \"beginning\" — and the harshness of the debate stems largely from this.\n\n■ A NOTE TO THE READER\nThis card shows the order of events and the point at which the two narratives diverge; it passes no judgement on which of them is right. Such a judgement cannot be given on the basis of a single family of sources.", "bba5af76"], "kisa": ["The organisations' method was not a mass uprising but attention-grabbing action — the aim was not to overthrow Ottoman rule but to call on Europe to intervene.", "8b8f9498"]},
"fatih-gizli-mektup-1451":{"soru": ["Which secret letter called him to the throne?", "1800e3e0"], "baslik": ["From the wedding to the throne: Çandarlı's secret letter", "4ed400bc"], "metin": ["His father Murad II had placed him on the throne in 1444, when he was only twelve, but two years later the young sultan had been obliged to give the throne back to his father. At the end of 1450 a magnificent wedding was held in Edirne with Sitti Hatun, daughter of the Dulkadirid Süleyman Bey, and Mehmed returned to Manisa with his bride. According to TDV, before long a secret letter sent by Çandarlı Halil Pasha informed him that his father had died and summoned him urgently to the capital (10 February 1451). Mehmed reached Edirne swiftly by way of Gallipoli and on 18 February 1451, at the age of nineteen, ascended the throne for the second time.", "57285695"]},
"fatih-zehir-iddiasi":{"soru": ["Did Mehmed the Conqueror die of poisoning?", "d9f46750"], "baslik": ["The death of the Conqueror: gout or poison?", "10c331b4"], "metin": ["Sultan Mehmed the Conqueror died on 3 May 1481 at Hünkârçayırı, between Üsküdar and Gebze, after he had crossed to the Anatolian shore to set out on a new campaign. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam attributes his death to gout. The claims that the sultan was poisoned, according to the encyclopaedia, arise from the interpretation of a piece of information in the chronicle of Âşıkpaşazâde and are not confirmed by other sources.", "ed387233"], "not": ["The card reports the claim; it does not confirm it.", "d7bc7fe8"]},
"fes-resmi-serpus-1829-ve-oncesi":{"ad": ["The fez: how did a headgear that took its name from Fez and came from Europe become the official headdress of the Ottomans?", "cdf5a05c"], "metin": ["What is the fez. A headgear of red broadcloth in the form of a cylinder narrowing towards the top, with a tassel hanging from the crown. It takes its name from the Moroccan city called Fès or Fez in Western languages; but it entered the Ottoman world mostly as an import from Europe. It was knitted from wool (merino being the most suitable) and felted, dyed, shrunk into the shape of a conical cap by a process called 'dink' (fulling), then napped and blocked on a mould. At first there were no craftsmen who knew the work; fezzes came from Europe, Egypt and Tunis. The first factory was established in Istanbul under the name Feshane; after the fez spread to civilians, factories were also opened in Bursa, Edirne, İslimye and Salonica, but domestic production could never meet demand, and the shortfall was covered by imports. In 1829 a fez with Tunisian finish sold on the market for 26 kuruş, Egyptian fezzes for 18 kuruş, a European unfinished fez for 5 kuruş 20 para and a locally made Istanbul fez for 6 kuruş 10 para.\n\nWHY it became the official headgear. When Mahmud II abolished the Janissary Corps in 1826 and set up in its place the army of the Asâkir-i Mansûre-i Muhammediyye (Victorious Soldiers of Muhammad), he wanted everything about this army, from its dress to its training and equipment, to be in the Western style. The soldiers were first given a uniform resembling that of Selim III's Nizam-i Cedid troops; two years later a change was made, it was decided that a dark red fez should be worn on the head in place of the şubara, and the sultan's imperial rescript (hatt-ı hümayun) on the matter was proclaimed (1244/1829). The TDV article on the kavuk summarises the same decision as follows: by the dress regulation issued in 1829, it was decided that the fez should be worn in place of the widely differing kinds of kavuk, in order to establish order. So the reason the source gives is 'order'; the source does not say why the sultan chose the fez in particular.\n\nREACTIONS of the period. At first the fez was compulsory only for soldiers. As it spread among civilians, they were required, so that they could be distinguished from state officials, to wear the 'dalfes' (a bare fez with nothing wound around it); only the gentlemen of the ulema might wind a white muslin cloth around it. When the people persisted in refusing to wear the bare fez, tradesmen were allowed to wind a yemeni kerchief, a çember scarf, an ağabani, a printed kerchief or a muslin cloth around it, and only then did the fez spread rapidly. The second reaction concerned the tassel: the abundant, untwisted blue silk tassel of Mahmud II's reign became dishevelled in the wind and with movements of the head, making its wearer look slovenly; small children in the streets earned money by combing tassels with a hand comb. It is probable that the expression 'püsküllü belâ' ('tasselled trouble') arose in this period, when the fez had not yet been accepted and was compulsory only for soldiers (TDV says 'it is probable' and does not state it with certainty). In 1845 a tassel regulation was issued that made a plaited (twisted) tassel and a thin brass disc called a ferahi on the crown of the fez compulsory for soldiers. In time the fez was so thoroughly adopted that it entered folk songs, amulets against the evil eye and charms were pinned to children's fezzes, and it is said that having one's photograph taken without a fez was considered shameful. How the ulema, the guilds and the non-Muslim communities each viewed the fez is not treated in detail in the TDV articles on the fez, the kavuk and the turban (sarık).\n\nHEADGEAR before the fez. Börk: an old Turkish headgear; it is seen in a crown-like form in Hun, Göktürk and Uyghur depictions and was made of fur, leather, wool or felt; the oldest known use of the word is in a Uyghur text of the ninth century. The 'külâh-ı Keykubâdî' of the Seljuk sultans was a crown set on a börk; Orhan Gazi chose the 'ak börk' (white börk) for himself and his household troops, and the white börk of the Janissaries ('elif börk') was introduced in the same period. Kavuk: in Turkish it means 'a hollow thing'; in Ottoman usage it mostly denotes a headgear with a turban wound on it. In the early period the üsküf was worn on campaigns (it is regarded as the invention of Süleyman Pasha, son of Osman Gazi); then came the mücevveze (30-35 cm, pasteboard, white muslin), the selîmî attributed to Sultan Selim the Grim (about 65 cm; the sultans wore it until the abolition of the Janissary Corps) and the kallâvî of the grand viziers and viziers. The shape of the kavuk indicated the wearer's class and profession; the ulema wound the 'örf', members of the Sufi orders wore white, red, green or black, and the common people an ağbani turban; the kavuk showing a person's rank was also carved on the gravestone. Sarık (turban): the muslin cloth wound around a headgear such as a kavuk, conical cap, skullcap or fez. In the Ottoman palace organisation the office of chief turban-keeper (tülbent ağalığı) was a respected post; the crafts of turban and kavuk making retained their importance until the time of Mahmud II. In general the turban was regarded as the symbol of the ulema; in some periods the size of a turban was considered a sign of the greatness of one's office. In the Abbasid and Mamluk periods rules on colour and length were imposed on dhimmis (Christians blue, Jews yellow, Samaritans red turbans; non-Muslims were forbidden to wind turbans longer than 5, later 7, zira'). Kalpak and conical cap (külâh): no TDV article under the heading kalpak was found, and the article on the külâh merely says 'the headgear of dervishes' and refers the reader to the article on the crown (taç); no details from TDV were found on the place of these two among the Ottomans.\n\nTHE DRESS Revolution. Describing the fez as a 'headgear revolution' is this page's interpretation: the sources describe what was done in 1829 in a single sentence as 'the fez in place of kavuks, for the sake of order'. The life of the fez also has a sourced symmetry: the fez was banned by a special law enacted in 1925 (TDV 'fez'); the Law on the Wearing of Hats, No. 671, also dated 25 November 1925, and the Law on the Prohibition of Certain Garments, No. 2596, dated 13 December 1934, likewise left the turban only to imam-preachers, muftis and preachers while on duty (and to the head of the Directorate of Religious Affairs in the street as well) (TDV 'sarık'). No details on innovations in dress other than the fez (frock coat, trousers, shoes etc.) were found in the TDV articles on the fez, kavuk and clothing (kıyâfet); the TDV article on clothing describes the early Islamic period.", "7666d0bd"], "kisa": ["The fez, which took the place of the turban and the kavuk in 1829, was an imported product; it was made compulsory for soldiers, while the civilian population resisted in order to be able to wind a turban around it.", "64014df0"], "not": ["The TDV article on the fez bases the decision of 1829 on an imperial rescript (hatt-ı hümayun); it writes not that the fez became widespread in 1829 but that it was made compulsory for soldiers. The source gives no date for its spread to civilians.", "7e24093c"], "bag": ["On the Feshane, where the fez was produced, see the card 'feshane-osmanli-sanayilesme-girisimi'; on the army's new uniform and the abolition of the Janissary Corps see the 1826 Auspicious Incident (Vaka-i Hayriye) cards.", "940c4af4"]},
"fetret-sonu-mehmed-birlik-1413":{"oneri": ["The eleven-year war between brothers was won not by the harshest prince but by one so conciliatory that he would even call his enemy \"father\"…", "84d09bc8"], "sebep.b": ["After the defeat at Ankara, Bayezid's sons Süleyman, İsa, Musa and Mehmed fighting one another for Bursa, the \"seat of the sultanate\" (darüssaltana), and for Edirne, the centre of Rumelia", "56b7fd37"], "sonuc.b": ["Mehmed Çelebi eliminating Musa on 5 July 1413 and ascending the throne at Edirne as sole ruler of the country", "b3ecbe41"], "bag": ["The TDV article on Mehmed I (Halil İnalcık) brings to the fore two factors that determined the winner. The first is the gap in the law of succession: in Turkish tradition there was no law regulating who would take the throne, every prince had an equal right, and the people tied legitimacy to winning the war, that is, to the help of God; being older was not binding. The second is style: Musa's harsh personality and centralising, autocratic attitude alienated the frontier lords (uç beys) and vassal rulers from him, and they went over to Mehmed's side. Mehmed, for his part, followed a line so conciliatory that he called the Byzantine emperor \"father\".", "5b306dab"], "metin": ["Throughout the Interregnum the neighbours used one prince against another. Byzantium played a central role because after the treaty of 1403 it controlled the crossings between Anatolia and Rumelia; Mircea, the voivode of Wallachia, and the Anatolian beys who had returned to the lands that Timur gave back to them likewise drew close to whichever prince held Bursa. Mehmed first defeated İsa and took Bursa; when he lost Bursa to Süleyman, he withdrew to Tokat-Amasya and directed Musa to cross over into Rumelia. In 1411 Musa entered Edirne in a sudden raid and killed Süleyman, but he did not keep to his agreement with Mehmed. In the final reckoning Mehmed had on his side the frontier lords, the vassal states and the Tatar and Turkmen contingents (tümens). Unity did not bring lasting peace: while Mehmed was in Rumelia, the ruler of Karaman besieged Bursa for thirty-one days; then his brother Mustafa, released by the Timurids, and the movement of Sheikh Bedreddin started a new wave of internal conflict. The menakıbname (book of deeds) contained in Neşri counts the reigns separately: Süleyman eight years and ten months, Musa two years and seven months, Mehmed seven years and eleven months.", "129fd530"]},
"fuzuli-bagdat-kasidesi":{"baslik": ["Fuzûlî's ode to Suleiman the Lawgiver after the conquest of Baghdad", "85f7a51c"], "metin": ["After Suleiman the Lawgiver conquered Baghdad in 1534, Fuzûlî presented the sultan with five odes (kaside). With this gesture Fuzûlî left the Safavid patronage to which he had been attached until then and turned to seeking the patronage of Ottoman statesmen.", "fa609fc5"], "sanatci.ad": ["Fuzûlî (original name Mehmed)", "9d32b64b"], "sanatci.hayat": ["The date and place of his birth are not known for certain; the sources write that he was born in the vicinity of Baghdad but do not agree on the place. His father's name was Süleyman. He died in 963 (1556).", "2934b3a4"], "sanatci.onem": ["In TDV's description, \"one of the greatest poets of classical Turkish literature\"; he commanded three languages well enough to compose divans in Turkish, Arabic and Persian.", "b400ef10"], "alinti.metin": ["\"The renowned sovereign has come to the tower of the saints\" — the first line of the ode, in the form reported in the TDV article", "4b2cb4b5"]},
"fuzuli-leyla-vu-mecnun":{"baslik": ["Fuzûlî completes his masnavi Leylâ and Majnun", "e3ef4085"], "metin": ["Leylâ and Majnun (Leylâ vü Mecnûn) was written and completed by Fuzûlî in 1535, at the request of the Istanbul poets who took part in Suleiman the Lawgiver's Baghdad campaign. The work, which treats an ancient love legend of the Arabian Peninsula, is counted among the finest masnavis of classical Turkish literature.", "86d4dd43"], "sanatci.ad": ["Fuzûlî (original name Mehmed)", "9d32b64b"], "sanatci.hayat": ["The date and place of his birth are not known for certain; the sources write that he was born in the vicinity of Baghdad but do not agree on the place. His father's name was Süleyman. He died in 963 (1556).", "2934b3a4"], "sanatci.onem": ["In TDV's description, \"one of the greatest poets of classical Turkish literature\"; he commanded three languages well enough to compose divans in Turkish, Arabic and Persian.", "b400ef10"], "alinti.metin": ["Leylâ's words to Majnun when they meet in the desert: \"If I am I, then what are you, beloved? / And if you are you, what am I, wretched as I am?\" — the couplet as reported in TDV's article on Leylâ and Majnun", "8fb3cda1"]},
"girit-seferi-sunbul-aga-gemisi-1645":{"soru": ["Did a harem agha's ship open the Cretan campaign?", "06924a7c"], "baslik": ["The ship raided by corsairs and the target disguised as a 'Malta campaign'", "0e730ce9"], "metin": ["As the dismissed chief black eunuch (kızlarağası) Sünbül Agha was travelling to Egypt with a large retinue and valuable goods, his ships were raided by Maltese corsairs off the island of Karpathos (Kerpe); the goods were plundered, some of the passengers, including Sünbül Agha, were killed and others taken captive. The news greatly angered the sultan: İbrahim had the ambassadors in Istanbul asked who had carried out the raid, and ordered the fleet to be made ready; Malta, and Venice, which was considered its protector, suddenly became targets. According to the reports of the Austrian ambassador Czernin, in Ottoman diplomatic circles nothing was talked of but the sultan's order for a naval expedition against Malta; yet the real target was Crete, and this was skilfully hidden from the foreign ambassadors. On 30 April 1645 the fleet left Istanbul on a 'Malta campaign'; that the target was Crete was announced only as it set out from Navarino. Thus began a war that was to stretch over many years; nevertheless, the sources state that in this campaign strategic calculations weighed more heavily than the sultan's anger.", "d22c4a9c"]},
"hamid-devri-sansur-ve-jurnal-teskilati":{"ad": ["Censorship and informer reports: how did a state listen in on its own subjects?", "5c117549"], "metin": ["Censorship did not begin with Abdülhamid. The High Decree (Kararname-i Âli), regarded as the first censorship regulation, was published on 5 March 1867, in the reign of Sultan Abdülaziz. Its cause was that criticism directed at the government's actions was finding its way into the developing press. After the decree, figures such as Ziya Pasha, Namık Kemal and Ali Suavi fled to Europe and continued their activities there; this group became known as the Young Ottomans.\n\nTHE PLACE of the administration changed. Until 1877 the press administration was attached to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs; at that date it was attached to the Ministry of the Interior, and its administration took on a more centralised character. For the reign of Abdülhamid II, the administration and control of the press were extremely important.\n\nTHE SULTAN'S own justification. Strict censorship was applied against the divisive publishing activities carried on in Europe. In the same period, although heavy censorship was applied, the number of books, periodicals and newspapers increased greatly because publishing was supported by the sultan himself — these are two faces of the same era, and the source records both together.\n\nINFORMING was not an institution. The spies (hafiye) were not so much members of a particular organisation as volunteers who were set in motion by various expectations and were rewarded with imperial favours for what they did. They had no particular offices or centres; ostensibly they were attached to the Ministry of Police, but they sent their reports (jurnal) to the office of the chief secretary of the Imperial Chancery of the palace (Mabeyn-i Hümayun) and received their orders from there too. They were divided into four groups: the palace dignitaries called the 'upper stratum' (tabaka-i bâlâ) submitted reports directly to the sultan; the second group consisted of people assigned in the centre, in the provinces or in foreign countries; the third group worked under these; the fourth group were generally public officials.\n\nWHY there came to be so many. Two things encouraged informing: leaving those who submitted false reports unpunished, out of fear that the intelligence network would collapse, and rewarding those who submitted reports with new ranks and posts. At one point matters reached such a stage that civil servants who submitted no reports began to fear that they would come under suspicion. At times when political events increased, the number of daily reports reached several thousand.\n\nTHE SULTAN did not read them all. The incoming reports were first read to a palace chamberlain or a secretary, and once he was satisfied of their accuracy they were sent to the relevant office for action; the sultan personally opened those coming from the grand vizier, the Shaykh al-Islam, the ministers and people in important positions. The claim that all the reports were opened and read by the sultan is not true: after he was deposed, thousands of reports that had never been opened were found in chests in his apartments.\n\nWHAT THE SULTAN said. In his own words, informing was a shameful and bad thing, but it was also impossible to give it up, for nowhere in the world had intrigue reached such dreadful proportions as in the Ottoman lands. He states that two assassination attempts had been plotted against him, and says that he was able to distinguish from the others the overzealous who wrote exaggerated reports to win favour. If things went to excess, he put the blame on the chief secretaries of the palace chancery.\n\nITS END. The abolition of the spy system was discussed and accepted in the Council of Ministers (Meclis-i Vükelâ), and when the sultan too accepted the decision as it stood, the imperial decree was issued on 31 July 1908. A telegram sent to the government from Rumelia wrote that the only thing that spoiled relations between the nation and the state was the spy system. After the deposition, the hundreds of thousands of reports found in Yıldız Palace caused great trouble; a committee examined them, and when it was seen that important people in the administration of the state had been informing on one another, it was decided to destroy them; the greater part was burned and very few were sent to the archive.\n\nAND a new one took its place. For the administration that took control of Istanbul after 31 March, the same source records this: the spy system took on a new form, and the press was muzzled; under the name of 'liberty', the constitutional regime became a strange absolutism with a parliament.", "f6f529a0"], "kisa": ["The first censorship regulation was issued in 1867, that is, nine years before Abdülhamid came to the throne. Informing, for its part, was not an institution but a system of incentives in which anyone could take part — on some days the number reached several thousand.", "205fd096"], "not": ["The custom of gathering information among the people on behalf of the state is much older: the hasekis who went about in disguise, the aghas of the Inner Palace (Enderun), dervish-lodge sheikhs and wandering dervishes performed the same task. The spy system was not an invention of Abdülhamid II but a practice that reached its most widespread form in his reign. This is not an excuse but a framework showing from where to where the scale had come.", "6e6e7662"], "bag": ["For the flight of the press to Europe and the Young Ottomans, see the cards on the High Decree and the Tanzimat press; for the abolition of the spy system and the opening of parliament, see the cards on the Second Constitutional Era.", "f77c91f7"]},
"hamidiye-alaylari-1890-tartisma":{"ad": ["The Hamidiye Regiments: frontier defence, or an instrument of pressure against the Armenians?", "1e8aaa1b"], "metin": ["Foundation. According to the TDV article on the Hamidiye Regiments, the regiments began to be formed in early 1890 from the Arab, Kurdish, Turkmen and Karakalpak tribes of Eastern Anatolia. The official justification was to extend military service to the tribes; among the aims the article also explicitly lists establishing central authority, strengthening the frontier against a possible Russian attack, and “preventing the activities carried on by the Armenians in the region”. The number of regiments rose to 45 in 1892 and to 65 in 1901; on 13 May 1896 the founding law, of 121 articles, was published. In return for weapons and ammunition alone, the state gained at least 35,000 horsemen; members of the regiments were exempt from all taxes other than the tithe and the sheep tax.\n\nThe STATE's expectation. According to the same article, the state hoped that by bringing the tribes under military discipline it would also prevent the lawlessness of which the Armenians complained, and so remove one of the great powers' pretexts for intervention; it writes that while Britain, after 1878, demanded arrangements in favour of the Armenians, Russia was supporting Armenian bands with arms.\n\nTHE OUTCOME — the state's own report. The TDV article reports that after the events of 1890-1895, in the reform project submitted by Britain, France and Russia on 11 May 1895, they demanded strict supervision of the regiments, and that Abdülhamid II opposed this demand and wished to protect the tribal chiefs. In 1897 Marshal Ahmed Şakir Pasha and Zeki Pasha were charged with putting the regiments in order, and in the report they prepared in December 1897 they acknowledged that discipline had not been achieved, that the regiments were only loosely subordinate to military authority, that some officers had entered into relations of mutual interest with tribal chiefs, and that the large tribes, by becoming dominant in their areas, had shaken the social balance.\n\nTWO readings. The picture drawn by the TDV shows the regiments as a force established for public order and frontier defence, but one that could not be controlled in practice. The great powers' project of 1895, and the reform draft of 1914 reported by the TDV article on tehcir, by contrast, regard the regiments as one of the sources of violence against the Armenians: according to the article on tehcir, the giving of lands belonging to Armenians to nomadic Kurds in the reign of Abdülhamid was one of the most important causes of Kurdish-Armenian hostility, and the 1914 draft provided for the return of these lands; according to the article on the Ottomans, too, the 1914 reform plan included the abolition of the Hamidiye regiments.\n\nAFTERWARDS. After the Second Constitutional Era the organisation was renamed the 'tribal regiments', and in 1912 'tribal cavalry divisions' were established; according to the TDV, these units took part in the defence of Eastern Anatolia in the First World War and in the National Struggle.", "86ba3fad"], "kisa": ["The cavalry regiments formed in 1890 from the tribes of Eastern Anatolia gave the state a cheap army; but the state's own inspectors also reported that the regiments could not be brought under discipline and had shaken the social balance of the region.", "eff1fda2"], "not": ["The sources consulted do not give the regiments' direct role in the events of the 1890s — which regiment was present at which event. The Armenian side's account of the regiments and that of Western consular reports were not read; the card mentions the opposing view only to the extent that the TDV reports it.", "2889c0b3"], "bag": ["For the events of the 1890s, see the card 'Armenian committees and revolts'.", "77e420a0"]},
"harfus-ailesi-balebek-yerel-hakimiyet":{"ad": ["The Harfush emirs of Baalbek: how was a local rule lasting centuries possible?", "d9917b7f"], "metin": ["Who the family was. The TDV article 'Harfûş' writes that the word was used in the Mamluk period for the lower-class people of the great cities, and that in Ottoman times it was also used as the name of the Shiite family of emirs that ruled the Baalbek and Beqaa regions of Damascus province; it is not known whether there is any connection between this family and the harfushes of Egypt. A member of the family known as Ibn al-Harfush became sheikh of the harfushes in Egypt and, after the Ottomans took Damascus, probably returned here. He joined Nasir al-Din Ibn Hanash, who rebelled against the Ottoman administration in Damascus and seized Sidon and the Beqaa, and was later killed by Janbirdi al-Ghazali, the beylerbey of Damascus.\n\nSETTLEMENT and power. According to TDV, the Harfush family settled in the Baalbek region during Janbirdi's revolt. Baalbek itself had passed under Ottoman rule in 1516 during the Syrian campaign of Sultan Selim the Grim, and from that date it remained in the hands of 'petty beys, especially the Harfush family'. Because the Harfush emirs tried to bring Baalbek and its surroundings under their influence, they were from time to time chastised by the beylerbeys of Damascus, Tripoli (of Syria) and Aleppo, and from time to time by the Ma'ns or the Shihabs, local powers in Damascus province. From the beginning of the seventeenth century rivalry arose among the Harfush emirs for dominance in Baalbek and the Beqaa, and some tried to cooperate with their enemies in order to reach their goal. The explicit reason TDV gives for the family's own power is this: the Harfush emirs took every opportunity to profit from the disputes that arose between the Ottoman administration in Damascus and the local powers, and in this way maintained their rule until the middle of the nineteenth century.\n\nTHE CONDITION of Baalbek. According to Evliya Çelebi, Baalbek was a subaşılık attached to the province of Damascus and a kaza of 150 akçe. With the shifting of the Mamluk postal routes to the Damascus-Homs and Damascus-Tripoli lines, the town's importance declined; later, affected by the quarrels among the families in Syria, the town's prosperity fell and its population diminished; in the earthquake of 1759 its buildings were destroyed. In 1831 it was seized by İbrahim Pasha, son of Mehmed Ali Pasha, the governor of Egypt, and was later recovered. In 1850, by a new arrangement of the Sublime Porte, it was made the centre of a kaza subject to the province of Damascus.\n\nWHAT remains open. (1) Tax-farming (iltizam), tribal and sectarian support and geography may be listed as sources of the family's power; but TDV gives as sourced only the sentence above (profiting from the disputes between the Damascus administration and local forces); the others are this page's interpretation, not information tied to a source. (2) The date of 'the end of local rule': TDV says only 'until the middle of the nineteenth century'; 1850 is the year in which Baalbek was made a kaza, and TDV does not directly connect this arrangement with the ending of the Harfush family. Some secondary accounts date the exile of the last members of the family to 1865; no academic basis for this information could be reached. (3) A year-by-year chronology of the family's rulers and the individual events (the clashes with the Ma'ns and the Shihabs) were not found in TDV.", "3e897fb1"], "kisa": ["A Shiite family of emirs that stayed within the Ottoman order and profited from the weaknesses of Damascus made its word count in the Beqaa valley until the middle of the nineteenth century.", "2458be4b"], "not": ["'Harfûş' occurs in TDV in two separate senses: the lower-class/gang element in Cairo of the Mamluk period and the Shiite family of emirs of Baalbek and the Beqaa. The card describes only the second.", "663358f2"], "bag": ["On local dynasties in Ottoman provincial administration see the ayan cards; on the taking of Baalbek by İbrahim Pasha see the Egyptian campaign cards.", "07d3a705"]},
"harp93-ayastefanos-ne-getirdi":{"baslik": ["What did the Treaty of San Stefano bring? (1878)", "ffb42cea"], "metin": ["■ How it came about\nWith the fall of Plevna and the loss of Kars in the east, the fronts collapsed. Under the Armistice of Edirne signed on 31 January 1878, Erzurum was to be surrendered to the Russians, an autonomous Principality of Bulgaria was to be established on condition that it be no smaller than the borders specified at the Istanbul Conference, the independence of Serbia, Montenegro and Romania was to be recognised, a war indemnity was to be paid, and the Russians were to be given certain privileges in the Straits. After the Russians moved their headquarters to Yeşilköy (San Stefano), the peace negotiations continued there, and the treaty was signed on 3 March 1878.\n\n■ TWENTY-NINE ARTICLES\nUnder the treaty, the Ottoman State was to accept the independence of Romania, Montenegro and Serbia. Bulgaria was to be turned into an autonomous principality subject to the Ottomans, and its borders were to extend FROM THE DANUBE TO THE AEGEAN SEA AND FROM ALBANIA TO THE BLACK SEA; the prince was to be freely elected by the people and appointed with the approval of the European powers and the confirmation of the Ottomans. The 1868 regulation was to be applied in Crete. The war indemnity was set at 1,410,000,000 roubles; against the greater part of this indemnity, certain places in Rumelia, together with Kars, Ardahan, Batum and Doğubayazıt, were to be ceded to Russia. Russian troops were to evacuate Rumelia, excluding Bulgaria, three months after the signing of the treaty, and Eastern Anatolia six months after it.\n\n■ WHY IT DID NOT SURVIVE\nA Bulgaria reaching down to the Aegean meant Russia's opening onto the Mediterranean, and neither Britain nor Austria could accept this. For this reason the articles of the treaty were taken up again and changed at the Congress of Berlin, which convened on 13 June 1878. San Stefano remained in force for four months.\n\n■ BUT ITS MARK REMAINED\nA treaty that was legally dead had a far longer political life: the broad frontier of San Stefano lived on for the next forty years as the map of the Bulgarian national goal. The annexation of Eastern Rumelia in 1885, the Balkan Wars of 1912-1913 and the choices made in the First World War are explained by this map. In other words, a text that lived for four months became the cause of four wars.", "25061208"]},
"harp93-balkan-goc-dalgasi":{"baslik": ["The Balkan wave of migration of the War of '93", "43ff598b"], "metin": ["■ What happened\nAt the end of the war the Turkish population, especially in Bulgaria, was removed from lands it had regarded as its homeland for centuries, both by being killed and by being forced to migrate. The source consulted describes this movement as \"a complete annihilation of the Turks\" and writes that the number of refugees in Istanbul came to be counted in the hundreds of thousands.\n\n■ WHAT THE CITY BECAME\nThis was more than a capital city could bear. The problem of shelter, food and epidemics fell on top of a state that had lost the war and was to pay an indemnity. The refugees (muhacir) were settled in settlements founded in various parts of Anatolia; this resettlement is the origin of the settlements whose names are still remembered today as \"refugee villages\".\n\n■ ITS POLITICAL CONSEQUENCE\nThe migration was not only a human catastrophe but also a source of political pressure. On 20 May 1878, with the support of a group of the Balkan refugees who had gathered in Istanbul, an attempted raid was made against the palace; this attempt, which aimed to depose the sultan and replace him with his predecessor, was one of the events that reinforced the suspicious style of government of the period. In other words, the wave of migration also shaped domestic politics directly.\n\n■ AFTER INDEPENDENCE\nThe migration did not end in 1878. In the newly founded states, Muslim emigration accelerated after independence, and the proportion of Muslims in the population of the Balkans continued to fall for decades. Something similar happened in the east: after the Kars region passed to Russian rule, the Muslim population declined because large numbers of people migrated to Anatolia; in the same years German, Ossetian, Greek, Molokan, Armenian and Polish communities were settled in the region, and in the 1897 census an abnormal increase was observed in the population of the sanjak.\n\n■ WHY IT DESERVES A SEPARATE CARD\nOn a map, a border line changes and can be seen with the eye. The movement of populations is invisible on the map, but its effect lasts longer: the political cadres of the following fifty years were the children of families that had migrated.", "c5f09769"]},
"harp93-kibris-ve-ingiltereye-yaslanma":{"baslik": ["The cession of Cyprus to Britain (1878) — the price of Berlin", "7ee91fa4"], "metin": ["■ The calculation\nThe Treaty of San Stefano was unacceptably heavy for the Ottomans, and the only way out was to bring the treaty to the common table of Europe and have it changed. The only state that could do this was Britain, which was the most disturbed by Russia's descent to the Mediterranean. But Britain did not give its support for nothing.\n\n■ THE SECRET AGREEMENT\nAt the end of secret negotiations, the agreement that temporarily left the administration of Cyprus to Britain was signed on 4 June 1878 — nine days before the Congress of Berlin convened. The sultan ratified the agreement after obtaining from the British a document stating that his sovereign rights over the island would in no way be harmed. In a supplementary agreement dated 1 July 1878, a clause was added stating that Britain would withdraw from Cyprus if Russia returned the places it had occupied in Eastern Anatolia, thereby establishing that the territorial ownership of the island belonged to the Ottomans.\n\n■ WHAT WAS WON IN BERLIN\nThe gain was real: the Bulgaria that reached down to the Aegean was divided into three, and Doğubayazıt and the Eleşkirt valley remained with the Ottomans. But the price was real too — and the price was permanent, while the gain was temporary.\n\n■ THE CHAIN EFFECT\nOttoman diplomacy had given up Cyprus for the sake of the support that Britain had promised at the Congress of Berlin. This had one further, less visible consequence: the handing over of the island increased the activity of other states in this regard. At Britain's encouragement, the administration of Bosnia and Herzegovina was left to Austria; in 1881 France seized Tunis, and the following year Britain itself seized Egypt by a fait accompli. In other words, one concession lowered the price of concessions.\n\n■ THE LESSON\n\"Leaning on Britain to be rid of the Russians\" was not a mistake but the least bad of the available options; the mistake was to regard the price paid as TEMPORARY. The administration of the island was transferred \"temporarily\" and the sovereign rights were secured by a document — and the island never came back. It is one of the clearest examples showing that a guarantee on paper has no value at all if there is no power to enforce it.", "a4fcc921"]},
"harp93-plevne-savunmasi":{"baslik": ["The defence of Plevna (1877) — a resistance that lasted five months", "950f4445"]},
"hasta-adam-tabiri":{"baslik": ["The phrase \"sick man\" — who said it, and why?", "57c33f09"], "metin": ["■ How it came about\nThe expression is a metaphor that the Russian Tsar Nicholas I used for the Ottoman State in January 1853. It was said neither on a battlefield nor in an official note, but in private conversations with the British ambassador in Russia. The purpose of the metaphor was not to make a diagnosis but to open an OFFER: to discuss how the inheritance would be divided before the patient died.\n\n■ WHY PRECISELY THAT YEAR\nThe timing was no coincidence. In the same year Russia formally made demands showing that it aimed to establish sole dominance over Ottoman lands, and the Crimean War broke out. When the war ended in Russia's defeat, what emerged was this: the Ottoman State could not resist alone, but with the support of France, Britain and Austria it could stand up to Russia. In other words, the diagnosis of the \"sick man\" was correct, and the treatment was under way — and those administering the treatment were at the same time candidates for the inheritance.\n\n■ THE BACKGROUND OF THE PHRASE: THE EASTERN QUESTION\nThe expression was not born in a vacuum. The fate of the Ottoman lands was referred to in European diplomacy as the \"Eastern Question\". It is claimed that the concept appeared as a historical term in the talks at Verona in 1822; but this was nothing other than the naming of a process that had already become evident at the beginning of the century. At that date the concept pointed mainly to the Ottoman lands in South-Eastern Europe; the Middle East and Africa remained outside it until they became an arena of Anglo-French struggle for influence.\n\n■ AND THE SICK MAN WAS NOT ALONE\nThe most striking detail is this: the same metaphor was also used for another empire. After Austria had been compelled to establish a new state with a Hungarian wing, it began to share the same weakness as the Ottomans, in that it had different ethnic and religious peoples who wanted to become nation-states, and it saw the Eastern Question being discussed with regard to itself too. The \"sick man on the Bosphorus\" and the \"sick man on the Danube\" ultimately fell victim to the same ailment — both came to an end in 1918.\n\n■ WHY IT CAUGHT ON SO WELL\nThe power of the phrase lies in the way it presents the collapse of a state as an inevitable and natural process. What is to be done for a sick person is clear: to wait and to arrange the inheritance. In such a framework, intervening counts not as aggression but as precaution. For this reason the expression is a TOOL OF POLICY more than an observation — and it served precisely the purpose of hastening the partition.", "f5b3263d"], "kisa": ["The phrase was used not by an enemy but by a partner who wanted to share the inheritance: declaring the patient sick made it legitimate to discuss the inheritance ahead of time.", "8dcb13f8"]},
"hezarpare-ahmed-pasa-bin-parca-1648":{"soru": ["Why was the grand vizier called 'Hezarpâre'?", "c24021a2"], "baslik": ["The grand vizier who hid Klis and his end in 'a thousand pieces'", "63125c44"], "metin": ["Ahmed Pasha, who had risen from the financial bureaus thanks to his fine and rapid handwriting, became grand vizier in September 1647, reportedly by presenting the sultan with 300,000 kuruş. When the Venetians took the fortress of Klis in Dalmatia, he hid this from the sultan and made what had happened look like the loss of a small church and a few unimportant palankas (palisaded forts); the sultan rebuked the vizier Fazlı Pasha, who told the truth, and had him removed from Istanbul. He even forbade talk of the war in the palace. Naîmâ relates that the grand vizier planned to have the aghas of the Janissaries, whom he had invited to his son's wedding, killed all together, and that the aghas sensed the trap and fled; this plot does not appear in the other sources of the period. When the aghas and the ulema rose, he fled in disguise with a saddlebag of gold, but the acquaintances with whom he sought shelter would not open their doors; caught after a denunciation, he was strangled on the night of 7-8 August 1648. According to Evliya Çelebi, his corpse, thrown into the Hippodrome (Atmeydanı), was cut to pieces in the belief that human fat was good for joint pains; from this comes the nickname Hezarpâre, meaning 'a thousand pieces'. The Istanbul district of Paşabahçe also takes its name from his garden there.", "544c0b67"], "not": ["The wedding trap appears only in Naîmâ as a source; the reason for the cutting up of the corpse is Evliya Çelebi's account.", "1a710b7d"]},
"hukum-alani-mesafe":{"baslik": ["How Far Can a Capital Rule? Post Stations, Day Marches and the Campaign Season", "072f6104"], "metin": ["How far away can a capital really rule over territory? The Ottomans answered this question not in kilometers but in hours — and the records they left behind can be measured today.\n\n■ THE MENZIL: THE SPEED OF NEWS\nThe empire had set up halting points called menzil (post stations) along its roads. These were not evenly spaced; the source says so plainly: menzils were established at distances of “from three hours up to twenty-eight hours”, “according to geographical conditions, security and the places where the needs of the post station could be supplied”. In other words, from the outset the measure was not a flat distance but the difficulty of the terrain.\n\n■ HOW MANY KILOMETERS IS AN “HOUR”?\nWe have the daily register of the Uyvar campaign of 1663: each of the sixty-four halts the army passed from Davutpaşa to Buda is recorded with its hours. When measured with modern coordinates, all seven separate stages give the same band: one Ottoman hour is 4.3 to 5.4 kilometers as the crow flies. Three independent sources converge on the same figure.\n\n■ THE SAME WORD, TWO DIFFERENT SCALES\nAnd here is a distinction that escapes most readers. A KONAK is one day's march of the army: 19 kilometers on average. A MENZIL, by contrast, is the interval between the courier's stations: 85 kilometers on average. There is a four-and-a-half-fold difference between them, and the reason is simple — at the menzil the courier CHANGES HORSES and rides on; the army walks and encamps. These are two different speeds on the same road network. The place an order reaches and the place an army arrives are not the same.\n\n■ THE BOUNDARY DRAWN BY LAW\nWhat is interesting is that Islamic law, too, gave an answer to the same question. The distance required for a person to count as a “traveler” and be allowed to shorten his prayers has been estimated in all four schools of law at between 83 and 99 kilometers. In other words, in law, anything beyond this is already SOMEWHERE ELSE. This figure also agrees with the boundaries of the historical kazas (judicial districts under a qadi).\n\n■ NESTED RINGS\nWhen all this is put together, a nested order emerges: a fortress or village could truly rule up to about 40 kilometers around it, a kaza center up to 90 kilometers, a sanjak center up to 150 kilometers, and a provincial (eyalet) center up to 270 kilometers. Each tier is roughly the square root of three times the one before it — the very pattern that geographers describe in central place theory.\n\n■ AND THE REAL ANSWER: VIENNA WAS NOT TOO FAR\nThe campaign of 1663 set out from Davutpaşa on 18 March and reached Buda on 17 July: 121 days. But within those 121 days there had been only 258 hours of marching. That is, almost the whole campaign had been spent not in marching but in encamping, supplying and waiting. The army's net progress was 8.8 kilometers a day.\n\nThe campaign season — the period between the melting of the snow and its return — was roughly 240 days, and the outward march, the fighting and the return HAD TO FIT into that time. By this measure:\n\n   Going to Belgrade and back takes 184 days — comfortable.\n   Going to Buda and back takes 243 days — the whole season; no days are left for fighting.\n   Going to Vienna and back takes 290 days — it does NOT FIT into the season.\n\nThis is the answer to why the empire's northwestern frontier stopped exactly there. Vienna was not too far; it was too LATE. What drew the frontier was not a distance but a CALENDAR — and the same reckoning also places Baghdad, Algiers and Mecca among the places that could not be fitted into a single campaign. Those lands were administered by other methods, through governors, garrison corps and vassal principalities.\n\n■ THE LIMITS OF THE MEASUREMENT\nThe daily speed used here is derived from the register of a single campaign; it is not an exact figure but an ORDER OF MAGNITUDE. Even if it varied by thirty percent, Vienna would still fall outside the season — this does not change the conclusion, but one should not ascribe precision to the number.", "0770eac2"], "kisa": ["Vienna was not too far — it was too late. An empire's frontier was drawn not by distance but by the calendar.", "6d8e95c7"]},
"hurrem-nikah-buyu-soylentisi":{"soru": ["Did Hürrem bind the sultan to her with sorcery?", "f0f268a4"], "baslik": ["A marriage that broke with custom and the rumour of 'sorcery'", "8c52c002"], "metin": ["In the Ottoman palace it was not an obligation for the sultan to enter into a marriage contract with his concubines. According to the TDV, after the death of his mother Hafsa Sultan in 1534, Suleiman the Lawgiver sent Mahidevran to join her son Mustafa in Manisa and, contrary to this custom, formally married Hürrem; a Venetian source writes that the decision was announced to the public and was received with discontent. Some contemporary European writers, such as Busbecq, report that Hürrem won the sultan's heart through sorcery — the encyclopaedia passes this on merely as a report.", "a3926b98"], "not": ["The marriage itself is documented; the 'sorcery' is a claim, and its truth is not asserted.", "95ded953"]},
"ibrahim-cinci-hoca-nefes-ve-dam-1642":{"soru": ["How did Cinci Hoca become a military judge, and how did he fall?", "b6b4e095"], "baslik": ["From a powerful breath to a leap off the roof: Cinci Hoca", "a5d59926"], "metin": ["Hüseyin Efendi of Safranbolu won fame in Istanbul, before he could finish his madrasa, for his 'powerful breath' and the prayers he recited. He was summoned to the palace by Kösem Sultan to give relief to Sultan İbrahim, who suffered from mental distress; once he became the sultan's favourite, a furnished mansion was built for him beside the Mahmud Pasha Mosque, and the hoca, who had not completed his madrasa education, became first a professor (müderris), then the sultan's tutor, and in 1644 military judge (kazasker) of Anatolia. His contemporary Karaçelebizâde speaks of him as one who had crept into the sultan like evil spirits. When İbrahim was deposed, money was demanded from him for the new sultan's accession largesse; the hoca resisted, and when his house was raided he panicked and leapt from the roof onto a neighbour's house, was dragged out of his hiding place and beaten. Chests full of gold and jewels and more than fifty sable furs came out of his house; for a while the people called the money of his that was distributed to the soldiers 'Cinci coins' (Cinci akçesi, from cinci, 'dealer in jinn'). A few months later, in the autumn of 1648, he was killed.", "59fbc84a"]},
"ibrahim-cocuk-yastaki-kizlar-damat-vezirler":{"soru": ["To whom did İbrahim marry off his little daughters?", "3396747b"], "baslik": ["A bride at four, a wife at two: the sultan's vizier sons-in-law", "9c814f9a"], "metin": ["One of Sultan İbrahim's customs that the sources regard as strange was marrying off his child daughters to viziers with great festivities. Silahdar Yusuf Pasha, who took Chania (Hanya), was a son-in-law who had been married to the sultan's daughter, then only four years old. However, gossip spread that he had not brought from Chania the booty the palace expected; when the sultan ordered that Crete be taken almost in its entirety, the pasha objected, citing the winter season, and argued with the sultan. He was executed on 22 January 1646. To make Ahmed Pasha, who had been trained in the chancery bureaucracy (kalemiye) and had risen to the grand vizierate, his son-in-law, the sultan separated him from his lawful wife and married him to his two-year-old daughter Beyhan Sultan (February 1648). A few months later this son-in-law too was to fall into the hands of the rebels.", "6693f2ed"]},
"ibrahim-culus-iki-kez-bakti-1640":{"soru": ["Why did İbrahim not believe that his elder brother had died?", "c1ea8ea0"], "baslik": ["The prince who would not leave his room: 'Go and look for yourself'", "2dce58eb"], "metin": ["During the reign of Murad IV his brothers Bayezid, Süleyman and Kasım had been strangled one by one; İbrahim, the last surviving male of the dynasty, lived in fear that his turn would come. When his elder brother died, the chief gate agha (kapı ağası) sent by the grand vizier came and told him that the throne was now his, but İbrahim took this to be a trap laid to get him out of his room and kill him, and would not step outside. According to Naîmâ's account, the chief gate agha and his mother Kösem Sultan took him by the arm and persuaded him, saying 'go and look for yourself'; İbrahim was not content with seeing his brother's body but looked twice to be sure, and only then sat on the throne. A report relayed by the French traveller Du Loir relates that on his deathbed Murad IV wanted İbrahim killed and that Kösem Sultan prevented it; the Ottoman historian Solakzâde, on the contrary, writes that the dying sultan summoned his brother to his side and bequeathed the throne to him.", "4b5cc6b8"], "not": ["The scene of looking twice at the body rests on Naîmâ's account. The order to kill given on the deathbed is the report of a foreign observer; among the Ottoman sources there is also an account saying the opposite.", "c0dad9b3"]},
"ibrahim-hal-gunu-yumruk-ve-oda-1648":{"soru": ["What did İbrahim do on the day he was deposed?", "86fd0daf"], "baslik": ["He handed over the seal and punched the grand vizier: the day of deposition", "95dc019c"], "metin": ["On the morning of 7 August 1648 the aghas of the Janissaries gathered at the Orta Mosque on the Hippodrome (Atmeydanı) and the ulema at the Fatih Mosque, and proclaimed Sofu Mehmed Pasha grand vizier. Sultan İbrahim summoned the new grand vizier to the palace and gave him the seal, asking only that his son-in-law Ahmed Pasha be left untouched. When the assembly would not accept this, Mehmed Pasha entered the presence a second time to announce the decision; according to Kâtib Çelebi, the sultan rebuked him, used harsh words and even punched him. The next day, when the delegation came with the decision to depose him, Kösem Sultan at first objected, saying 'there can be no accession in a mosque', and argued that raising a small child to the throne would not be in accordance with the sharia; in the end she brought out the seven-year-old Prince Mehmed. Karaçelebizâde, who was himself involved in the events, relates that while İbrahim's shouts could be heard from inside, he took the bewildered prince by the arm and seated him on the throne. According to Naîmâ, the delegation accused the former sultan in harsh words, and he in turn cursed them. İbrahim was shut up with two of his concubines in a two-domed room prepared a day earlier; ten days later he was to be strangled in that same room.", "7c0b1c76"], "not": ["The exchange of words between the delegation and İbrahim is Naîmâ's account; Karaçelebizâde, who was involved in the events, does not mention this conversation.", "8712bff7"]},
"ibrahim-hasekiler-pasmaklik-eyaletler":{"soru": ["Did the sultan hand out provinces to his hasekis?", "23f73e61"], "baslik": ["Seven hasekis, estates worth a hundred thousand kuruş and 'slipper-money' provinces", "fdcb6e70"], "metin": ["That İbrahim, the only male heir of the dynasty when he came to the throne, should have children was Kösem Sultan's first concern; the number of concubines and favourites presented to the sultan rose rapidly. The historian Vecîhî writes that he made his favourites hasekis (favourite consorts), assigned them large revenue estates (has), and that choice provinces and sanjaks were given to the hasekis under the name of 'paşmaklık' (literally 'slipper money'). The sources of the period particularly stress that the annual revenues of the estates of the seven hasekis reached a hundred thousand kuruş, that the revenue of the province of Damascus was set aside for one of the hasekis, and that offices were bought and sold through the mediation of women and by bribery. The granting of important vacant provinces to the favourites as revenue estates disturbed even Grand Vizier Salih Pasha; and Varvar Ali Pasha, beylerbeyi of Sivas, based his rebellion of 1648 on the claim that the affairs of state had passed into the hands of women. Recent research, however, reminds us that these accusations against women gained weight with the political tension of the last years of the reign. Of the sons born of this crowded harem, three, Mehmed IV, Süleyman II and Ahmed II, later became sultans.", "d2639daf"], "not": ["The number and revenues of the hasekis rest on the chronicles of the period; how far these accounts are exaggerated is debated among historians.", "5b5e14e7"]},
"ibrahim-katli-kim-emretti-1648":{"soru": ["Who had the confined Sultan İbrahim strangled?", "b9e03ae8"], "baslik": ["The room whose lock was filled with lead", "1f0addfc"], "metin": ["The deposed Sultan İbrahim was shut up with two concubines in a room with two domes; according to Karaçelebizâde, lead was poured into the lock of the door. Ten days later he was strangled, but the sources differ on the reason and on who was responsible. Mehmed Halîfe writes that the sipahis, who were at odds with the Janissaries over the accession gratuity, wanted to restore him to the throne, and that thereupon the grand vizier, the Shaykh al-Islam and the naqib al-ashraf (head of the Prophet's descendants) secured his execution. Karaçelebizâde, who was involved in the events, relates that some of the bostancıs (palace guards) attempted to get him out of the room, that Kösem Sultan passed on the news, and that he himself was deliberately kept away from the decision; he places the responsibility on Kösem Sultan and the statesmen. The TDV notes that these statements blaming Kösem cannot be confirmed by any other source and that Naîmâ's dramatic account is far from credible.", "111a2a49"], "not": ["Kâtib Çelebi writes that the execution was carried out by the executioner Kara Ali. The account of the day of his deposition is on a separate card, and the debate over the nickname \"the Mad\" is in the entry dated 1644.", "b71f2268"]},
"ibrahim-kosem-anne-ogul-cekismesi":{"soru": ["Why did Kösem Sultan fall out with her son İbrahim?", "5a1bbc93"], "baslik": ["The mother who saved her son, the valide removed from his palace", "d6816835"], "metin": ["While Murad IV was having his brothers killed one by one, Kösem Sultan had saved İbrahim by presenting him as a helpless and pitiable creature, and had also secured his accession. In her son's first years she again became the most powerful woman of the palace, but in time İbrahim ceased to listen to her. Kösem made great efforts to remove the sultan's female companion (musâhibe) Şekerpâre Hatun from the palace, and managed only with difficulty to have her exiled. According to the historian Vecîhî, the grand valide often warned her son about the bad course of affairs, but İbrahim paid no heed; Kösem was first forced to leave the Harem and settle in a garden at Topkapı, then in the İskender Çelebi Garden at Bakırköy. According to one report, the sultan even thought of banishing his mother to Rhodes. In 1648, when the decision to depose him was brought, Kösem at first defended her son, but in the end consented to her grandson Mehmed being raised to the throne; the historian Karaçelebizâde, who was himself involved in the events, writes that she too had a share in the decision to strangle İbrahim.", "1026ba38"], "not": ["The plan to exile her to Rhodes is a report. Kösem's share in the execution decision appears only in Karaçelebizâde's account.", "b994916c"]},
"ibrahim-ot-arabasi-salih-pasa-1647":{"soru": ["Did a hay cart cost a grand vizier his life?", "568bce01"], "baslik": ["The hay cart in the way and the grand vizier strangled with a well rope", "3c2e22a6"], "metin": ["As his ailments grew worse, Sultan İbrahim began to go frequently to the houses of people whose breath or recitations he believed did him good. Since he did not want carts to get in his way on these outings, he ordered Grand Vizier Salih Pasha to have the roads supervised. According to most of the sources, one day, as he was going to the house of an imam at Davutpaşa to have prayers recited over him, a hay cart came out in front of him; thinking that his order had not been carried out, the sultan had the grand vizier, who was then presiding over the afternoon council (ikindi divanı), summoned there and strangled him with a well rope in that same house (September 1647). The historian Vecîhî gives a different reason: he writes that Salih Pasha had secretly discussed deposing the sultan with the Shaykh al-Islam, and that the Shaykh al-Islam would not go along with it and informed Kösem Sultan, who in turn informed her son; some researchers find this second account the stronger one. The seal of the grand vizierate was sent to Köse Musa Pasha, husband of the sultan's female companion Şekerpâre Hatun, but before he arrived Ahmed Pasha, who was holding his place, succeeded in getting the seal for himself.", "682b19de"], "not": ["The reason for the killing of Salih Pasha is told in two different ways in the sources; the card gives both.", "de9f0300"]},
"ibrahim-samur-meraki-falci-hikayesi":{"soru": ["Was İbrahim's passion for sable born of a tale?", "664599e3"], "baslik": ["The fortune-teller's tale and the pavilions lined with sable", "39efd96c"], "metin": ["Sultan İbrahim's name is mentioned together with his fondness for sable fur and ambergris. According to what Naîmâ relays from Şârihülmenârzâde, this passion began with the stories, which reached the palace, of a woman fortune-teller living in Eyüp and known as 'the Voivode's Daughter': one night the woman told of a sultan of olden times who had everything, from his clothes to his pillow, made of sable, and İbrahim was impressed by the story. The historian Vecîhî writes that viziers and administrators in the provinces were ordered to line their pavilions with sable fur, that those who did not comply were dismissed, and that viziers and ulema were each asked to present a robe of sable inside and out. Grand Vizier Ahmed Pasha turned this passion into a sable and ambergris levy collected from the leading men of the state; when he demanded the levy from the aghas of the Janissaries as well, the revolt that prepared the sultan's end began. A recent study that re-examines archival documents, however, argues that sable circulated in the palace as a diplomatic gift and a ceremonial bestowal, and that the documents do not confirm an obsession that ruined the state.", "dc2682e0"], "not": ["The story of the fortune-teller is a report resting on the account of a single chronicler. The extent of the passion for sable is disputed among historians.", "b90f0d89"]},
"ibrahim-telli-haseki-nikahi":{"soru": ["What happened in Istanbul at night for the sake of Telli Haseki?", "a042177e"], "baslik": ["The eighth haseki: a marriage contract, a wedding and shops forced open at night", "ff10b9b5"], "metin": ["The Ottoman sultans had not made it a custom to contract marriage with their concubines. Breaking with this practice, Sultan İbrahim formally married his eighth haseki (favourite consort), Hümâşah, the favourite who is more often called 'Telli Haseki' in the sources, and held a lavish wedding for her in the palace; those around him were expected to give large presents. The İbrahim Pasha Palace on Sultanahmet Square was furnished for the new lady to live in. For this purpose inns and covered markets (bedestens) were forced open at midnight; the valuable fabrics, sable and lynx furs and jewels in them were taken by force, to be paid for later. A room in the palace was covered with sable furs; the finance official (defterdar) Halıcızâde, who was in charge of the work, was imprisoned for three nights on the grounds that he had used defective material in the furnishing.", "b95180cf"], "not": ["In the sources she is also referred to by the names Hümâşah and Şah Sultan.", "8646e8f7"]},
"ihtilal-avusturya-etkisi":{"baslik": ["Why did Austria dissolve with its own hand an 844-year-old crown?", "d5ad28c5"], "metin": ["TDV's `avusturya` article does not treat this period (the Napoleonic wars of 1792-1815) in detail — it only briefly mentions the changes of title of 1804 and 1867; this is a gap where the encyclopaedia does not go into detail (TDV views Austria as a neighbour of the Ottomans and does not treat purely internal European history in such detail). This card rests on the page's chronology entries previously verified with an academic source (Die Welt der Habsburger).\n\nA DYNASTIC and ideological war. On 20 April 1792 revolutionary France declared war on Austria; for the emperor the matter had two layers — dynastic, as a relative of Marie Antoinette, but at the same time ideological, against a regime that threatened absolutism. Thus began a series of wars that was to last twenty-three years. The first great reckoning ended to Austria's disadvantage: by the Treaty of Campo Formio of 17 October 1797 the Austrian Netherlands (Belgium) was ceded to France, and in return the territories of the Republic of Venice were obtained (see the Italy card) — the Habsburg centre of gravity shifted from the West to the Adriatic.\n\nA DEFENSIVE move — a new crown. In response to Napoleon's proclaiming himself Emperor of the French in 1804, Emperor Francis issued a decree on 11 August 1804 and took the title of Emperor of Austria; the Habsburg lands were for the first time united under a single state name. This move was both a reply to Napoleon's title and an insurance against a future loss (below).\n\nAUSTERLITZ and collapse. On 2 December 1805 the Russians and Austrians were decisively defeated in the \"Battle of the Three Emperors\" (Austerlitz). The Peace of Pressburg that followed brought a heavy price: Venice, Istria and Dalmatia passed to France, and all of Further Austria to Bavaria — a loss of about three million inhabitants.\n\nThe end of the HOLY Roman Empire. The insurance worked: on 6 August 1806 Francis withdrew from the title of Holy Roman Emperor, and the 844-year-old institution came to an end — the Habsburg dynasty's four-hundred-year monopoly on the crown was over, and the dynasty was now the ruler only of its own (newly proclaimed) Austrian Empire. A new defeat at Wagram in 1809 (after the temporary success at Aspern) ended the war that Austria waged single-handed against Napoleon.\n\nRESULT. Austria was the European power that resisted Revolutionary France the longest, but this resistance took it from being the heir of a thousand-year-old institution (the Holy Roman Empire) and turned it into a single, hereditary empire in the modern sense (the Austrian Empire, after 1867 Austria-Hungary) — while fighting the Revolution, it was compelled to transform its own institutional identity as well.", "33f49d5e"], "kisa": ["Austria was the first great power to fight Revolutionary France; fourteen years later, at the end of these same wars, it was compelled to dissolve with its own hand its own 844-year-old crown (the Holy Roman Empire).", "5ae766e9"]},
"ihtilal-italya-etkisi":{"baslik": ["Italy's first rehearsal for unification — and the end of Venice", "5fdeb9f0"], "metin": ["The campaign and the first union. In 1796 the twenty-six-year-old General Napoleon Bonaparte took over the Italian wing of the Directory's army and won a series of brilliant victories against the Austrian forces (the site's chronology record, 1796-04-12). TDV's `italya` article sums up the result of the campaign as follows: “Napoleon Bonaparte organised the Italian campaign in 1796, and after centuries the city-states were united in national and political integrity.” The concrete step was taken on 9 July 1797: the conquered lands of northern Italy, centred on Milan, were merged and a Cisalpine Republic on the French model was proclaimed. According to the site's own record, this was “the first (and short-lived) attempt to unite Italian lands under the roof of a single state in the modern sense.”\n\nThe END of VENICE. A thousand-year-old republic paid the price of the same campaign: TDV's `italya` article states that “the French Revolution of 1789 and Napoleon Bonaparte's occupation of Italy … were the end of the Republic of Venice (1798).” The site's own chronology ties this end to the Treaty of Campo Formio of 17 October 1797: while the Austrian Netherlands (Belgium) was ceded to France, the territories of Venice were in return handed over to Austria. The two dates (end of 1797 / 1798) do not contradict each other. The difference is probably that between the fall (Napoleon's entry into Venice, May 1797) and the formal transfer of the territories (the implementation of Campo Formio); this distinction is not clear in TDV's summary sentence, so it is not presented here as established fact.\n\nFROM REPUBLIC to kingdom. TDV also relates how the process continued: “When Napoleon founded the Italian Republic in 1802 he became its first head of state, and in 1804, in the country he turned into a kingdom, he proclaimed himself emperor.” In other words, France's own change of regime (from Republic to Empire) led the satellite state in Italy to be transformed in the same order.\n\nThe root of the RISORGIMENTO. TDV states that these attempts at unification in the Napoleonic period formed the foundation of the movement for national unity later to be known as the “risorgimento” (rising again). According to the site's own record, this first attempt became permanent with the proclamation of the Kingdom of Italy on 1861-03-17 (Turin, Victor Emmanuel II): it was the first time in thirteen centuries that the peninsula (except Venice and Rome) was united under the roof of a single state. There is no direct institutional continuity between Napoleon's Cisalpine Republic of 1797 and the Kingdom of Italy of 1861 (the intervening fifty-four years contain the Restoration, the revolutions of 1848 and the leadership of Piedmont), but both carry the same idea (the union of the peninsula under a single roof).\n\nCONCLUSION. The most tangible legacy of the French Revolution in Italy is that it brought about at the same time the end of one republic (Venice) and the first draft of another union (modern Italy).", "67af6bc2"], "kisa": ["Napoleon's Italian Campaign of 1796-97 gathered the peninsula under a single political roof for the first time and wiped out the thousand-year-old Republic of Venice — like a first rehearsal for the unification (Risorgimento) sixty-four years later.", "5fa583f6"]},
"ihtilal-misir-istah-nedenleri":{"baslik": ["Why did France want Egypt? Three reasons", "b13acf09"], "metin": ["Reason 1 — England's route to India. The site's own chronology entry (source: TDV `misir`) puts this motive clearly in the foreground: “General Bonaparte's army landed at Alexandria and occupied Egypt; aimed at England's route to India, this expedition was the first direct attack by a European power on the central lands of the Ottomans.” For the Directory, which knew it could not defeat England directly in its own islands, cutting through Egypt the Mediterranean-Red Sea-India link, the lifeline of the British East India Company, was considered an indirect but well-aimed blow — the Isthmus of Suez was the shortest overland passage from the Mediterranean to the Red Sea.\n\nREASON 2 — a power vacuum and weak Mamluk rule. The 'Ottoman Period' section of TDV's `misir` article states plainly how loosely Egypt was tied to the centre at the end of the 18th century: the rival Mamluk beys (Murad Bey and Ibrahim Bey) “joined with the Mamluk beys in Said (Upper Egypt) and rebelled again”, and the central government had to tolerate this de facto autonomy — “The Sublime Porte, for its part, had to accept this situation until the French entered Egypt.” In other words, Egypt looked like a land that was on paper an Ottoman province but in fact shared between two rival beys and without a regular central army; it appeared to Napoleon's army an easy target (and so it proved at the Battle of the Pyramids on 21 July 1798; see the `ihtilal-misir-seferi-ve-kavalali` card).\n\nREASON 3 — Egypt's own wealth. The Nile valley was an exceptional agricultural basin, regarded since antiquity as the granary of the Mediterranean world, whose irrigation was provided by the river's own flood; Cairo and Alexandria had for centuries been the crossroads of Mediterranean-Red Sea trade. This general attraction is not stressed in a separate sentence in the sections of TDV that were read (standard academic/geographical knowledge, §4 granularity gap) — but France's expedition was not merely military; it was also a colonial calculation: the scientific mission that Napoleon took with him (the Institut d'Égypte, see the site's chronology record, 1798-07-01) was also charged with examining the country's agriculture, irrigation and resources — the beginning of modern Egyptology and at the same time a colonial feasibility study.\n\nFOURTH (political) reason — keeping him away from Paris. To these three reasons concerning the theatre itself must be added one of domestic politics: the Directory wanted to keep the young General Napoleon, who had become excessively popular through the Italian campaign, away from the capital (see the `ihtilal-misir-seferi-ve-kavalali` card). Thus the expedition was born at the point where a strategic, an economic and a local political need intersected.\n\nWhere the CALCULATION failed. France's plan collapsed at Aboukir (1 August 1798): even the only direct sentence given by TDV's `misir` article bears the trace of this — “The French occupation of July 1798 opened a new period in Egypt, and the British intervened in Egyptian affairs on the grounds of protecting their interests in the East.” Admiral Nelson's almost complete destruction of the French fleet in Aboukir Bay (the site's state record, 1798-08-01; the site's chronology record) cut Napoleon's army in Egypt off by sea — the plan to make Egypt a base against England ended, on the contrary, with the French army itself being trapped.\n\nCONCLUSION. Egypt's attraction did not come from a single reason: strategic position (the route to India), a de facto power vacuum (weak Mamluk rule), economic and scientific curiosity (the wealth of the Nile, the Institut d'Égypte) and the internal politics of Paris all at once made the expedition seem logical — but none of them had taken British naval superiority into account.", "2a3418e0"], "kisa": ["Egypt attracted France for three separate reasons: a dagger thrust into England's route to India, a land rich in its own right, and a power vacuum that no one in fact fully controlled — all three came together in the justification for a single expedition.\n", "f315f8d5"]},
"ihtilal-misir-seferi-ve-kavalali":{"baslik": ["Who filled the vacuum Napoleon left in Egypt?", "f8e8bf48"], "metin": ["Why Egypt. The Directory's purpose in sending troops to Egypt was not Egypt itself: it was to cut Britain's land and sea link to India and to keep the young General Napoleon, who had become too popular with the Italian campaign, away from the capital (see the site's chronology entry, 1798-07-01). The expedition also turned the relationship with the Ottomans that had lasted since 1536 into direct war for the first time (see the Ottoman card).\n\nOCCUPATION and collapse. On 21 July 1798 Napoleon's army won the Battle of the Pyramids and seized Cairo; the atlas's `misir-eyaleti` identity card records this day as \"Egypt came de facto under French occupation\" (a standard academic and encyclopaedic source; TDV does not give this detail — the Battle of the Pyramids is a very well documented event). The occupation lasted three years; with the surrender of the French forces at Alexandria on 2 September 1801 (the Capitulation of Alexandria), Egypt returned to joint Ottoman-British control.\n\nVACUUM and rise. According to TDV's `kavalali-mehmed-ali-pasa` article, \"the date on which Mehmed Ali, who was among the 300 soldiers sent out from Kavala as part of the forces being prepared against the French occupying Egypt, arrived on Egyptian soil is 8 March 1801.\" In the same year, for the success he showed against the French forces, he was promoted to the rank of major (binbaşı) by the governor of Egypt at the time, Koca Hüsrev Pasha. After the French withdrew, neither the returning Mamluk beys nor the Ottoman governors who followed one another in quick succession could fill the vacuum of authority that remained; at the end of a power struggle lasting four years, in TDV's words, he was \"appointed governor by the Sublime Porte (3 July 1805)\" — this was the decision of a central government compelled, under pressure from the ulema of Cairo, to recognise the de facto situation.\n\nRESULT. Napoleon's expedition did not achieve its own goal (cutting the route to India) and was wound up in three years; but the military and administrative chaos it created was the first link in the chain that would take Egypt, for the first time since 1517, outside direct Ottoman provincial administration — the dynasty of Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala, which began in 1805, was consolidated by the hereditary privilege of 1841 and lasted until 1914. The most lasting indirect result of the French Revolution was a semi-independent dynasty founded in Egypt by one of the very men who fought to stop it (Mehmed Ali).", "e69a5225"], "kisa": ["Napoleon held Egypt for three years and then left empty-handed — but the one who filled the vacuum of authority opened by the occupation was neither the returning Mamluks nor the Ottoman governor: it was an Albanian officer born in Kavala.", "a8a01018"]},
"ihtilal-osmanli-genel-baglam":{"baslik": ["The Revolution in the Ottoman Empire: France declared an enemy, a borrowed concept", "76681831"], "metin": ["Conceptual inspiration. Even the name of the Ottoman reform movement owes something to the period: TDV's `nizam-i-cedid` article writes explicitly that the concept was \"perceived at that time in Europe, especially on the basis of developments in revolutionary France …, as a restructuring against the old order\". The same article explains that the material conditions changed by the Enlightenment philosophy of the eighteenth century and by the industrial revolution that had begun fed the revolution that erupted from below in France — the Nizam-i Cedid borrowed this language of \"restructuring against the old order\". But in the same place TDV adds a counterbalancing note: the Ottoman reforms were in essence influenced more by the examples of administrative and military reform in the Austria of Joseph II's reign and in Poland than by the French Revolution. In other words, the Revolution provided a vocabulary, not a direct model.\n\nAn enemy while still a FRIEND. According to TDV's `selim-iii` article, from 1792 onwards \"the struggle waged in Europe against revolutionary France spread to the Ottoman world as well\"; in these years Selim III himself employed French military experts and technical personnel and founded the Nizam-i Cedid army — that is, revolutionary France was not yet an enemy but a technical source of reform. This alliance broke down suddenly in July 1798: the same article records that \"the attack on Egypt (July 1798) as an extension of the Anglo-French struggle, and its capture within a short time, brought the Ottoman state, as a member of an alliance that also included Russia, face to face with France\". The Ottomans formally joined this alliance in January 1799 — this is the moment when 250 years of French friendship turned into actual war for the first time (see the Egypt card).\n\nA SHORT peace. TDV `selim-iii`: \"France was at last forced, in 1802, to make peace and to leave Egypt.\" The peace did not last — Napoleon's march to empire and the series of wars in Europe forced Ottoman diplomacy in the following decade to be swept along once more within the France-Russia-Britain triangle.\n\nSUMMARY. The French Revolution was not just one thing for the Ottomans: it was at once a source of ideas that entered the vocabulary of reform, a source of technical personnel for the Nizam-i Cedid and (with Egypt) a direct enemy. These three became intertwined within the same decade, in the reign of the same ruler (Selim III).", "659b0041"], "kisa": ["Since 1536 France had been the Ottomans' oldest Christian friend; in 1798 it suddenly became an enemy — but the ideas of the Revolution had already entered Selim III's reform vocabulary before it was declared an enemy.", "6c00fdb9"]},
"ihtilal-prusya-etkisi":{"baslik": ["What did the rout at Jena bring Prussia?", "d0618417"], "metin": ["Early war and withdrawal. In 1792 Prussia joined the First Coalition at Austria's side and went to war against revolutionary France. Unlike Austria, however, it did not see the struggle through to the end: with the Peace of Basel in 1795 it made a separate peace with France, left the war and remained neutral for more than ten years. During this period, while Austria and Russia were paying a heavy price against Napoleon (see the Austria and Russia cards), Prussia stood aside and preserved its strength (standard academic source; TDV does not give this detail).\n\nJENA-AUERSTEDT (14 October 1806). Neutrality did not last: on a single day, in two separate battles (Jena and Auerstedt), Napoleon's army inflicted a crushing defeat on the Prussian army. TDV's `prusya` article also touches on this period: “Throughout the Napoleonic Wars (1792-1814) it, like the whole of Europe, suffered French attack and occupation.” The site's own record (drawing on Christopher Clark's Iron Kingdom) clarifies the meaning of the rout: “It revealed that the legendary army of Frederick the Great was an outdated war machine.”\n\nTILSIT — losing half. Nine days later, in TDV's words, “after the victories of Jena and Auerstedt … it was forced to accept a harsh peace (9 July 1807)”; “it lost half of the lands it held, above all those it had seized from Poland.” The Kingdom of Westphalia was created out of all its lands west of the Elbe, and the Duchy of Warsaw out of the seized Polish lands; Prussia was all but erased as a great power, and its army was limited to 42,000 men.\n\nWinning with the MIND what was lost by the sword. TDV also records the wave of reform that the defeat produced: “In the time of von Stein, who was made head of government in the autumn of 1807, … efforts were made to restructure the state.” The edict that Stein prepared on behalf of the government, which had taken refuge in Königsberg under French occupation, abolished hereditary serfdom; the University of Berlin, founded by Wilhelm von Humboldt in 1810 as a new model combining research with teaching, was the civilian arm of this wave of reform.\n\nCONCLUSION. For Prussia, the wars of the French Revolution were first a threat that kept it on the sidelines for ten years, then a disaster that almost destroyed it in a single day, and finally a catalyst that forced it to reinvent itself. Having recovered most of its territory at the Congress of Vienna in 1815, Prussia would fifty years later (1871) be the founding power of a unified Germany.", "556a1018"], "kisa": ["The legendary army of Frederick the Great was crushed twice in a single day at Jena; Prussia lost half its territory, but the reforms born of the defeat prepared it to be the core of the unified Germany of a century later.", "735577b0"]},
"ihtilal-rusya-etkisi":{"baslik": ["How did Russia react to the Revolution: from Catherine to 1812", "40b1eff1"], "metin": ["Catherine's reaction. According to TDV's `rusya` article, \"Russia, with its conservative structure, continued to stand against the French Revolution of 1789 in particular and its ideas\" — Catherine II kept her distance from the Revolution as a precedent that threatened her own absolutist regime.\n\nThe TEMPORARY Ottoman alliance (1798-1806). Napoleon's attack on Egypt (see the Ottoman and Egypt cards) reversed for a short while the centuries-old Ottoman-Russian enmity: the two states formed an alliance against a common enemy (France). TDV `rusya` also explains how this alliance broke down: \"When the allied Russians' incitement of the Greeks living on the islands of the Aegean and Venetian seas and in the Morea to revolt against the Ottomans put an end to this forced friendship, war broke out again between the two states (1806).\" That is, the alliance formed against France fell apart because of the incitement of the Greeks within it, and the parties returned to their old enmity — here too the Greek movement's link with Russia (see the previous card) is visible.\n\nDefeats against NAPOLEON. In the coalition wars Russia (TDV: it \"was the main factor … in liberating Europe from French domination in the wars waged against Napoleon (1798-1814)\") first paid a heavy price: on 20 November 1805 it was decisively defeated, together with Austria, at Austerlitz (the \"Battle of the Three Emperors\") (the site's chronology entry, Riasanovsky & Steinberg). Two years later, on 7 July 1807, it was obliged to sign a treaty of peace and alliance with Napoleon at Tilsit and to agree to join the Continental System — the peace was temporary.\n\n1812 and peace with the Ottomans. Five years later, on 24 June 1812, Napoleon invaded Russia (the \"Patriotic War\"); TDV's `rusya` article also records an indirect result of this campaign: it states that \"Napoleon's Moscow campaign (1812) facilitated the Russo-Ottoman peace\" — Russia's need to close its southern (Ottoman) front and direct all its strength westward (against Napoleon) ensured that the Peace of Bucharest of the same year was concluded quickly \"on not very harsh terms\". Russia emerged victorious from this campaign, and this victory, the exact opposite of Austerlitz in 1805, took its place in Russian collective memory.\n\nRESULT. The French Revolution and the wars that followed it made Russia for a short time an ally of the Ottomans, then an enemy again, and at the same time the greatest power in Europe — Russia entered the Congress of Vienna of 1815 both as a key member of the coalition that defeated Napoleon and with the strongest land army on the continent.", "a0b75264"], "kisa": ["Catherine II saw the Revolution as a threat; her grandson Alexander I first fought against France on the same side as the Ottomans, then was twice defeated by Napoleon, and finally was on the winning side in 1812.", "68efbb3f"]},
"ihtilal-sirp-yunan-milliyetcilik":{"baslik": ["Why are the links of the Serbian revolt and the Greek movement to the Revolution not equal?", "ade9ab66"], "metin": ["The Greek movement — a direct link. TDV's `yunanistan` article does not leave this connection indirect but establishes it explicitly: \"The Greek independence movement is linked to the process that developed with the French Revolution.\" The first concrete name of the movement is Rigas Velestinlis — the son of a Greek merchant from Thessaly, who wrote an independence manifesto and poems in the 1790s and was executed in Belgrade in 1798 (that is, the first martyr of the Greek movement lost his life in the very city where the Serbian revolt would erupt six years later — a geographical coincidence, not a direct organisational link between the two movements). The organisation matured in 1814: \"A group including Greek merchants and politicians founded a secret organisation under the name Filiki Eteria (Society of Friends) in Odessa, Russia's important port city on the Black Sea.\" Seven years later, \"in 1821 the Greek revolt began in Wallachia and Moldavia, which were governed by Phanariot beys, and then in the Morea\" (the page's own record: 1821-03-25). TDV adds that the current of Philhellenism (love of the Greeks) that developed in Europe also fed this process.\n\nThe SERBIAN movement — NO such link IN TDV. The `sirbistan` article, searched by the same method, places the cause of the revolt of 1804 on different ground: \"The ever-increasing oppression of the Janissaries known as the dayıs and yamaks brought with it the Serbian revolts, and finally in 1804 the Serbian revolt broke out under the leadership of Karađorđe.\" In this sentence there is not a single word about the French Revolution, Enlightenment ideas or an ideology of national independence — in TDV's own account the trigger was local and administrative (Janissary tyranny in the sanjak of Belgrade, the massacre of the knezes in January 1804), born not in Paris but in Belgrade.\n\nA NOT FOUND stamp. This card does not invent a link between the Serbian revolt and the French Revolution. It may be said that the idea of national self-determination of the Age of Enlightenment fed the general European atmosphere of the period, but this general claim is not in TDV's `sirbistan` article, nor is it separately confirmed by an academic source; for this reason it is not given here as established knowledge.\n\nRESULT. In the end both movements arrived at the same goal (breaking away from the Ottomans and founding a national state), but their origins must be read differently: the Greek movement, in the words of its own source, was born directly of the ideological legacy of the Revolution; the Serbian movement of a reaction to local administrative tyranny. That both erupted within the same decades (1804-1821) does not mean that they arose from the same cause.", "42ffc221"], "kisa": ["The links to the French Revolution of two Balkan movements that erupted within the same span of years are not equal: TDV explicitly links the Greek movement to the Revolution, but the Serbian revolt only to local Janissary tyranny.", "d120ade8"]},
"ihtilal-venedik-paylasimi":{"baslik": ["Why was Venice split in two at Campo Formio?", "f232857e"], "metin": ["Why it was partitioned — the logic of the bargain. In the Campo Formio negotiations what France really wanted was the left bank of the Rhine and the Austrian Netherlands (Belgium); Austria asked for compensation for this loss. Venice was at that very moment a piece of territory that had in effect been occupied by Napoleon's army, was defenceless and had no one representing it at the negotiating table. It became a means of payment that two great powers used to settle their own main disputes. The republic itself was never a party to this bargain; its fate was decided without its consent, at a table far from its own capital.\n\nPRETEXT and dissolution. The immediate trigger was the Lido incident of 20 April 1797: the Venetian batteries at the mouth of the Lido opened fire on a French warship entering forbidden waters and killed its captain; Napoleon treated this as a cause for war. Three weeks later, on 12 May 1797, the Great Council met for the last time and decided to dissolve the republic; Ludovico Manin, the 120th and last doge (elected on 9 March 1789), gave up his office. According to the site's own record (Treccani, 'Manìn, Ludovico'), “the thousand-year republic came to an end without a battle being fought” — in Venetian memory this is regarded as the gravest moment of shame.\n\nHOW it was divided. Five months later, on 17 October 1797, the partition was formalised by the Treaty of Campo Formio, and it went in two separate directions: (1) the mainland territories (the terraferma — Venice's mainland lands in the Veneto, Istria, Dalmatia) were left to Austria; the site's own record confirms this with “Con il trattato di Campoformio (1797) Venezia fu ceduta da Napoleone all'Austria” (Treccani) — the city later became part of Austria's province of Lombardy-Venetia. (2) The Ionian Islands (the Seven Islands: Corfu, Cephalonia, Santa Maura (Lefkada), Zante, Ithaca, Paxos, Cerigo) — Venice's most valuable naval bases outside the Adriatic — went not to Austria but to France. TDV's `yedi-ada-cumhuriyeti` article states this clearly: “Article 5 of the Treaty of Campo Formio provided for the Ionian Islands belonging to Venice to be ceded to France.”\n\nWHY France wanted the islands. This separation was no accident: while giving Austria land (mainland Venice), France kept for itself a gateway to the eastern Mediterranean. According to TDV's `yedi-ada-cumhuriyeti` article, “the French turned Corfu into a base for the military operations they would later carry out in the Mediterranean” — this base was the first springboard in the eastern Mediterranean for the Egyptian Campaign that would begin nine months later (see the `ihtilal-misir-istah-nedenleri` card).\n\nAND France lost its own share too. Napoleon's attack on Egypt drove Russia and the Ottomans into a temporary alliance (see the `ihtilal-rusya-etkisi` card); this allied fleet took the islands back from the French — TDV `yedi-ada-cumhuriyeti`: Zante was captured in 1799, and the fortress of Corfu “on 5 March 1799”. A year later, under a founding convention of twelve articles signed on 21 March 1800, the seven islands were turned into an autonomous republic under joint Ottoman-Russian protection (the “Cezâyir-i Seb'a-i Müctemia Cumhuru”, the Septinsular Republic). The share that France had set aside for itself at Campo Formio slipped from its hands before even three years had passed.\n\nCONSEQUENCES. For Venice the result was final and permanent: an independent oligarchic republic more than 1,000 years old passed into history without even using its own army, and never again returned as a separate state. The Congress of Vienna of 1815 left it with Austria once more, and in 1866 (as a result of the Austro-Prussian War, despite Italy having been defeated on the battlefield) it joined the Kingdom of Italy. In Italian historiography the overthrow of Venice is regarded as “the symbol of the loss of independence” (Treccani). The more general consequence is this: the wars of the French Revolution did not merely defeat a great republic, they reduced it at a negotiating table to a property that could be divided into pieces. This was a pattern that the same wars would repeat in Poland (see the relevant further-reading cards) and many times afterwards.", "755aa8a4"], "kisa": ["The thousand-year republic was dissolved without a single shot being fired, then split in two like a bargaining chip: France left the mainland territories to its enemy (Austria), kept the most valuable piece (the Ionian Islands) for itself — and a year later lost that piece to the Ottoman-Russian fleet.\n", "8441c2d5"]},
"ikinci-balkan-savasi-1913":{"ad": ["The Second Balkan War: when the victors could not divide the spoils", "58655d32"], "metin": ["The cause in one word: Macedonia. The Treaty of London had left a large part of Macedonia to Bulgaria; Serbia and Greece objected to this. Serbia found the northern and central Macedonia allotted to it insufficient, and Greece wanted to expand southwards. Romania, which had taken no part at all in the First Balkan War, was uneasy at Bulgaria's growth and had not been satisfied by being given Silistra either. On 1 June 1913 Serbia and Greece formed an alliance against Bulgaria.\n\nWHEN the war began — the sources diverge. In Western literature the war began on the night of 29-30 June 1913: that night King Ferdinand of Bulgaria ordered his army to attack the Serbian and Greek forces in Macedonia. By contrast, in Turkish encyclopaedic literature the date on which Bulgaria began war against Serbia, Montenegro and Greece is given as 23 June 1913. The two dates answer the same question — 'on what day did the war begin' — and differ by six days; no separate event corresponding to 23 June (an order, a mobilisation or a declaration of war) could be identified in the sources surveyed. This card takes the night of 29-30 June as its basis and records the divergence openly.\n\nTHE WARRING parties. Thanks to the advantage of surprise the Bulgarian offensive was at first successful, but the Greek and Serbian defence withdrew without losing its order. On 2 July the Serbian army went over to the counter-offensive and drove a wedge into the Bulgarian line; the Greek reserves were committed to the front on 3 July. On 10 July the Bulgarian offensive came to a complete halt. On 11 July the Romanian army crossed the Bulgarian frontier and marched towards Sofia without meeting any resistance. Thus a second war had begun among the allies because they could not share out the Ottoman inheritance, and Bulgaria found itself at war with four states at once: its northern frontier lay open to Romania, its western frontier to Serbia and its southern frontier to Greece.\n\nTHE OTTOMANS saw their opportunity. While the allies were advancing towards Sofia, the Committee of Union and Progress government took action. Complaining of the attitude of Bulgaria, which was unwilling to have the Midye-Enez line agreed in the Treaty of London delimited, it delivered a note to the great powers on 19 July 1913 and stated that the Maritsa frontier would not be violated. Edirne, which Bulgaria, at war with four states at once, had left without forces, was recovered on 21 July 1913 without any resistance being encountered. Western literature records the same move as 'the Turks entering Thrace in violation of the armistice they had made with Bulgaria' and places the reoccupation of Edirne on 22 July — this difference of one day may arise from the entry into the city and the taking over of it falling on different days; it could not be resolved.\n\nOUTCOME. The war ended with the Treaty of Bucharest, signed on 10 August 1913 between Bulgaria on the one side and Serbia, Greece and Montenegro on the other. By this treaty Western Thrace and the Kavala basin passed from Bulgaria to Greece. The Ottoman-Bulgarian treaty was signed separately: in Istanbul on 29 September 1913, with a text of twenty articles. Edirne and a semicircular piece of land to its west, with a diameter of 30 kilometres, remained Ottoman, Western Thrace was returned to Bulgaria, and the river Maritsa was accepted as the frontier.\n\nAN INTERIM state. While Western Thrace was being returned to Bulgaria, a short-lived organisation was set up in the region: on 31 August 1913 the Provisional Government of Western Thrace (Garbî Trakya Hükûmet-i Muvakkatesi), centred on Komotini (Gümülcine), was proclaimed. It disappeared when the Ottomans withdrew as the treaty required.\n\nHOW the balance changed. Bulgaria, the greatest winner of the first war, became the greatest loser of the second; Greece added Kavala to Salonica; Serbia enlarged its share of Macedonia; Romania took Southern Dobruja. The fact that Bulgaria, seeking to make good its loss, sided with the Central Powers in the First World War a year later is the continuation of this reckoning: on 6 September 1915 a frontier convention was signed with the Ottomans in Sofia.\n\nWHY it matters. The second war did not correct the outcome of the first; it merely rewrote who would take how much. For the Ottomans the gain was limited to Edirne, and this gain was the product less of a military success than of the enemy's being occupied elsewhere — the source records this with the words 'without any resistance being encountered'.", "4b4292bc"], "kisa": ["The four states that won the first war fought the second against one another. In a war lasting thirty-three days Bulgaria had to face four fronts at once and lost the very place where it had gained the most.", "f5baa75c"], "not": ["The most widespread account of this war on the Ottoman side is 'the liberation of Edirne'. The recovery of Edirne is real; but two months earlier the same Edirne had been ceded to Bulgaria by treaty by the government that had come to power claiming it would save the city. Both sentences are true, and neither is understood unless they are read together. The divergence between 23 and 29 June over the day the war began could not be resolved: two sources give different days in answer to the same question, and no separate event corresponding to 23 June was found.", "a50df638"], "bag": ["For the first phase of the war and the Treaty of London see the card on the results of the Balkan wars; for the coup staged with the claim of saving Edirne, the card on the Raid on the Sublime Porte.", "45ed48e3"]},
"iktisat-aydinlanma-osmanliya-etkisi":{"baslik": ["Where did the Enlightenment enter the Ottoman Empire? Reason, the mother tongue and Balkan nationalism", "ab2c78f5"], "metin": ["■ What is the Enlightenment?\nAccording to Kemal Beydilli's article on Europe in TDV, the Enlightenment was an intellectual movement that became distinct at the end of the 17th century and lasted throughout the 18th century. In place of tradition, superstition, religion and state authority it wanted to set reason, the courage to criticise, freedom of conscience and religious tolerance. It was carried mostly by the bourgeoisie. Rulers such as Frederick the Great and Joseph II also embraced it.\n\n■ What did it change?\nAccording to Beydilli, its influence was seen most in the state and the law. The idea that every human being is born free and that the state was founded by a social contract became the principles of the American constitutional movement of 1776 and the French one of 1789. The separation of powers was developed. Torture and witch trials were banned. It was argued that no punishment not written in the law could be imposed (Beccaria). Church property was seized in some places, and in 1773 the Jesuits were suppressed.\n\n■ The road to the Ottomans\nBeydilli distinguishes this movement from the Renaissance. The Renaissance bore a Catholic stamp, whereas the Enlightenment was secular. For this reason it was able to enter the Orthodox-Byzantine cultural sphere as well, and it was also influential among Jews. Holding church services in the language of the people instead of Latin was considered a requirement of reason. Teaching and worship in the mother tongue awakened national feelings especially among the peoples of Eastern and South-Eastern Europe. Beydilli's conclusion is clear: a new age was beginning that threatened the multinational empires, namely Austria and the Ottoman Empire.\n\n■ Reading it on the map\nAccording to Beydilli, in post-Congress of Vienna Europe the ideas of the Enlightenment and the French Revolution lived on as liberal and nationalist currents. The piece-by-piece retreat of the Ottoman colour from the Balkans on the map throughout the 19th century is the image of the century in which these ideas turned into politics.", "95d2a0c6"]},
"iktisat-devletin-gelirleri-butce":{"baslik": ["Where did the state's money come from? The treasury, the timar and the waqf", "ead68876"], "metin": ["■ Three purses\nAccording to Erol Özvar's article in TDV, Ottoman public revenues were gathered into three purses. The first was the central treasury in Istanbul. The second was the timars: these revenues never entered the treasury at all but were left directly to the sipahi (cavalryman) in return for bringing soldiers to war. The third was the waqfs (pious foundations): semi-autonomous, but under state supervision. According to the budget of 1527-1528 published by Ömer Lutfi Barkan, 51 per cent of revenue went to the treasury, 37 per cent to dirliks (revenue grants) and 12 per cent to foundations and private property.\n\n■ The three sources of the treasury\nThe revenue of the central treasury consisted essentially of three items. The jizya was the sharia tax levied on non-Muslim men able to work, and almost all of it went into the treasury. The avarız was a tax that the state imposed on the people according to need; after the 17th century it became regular. The mukataa comprised the taxes levied on every kind of economic activity, from customs to mines. The state left to the treasury the large mukataas that could easily be turned into cash, and to timars and waqfs those that were hard to convert into cash.\n\n■ Where did the money go?\nThe largest expense was the pay of soldiers and officials. According to Özvar, in the 16th-18th centuries these salaries took up 45-70 per cent of the budget, and the share rose whenever war broke out. Next came the provisioning of the palace and the army, the pilgrimage, and the surre (annual gift) sent to Mecca and Medina.\n\n■ How was the deficit closed?\nAfter the 17th century expenditure grew faster than revenue; the period after the Cretan wars and the defeat at Vienna was especially difficult. The deficit was closed by farming out tax revenues for cash in advance (iltizam), by borrowing from statesmen and merchants, by delaying salaries and by new extraordinary taxes. In the first half of the 18th century the finances eased. But the Russian wars wrecked them again: first the state turned to domestic borrowing through esham (revenue shares) and kaime (paper money), and in the mid-19th century to foreign loans.\n\n■ The difference from Europe\nAccording to Beydilli, in Europe the central power of kings could be established only once the feudal lords had weakened. New bureaucracies, regular finances and standing armies appeared in France after the Hundred Years' War. In the Ottoman Empire, by contrast, a large part of the revenue from land was from the very beginning recorded in the state's tahrir (survey) registers. The sipahi received this revenue not as his own property but in return for service.", "51553b6a"]},
"iktisat-esnaf-burjuvazi-neden-yok":{"baslik": ["Why did no bourgeoisie arise in the Ottoman Empire? 10 per cent profit, 20 per cent interest and the gedik", "3f9f4a52"], "metin": ["■ The ceiling on profit\nAccording to Mehmet Genç's article in TDV, so that goods would be cheap, the state deliberately kept low the profit it allowed to artisans and merchants. From the 16th century to the mid-19th century, legitimate profit was generally between 5 and 15 per cent. A kanunname dated 1680 sets the legitimate profit in the narh (official price fixing) at 10 per cent; in very laborious trades this rate could rise to 20 per cent at most. Even where no narh was imposed, the system pulled profit towards this average. Each stage of production was a separate guild, and if one raised its profit, another's profit fell.\n\n■ The price of interest\nCredit was needed to enlarge capital. But interest was free only in a few fields: moneylenders (sarraf), orphans' property and cash waqfs. According to Genç, interest in these fields was 12-25 per cent, averaging around 20 per cent. Someone working at a 10 per cent profit could not borrow at 20 per cent interest. And the place where liquid capital chiefly went was tax farming, that is, iltizam. Probate inventories confirm this picture. In 17th-18th century Bursa, Damascus and Cairo, the average wealth of an artisan was between 400 and 1000 kuruş, and that of a merchant between 1500 and 4000.\n\n■ The gedik: monopoly and equality\nIn time the guilds closed themselves to outsiders. From the 17th century the monopoly right called 'gedik' spread: a given trade could be practised only by a member of that guild, and the member could not practise any other. The number of masters, journeymen and apprentices was also limited. The artisans themselves defended equality. In 1813 the ribbon makers complained about masters who had raised their number of looms to five and asked for the surplus looms to be given to poor masters; their request was granted. Genç sums this up as follows: the Ottoman administration chose not the path of accumulation and development but that of distribution and the spreading of prosperity.\n\n■ But skill was superior\nThis order also preserved craftsmanship. As Genç reports, the French consul Beaujour in 1797 was filled with admiration for the copper and iron work. And in the 1750s Flachat of Lyon took dyers and morocco-leather craftsmen from Istanbul.\n\n■ The European side\nAccording to Beydilli, from the 14th century onwards the middle class in Europe grew stronger through trade. It was also chiefly this bourgeoisie that carried Enlightenment thought. The Ottoman Empire had an urban producing class, but these rules had prevented it from turning into a capital-accumulating bourgeoisie as in Europe.", "046d327e"], "kisa": ["The legitimate profit under the narh was around 10 per cent. The cost of obtaining credit, however, was interest of around 20 per cent. On this reckoning it was almost impossible for artisans and merchants to accumulate capital.", "5dbc0d93"]},
"iktisat-kilise-ilmiye-karsilastirma":{"baslik": ["Church and ilmiye: could a cleric excommunicate the king?", "f709b18d"], "metin": ["■ Europe: two swords\nIn TDV's article on Europe, Kemal Beydilli relates that from the 4th century the bishop of Rome claimed to be superior to the other bishoprics. In time the popes maintained that secular rule not based on the Church and divine law was worthless. The quarrel over who would appoint bishops grew. Pope Gregory VII excommunicated the German King Henry IV (1075-1077). According to Kürşat Demirci's section, from the 14th century onwards the Church also grew rich. The papacy tried to gather church revenues into a single hand and left tax collection to banks. According to Demirci, the intertwining of religion and money matters was the basic source of the corruption that led as far as the Reformation.\n\n■ The Ottomans: a class within the state\nAccording to Mehmet İpşirli's article on the ilmiye in TDV, the ilmiye was the organisation of education, justice and fatwa formed by the ulema trained in the madrasas. It was part of the ruling askerî class. From the 17th century onwards appointments were made on the proposal of the Shaykh al-Islam, the submission of the grand vizier and the imperial rescript (hatt-ı hümayun) of the sultan. In other words, the ulema were not a church standing against the throne with a separate claim to sovereignty.\n\n■ But it was privileged\nAccording to İpşirli, the ilmiye had the widest privileges within the askerî class. Its tax exemption was broader than that of the others. While any punishment, including execution, could be inflicted on military officials, the heaviest penalty for the ulema was dismissal or exile; execution was very rare. Murad II granted the descendants of Molla Fenari the privilege of starting at a high rank as müderris (madrasa professors). In time this privilege spread to all ulema families. Some twenty families easily obtained the highest offices. İpşirli writes that this 'mevalizade' privilege (that of the sons of high-ranking ulema) set the profession back and corrupted it.\n\n■ The Enlightenment's blow to the Church\nAccording to Beydilli, the Enlightenment shook the authority of the Church in Europe. In 1773 the Jesuit order was suppressed everywhere except Russia and Prussia. In some places the extensive properties of churches were seized and the number of monasteries was reduced. The problem of the Ottoman ilmiye was different. As İpşirli reports, at the end of the 16th century Âlî Mustafa Efendi and Selânikî complained that privileges, favouritism and judgeships obtained by bribery were corrupting the profession.", "e9bb199b"], "kisa": ["In Europe the pope could excommunicate the emperor, and the Church collected its own tax. In the Ottoman Empire, the ilmiye was a state class appointed by the sultan. It was privileged, but it was not a separate centre of power.", "80343113"]},
"iktisat-koylu-serf-miydi":{"baslik": ["Was the Ottoman peasant a serf? Miri land and Europe's peasant bound to the soil", "7a05fdd4"], "metin": ["■ What is miri land?\nAccording to Mehmet Genç's article in TDV, most of the agricultural land in Anatolia and Rumelia was 'miri'. Ownership belonged to the state, while the peasant family received the right to work the land in return for a title deed (tapu). Depending on the fertility of the soil, a family farm measured between 60 and 150 dönüms. The peasant could not sell the land, endow it as a waqf or give it away without permission. If he left it idle for three years without a valid excuse, the land was taken from him and given to someone else. If he abandoned his land and left, he could be brought back within ten years, or else he paid a heavy tax called the 'çiftbozan resmi' (farm-breaker's fee).\n\n■ But isn't this serfdom?\nGenç writes that it is not possible to look superficially at these rules and liken the Ottoman peasant to a slave bound to the soil, that is, to a serf. He adds that this was clearly shown in the detailed studies of Ömer Lutfi Barkan. There was no rule compelling a peasant who did not wish to work the land. The only sanction was that the land would be given to another peasant willing to work it. And the departing peasant could free himself of his obligation by paying a kind of compensation. This compensation was the çiftbozan resmi, which could amount to as much as seven to ten times the normal farm tax.\n\n■ Through the peasant's eyes\nWhen a peasant died, the land passed to his son without being divided and without inheritance tax. According to Genç, although the land legally belonged to the state, the peasant psychologically regarded it as his own property. He farmed it with his family as he wished, paid the moderate tax set by law and freely consumed the rest. Genç believes that this two-sided security played no small part in the rapid spread of the Ottomans in the Balkans and their remaining there for centuries.\n\n■ The çiftliks and the end\nFrom the 17th century onwards large çiftliks (estates) came into being. But according to Genç they were mostly established on empty land, the peasants resisted, and the state did not regard these estates as legitimate. The estate owners were unable to bring about a capitalist transformation of agriculture like that in Europe. The distribution of land remained largely egalitarian until the end of the Ottoman Empire. According to Genç, after the Tanzimat the state gave up its old controls over the economy within twenty-five to thirty years; control over land, however, remained outside this.\n\n■ The European side\nTDV's article on Europe writes that the order in force since the Middle Ages, based on social stratification (état), was shaken by the French Revolution only at the end of the 18th century. For the difference between the sipahi and the feudal lord, see also the card 'The timar and European feudalism'.", "f50cb40a"], "kisa": ["The land belonged to the state; the peasant could not sell it or abandon it. This seems to resemble serfdom in Europe. But the TDV article, based on Barkan's studies, says that the comparison is superficial.", "51838e41"]},
"iktisat-sanayi-devrimi-yatirim-cozulmesi":{"baslik": ["What was happening in the Ottoman Empire while Europe had its industrial revolution? Seventy years of 'disinvestment'", "988f97c2"], "metin": ["■ Europe: machine, factory, worker\nAccording to Kemal Beydilli's article on Europe in TDV, the industrial revolution began first in England after 1775. It then passed to Belgium, Germany and France. Mechanical looms, steam pumps in mines and improved blast furnaces changed production radically. The price too was heavy: 14-16 hour working days, women and children employed at low wages, unhealthy workers' quarters. The workers blamed their desperation on the machines, and machine-breaking began. In 1812 England introduced the death penalty for it. According to Beydilli, the industrial revolution was the chief cause of the birth of socialism in the 19th century.\n\n■ The Ottomans: the melting away of capital\nAccording to Mehmet Genç, the opposite was happening in the Ottoman Empire in the same years. From the 1770s, under the heavy burden of the Russian wars, the state began to seize the larger estates of deceased artisans and merchants. Since it had no legal right to do so, it did this as a compulsory loan to be repaid after the war, and gave esham (revenue shares) in return. Genç openly calls this 'disinvestment'. The capital in a business was turned into cash and spent on war. The miri mübayaa, the state's right to buy goods cheaply, also grew more burdensome in wartime. Those who suffered most were the producers trying to grow.\n\n■ The paradox\nAccording to Genç, this process lasted about seventy years, from the last quarter of the 18th century to the Tanzimat. This was precisely the period when the industrial revolution was taking root in Europe and capital accumulation should have been accelerating. Genç counts this coincidence among the paradoxical ironies of Ottoman history.\n\n■ The state becomes a factory owner\nThen the state itself entered production. For the new army it produced in its own factories, besides arms and ammunition, goods such as sailcloth, woollen cloth, leather, fezzes and paper. After 1826 it took into its monopoly, under the name 'yed-i vâhid' (single hand), the trade in many goods such as opium, wool, silk and olive oil. The Treaty of Balta Limanı of 1838 abolished these monopolies. According to Genç, despite the efforts at development after the mid-19th century, the transition to modern growth was delayed by a century. One of the main causes of the delay was these seventy years of disinvestment.", "8ea752f3"]},
"iktisat-uc-ilke-provizyonizm":{"baslik": ["What did the Ottoman economy rest on? Three principles: provisioning, the treasury, tradition", "f3759c46"], "metin": ["■ First principle: need comes first (provisionism)\nAccording to Mehmet Genç, writing in TDV, the economic outlook of the Ottoman administrator in the classical period rested on the concept of 'need'. The task of the economy was to ensure that goods within the country were plentiful, of good quality and cheap. The basic unit was the kaza (judicial district): a town and the dozens of villages attached to it, headed by a kadı (judge). Goods produced in a kaza went first to meet the needs of the kaza, then to the army and the palace, then to crowded Istanbul, then to other regions. Only if something was left over from all this was export permitted.\n\n■ Second principle: the treasury (fiscalism)\nThe second aim was to protect the revenue of the treasury. But in an economy with low productivity and very expensive transport it was hard to increase revenue. Genç writes that Ottoman budgets, measured in gold and silver value, did not grow much from the 1580s to the 1780s and were almost always in deficit. When the two principles clashed, it was generally the treasury that was sacrificed and the people's provisioning that was protected.\n\n■ Third principle: tradition\nWhen the institutions matured in the mid-16th century, a third principle was added: preserving the old balance. The same formula was always used in disputes: nothing was to be done contrary to 'what has been done since time immemorial' (kadimden olagelene). One kanunname (law code) defines 'kadim' (ancient) as that whose beginning no one remembers.\n\n■ The difference from Europe: mercantilism\nIn the same centuries, according to Kemal Beydilli's article on Europe in TDV, mercantilism prevailed in Europe: preventing precious metal from flowing out by means of an active foreign trade, and protecting domestic industry behind high customs walls. The aim was to increase the power of the state. The Ottomans did the opposite: they restricted and taxed exports and let imports in freely at low duties. According to Genç, the capitulations too were nourished by this outlook. That is why, even when it was at its strongest, the state did not curtail them but on the contrary extended them.", "154437a7"]},
"ilk-meclis-mebusan-secim-ve-yapi":{"baslik": ["How was the first Chamber of Deputies elected? (1877)", "088e9814"], "metin": ["■ A bicameral parliament\nThe constitution that came into force on 23 December 1876 provided for a parliament called the General Assembly (Meclis-i Umûmî), and this parliament consisted of two chambers: the Chamber of Deputies (Meclis-i Meb'ûsan), composed of deputies elected by the people, and the Chamber of Notables (Meclis-i A'yân), composed of members appointed by the sultan. According to the constitution, while one of the chambers was closed the other could not meet — that is, the two houses were tied to each other.\n\n■ THE ELECTION PROVIDED FOR BY THE CONSTITUTION\nThe constitution provided that one deputy should be elected for every 50,000 male inhabitants; that deputies should be chosen by SECRET BALLOT from among Ottoman subjects who had reached the age of thirty, had not lost their civil rights and good repute, did not enjoy the privileges of a foreign state and KNEW TURKISH; that elections should be held every four years; and that a deputy should represent not his electoral district but ALL OTTOMANS.\n\n■ WHAT HAPPENED IN PRACTICE\nBecause the electoral law was not ready in time, elections in the provinces were held under a provisional instruction dated 29 October 1876, and the conditions differed from those of the constitution: the ratio of one deputy per 50,000 people could not be applied, the age requirement of thirty was lowered to twenty-five, conviction for murder or a political offence was deemed a bar to election, and a requirement was introduced that deputies own property in their home region. The number of deputies was set at a total of 130, eighty Muslims and fifty non-Muslims, and the quotas were communicated to the provinces.\n\n■ WHO DID THE ELECTING\nThe deputies were elected NOT by the people but by the members of the provincial, sanjak and district (kaza) administrative councils whom the people had previously elected. The members put the slip on which they had written the candidate's name into a closed and sealed envelope and handed it to the district governor (kaymakam) in the kaza, the mutasarrıf in the sanjak and the governor in the province; the governor then formed a council of the leading men of the province and determined those elected. Whoever received the most votes was elected, and in case of a tie the winner was determined by DRAWING LOTS. A separate proclamation was issued for Istanbul and the election was held in two stages: the city was divided into twenty electoral districts and each district chose two electors, one Muslim and one non-Muslim; the electors met at the city prefecture and chose ten deputies, five Muslims and five non-Muslims.\n\n■ WHERE THE ASSEMBLY MET\nThe commission charged with finding a suitable place of work settled on the old university (darülfünun) building near Hagia Sophia. Places were made in the building from which the sultan, members of the government and visitors could follow the proceedings; a secretarial staff was set up to record the speeches, and stenographers were employed.\n\n■ THE OPENING\nThe General Assembly was opened on 19 March 1877 in the Ceremonial Hall (Muayede Salonu) of Dolmabahçe Palace, with a ceremony attended by the sultan, the dignitaries of state, the ulema, religious leaders and the heads of foreign missions; the opening speech was read on the sultan's behalf by the first secretary of the palace chancery (Mabeyn). Some deputies coming from remote parts of the country could not arrive in time for the ceremony. The next day the deputies took the oath and began their duties.", "8febc96f"], "kisa": ["In the first Ottoman parliament the deputies were not elected by the people: they were elected by the members of provincial and district councils whom the people had previously chosen — and the quotas were fixed from the start.", "359ebf39"]},
"ilk-meclis-mebusan-yetki-ve-akibet":{"baslik": ["The powers and closure of the first Chamber of Deputies", "518765b5"], "metin": ["■ The assembly's real task\nThe main duty of the assembly was to make laws and to examine and approve the annual budget law. But there was a critical limit: the right to propose new laws or to amend old ones belonged to the GOVERNMENT; for the deputies to use this right, the SULTAN'S PERMISSION was required. In other words, the assembly was not a body that made laws but one that approved them.\n\n■ HOW A LAW WAS PASSED\nA proposal was first referred to the Council of State (Şûrâ-yı Devlet), and once the procedures there were completed it was sent to the government or to the deputies. The president of the chamber referred it to the relevant committee, the committee prepared its report, and the bill was debated twice in the general assembly: in the first reading it was examined in broad outline, its articles were read out and those wishing to speak were identified; in the second reading, five days later, the articles were debated one by one. Budget bills were discussed chapter by chapter. A bill that was passed went to the Chamber of Notables (Hey'et-i A'yân), and once approved there too, it became law with the sultan's assent. Rejected bills could not be brought up again within the same year.\n\n■ THE LIMIT OF THE POWER OF OVERSIGHT\nUnder the constitution, the government was responsible not to the assembly but to the SULTAN. If a deputy lodged a complaint against a member of the government and a two-thirds majority of the assembly found the complaint justified, that minister could be sent to the High Court (Dîvân-ı Âlî) — that is, oversight existed, but its threshold was very high. If a disagreement arose between the government and the assembly and both sides persisted, the sultan could dissolve the assembly for new elections or change the government. While the assembly was not in session, the government could issue provisional laws.\n\n■ THE ASSEMBLY'S OWN WORKING ORDER\nOn 13 May 1877 the assembly drew up detailed standing orders; it was divided into five sections and various committees. Voting could be conducted by standing up, by show of hands, by collecting slips in a box or by secret ballot. A two-thirds vote was required to amend the constitution or to accept a motion of inquiry concerning a member of the government. Citizens could submit petitions to the assembly on condition that they stated their identity and address. Sessions could be held in three ways: open to an audience, private without an audience, and secret, with only the deputies and clerks present. Members could not be charged because of the opinions they expressed or the votes they cast, and could not be arrested without a decision of the assembly. The minutes were edited and made known to the public through the official gazette. Because representatives of different religious groups were present, both Friday and Sunday had been declared days off.\n\n■ TWO SESSIONS AND THE END\nThe first session ended on 28 June 1877; during this period many subjects were debated, from its standing orders to the war and the press regulation. Since the electoral law was still not ready, the deputies of the second session were also elected under the provisional instructions, and the assembly was opened with a modest ceremony on 13 December 1877; it had ninety-six members in all, fifty-six of them Muslims and forty non-Muslims. Working under the shadow of the war and of domestic and foreign problems, this assembly adopted a HARSHER tone toward the government than the first one had. The tension that built up between the assembly on one side and the government and the palace on the other ended when the sultan, relying on the authority granted by the constitution, suspended the assembly indefinitely on 13 February 1878. The deputies, who learned of the decision at the meeting the following day, returned to their home regions.\n\n■ SIGNIFICANCE\nThis assembly, which worked for eleven months, saw two sessions and then remained closed for thirty years, proved one thing despite its short life: Ottoman society was able to hold elections, and deputies of different religions and languages were able to debate the budget in the same hall. When the assembly was reopened in 1908, the electoral principles that were used also rested on two texts inherited from this period.", "c79852f8"]},
"iltizam-malikane-esham-zinciri":{"oneri": ["The state redesigned the right to collect taxes three times", "4065c421"], "sebep.b": ["Under the tax-farming (iltizam) system, the sale of the right to collect taxes at auction for short terms (one to three years) drove the tax farmer to overtax the reaya (tax-paying subjects) for quick profit and to exhaust the productive capacity of the revenue source (mukataa) — a fiscal vicious circle that repeated itself every time the tax farmer changed", "e05e4ab1"], "sonuc.b": ["With the introduction of the malikâne system, revenue sources (mukataas) were granted to the tax farmer (malikâne holder) for life at a fixed tax — an attempt to encourage the tax farmer from then on to invest in the reaya, since the tax was fixed while the profit was left free", "99c06f82"], "bag": ["According to TDV's article on the malikâne, in the last quarter of the seventeenth century rising war expenses and falling tax revenues had created a vicious circle; the malikâne 'was conceived as an institution that would make it possible to overcome this paradox'. The difference: in ordinary tax farming the auction was held on the amount of the annual tax (mal), whereas in the malikâne the amount of the annual tax was fixed in advance by the treasury, and the malikâne holder acquired his right by paying a lump sum down (muaccele). According to TDV's article on the esham, demand for the malikâne was limited to a narrow military class (women and non-Muslims were excluded); when, after the Ottoman-Russian War of 1768-74, the obligation arose to pay Russia an indemnity of 7.5 million kuruş, the state had to go beyond this limit and devised the esham system: the revenue of a mukataa was sliced into shares (sehim) and sold in return for a down payment (muaccele); the buyer did not interfere in management and only received an annual cash income in proportion to the amount he had invested.", "117f867a"], "metin": ["All three systems tried to solve the same problem — meeting the state's ever-growing fiscal needs without the capacity to collect taxes directly — in different ways. In tax farming, the short term (one to three years) pushed the tax farmer into exploitation; the malikâne softened this with a fixed tax for life, but it was open only to the military class, which limited demand; the esham widened demand by dividing the malikâne right into shares and making it — in theory — saleable to anyone. But the esham also bred its own crisis: according to TDV's article on the esham, the system grew rapidly (the annual interest payment rose from 400,000 kuruş to 2 million kuruş in ten years), and when the finance officials did the calculations in the 1780s, they found that the income from reselling the shares of deceased shareholders covered only a small part of the annual interest paid; the treasury came under a growing interest burden, and from 1792 new sales were halted. Malikâne sales themselves ended completely in the 1840s. Taken together, the three show the cycle of Ottoman finance in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries of 'inventing ever more complex financial instruments to raise revenue, with each instrument breeding its own crisis'.", "cdd1a571"]},
"islahat-fermani-tanzimatin-eksigi":{"sebep.b": ["Although the Tanzimat Edict proclaimed security of life, property and honour for all subjects, it left unchanged the non-Muslims' obligation to pay jizya, the barrier to their testimony in court and their exclusion from military service", "5b365101"], "sonuc.b": ["The Reform Edict of 1856 abolished the jizya, established mixed courts and non-Muslim testimony, and extended the military service obligation to all subjects, with the option of the military exemption tax (bedel-i askerî)", "dbc17bc6"], "bag": ["The two edicts state the same principle at two different depths: the Tanzimat proclaims equality as a guarantee, while the Reform Edict carries it down to concrete institutions such as taxation, courts and military service. The provision on military service remained on paper; it was not until 1908 that non-Muslims were actually taken into arms.", "72b80182"], "metin": ["The Reform Edict of 1856 was proclaimed on the eve of the Congress of Paris, which ended the Crimean War, and aimed to close four doors that the Tanzimat had left open. The jizya was abolished and replaced by the military exemption tax (bedel-i askerî); the obligation of military service for non-Muslims was introduced in principle. Mixed courts composed of Muslim and non-Muslim members were established, and non-Muslim testimony was accepted with an oath according to the witness's own faith. Foreigners and non-Muslims were granted the right to acquire and lease property. The internal order of the communities was rewritten, placing lay councils alongside the clergy and limiting the financial and administrative powers of the men of religion. All this changed the legal foundation of the millet system; its implementation, however, remained disputed and incomplete both at the centre and in the provinces.", "57c3a3ce"]},
"isyan-fenerli-voyvodalar":{"baslik": ["The Phanariot voivodes: why did Greeks from Istanbul rule Wallachia and Moldavia for 110 years?", "99330a42"], "metin": ["① The cause — the Pruth, 1711. The voivode of Moldavia, Dimitrie Cantemir (Kantemiroğlu), was a man who had lived in Istanbul for many years and had written an Ottoman history and a book on Turkish music; during his time as voivode he signed an alliance at Jassy with the Russian Tsar Peter. After the Ottoman forces had surrounded the tsar's army on the Pruth and Baltacı Mehmed Pasha had signed a treaty, Cantemir fled to Russia and became the tsar's adviser. The voivode of Wallachia, Constantin Brâncoveanu, on the other hand, was brought to Istanbul on the allegation that he had helped Peter on the Pruth, and was executed together with his four sons (1714) — curiously, it was Cantemir who had denounced him to the Sublime Porte, although it was he himself who had given the real help. The decision taken in the reign of Ahmed III arose from these two: no longer would native princes be appointed to the voivodeships of Moldavia and Wallachia, but Greek beys who could remain permanently under the control of the Sublime Porte. The first appointee was Nicholas Mavrocordatos, son of Alexander Mavrocordatos, dragoman of the Imperial Council: to Moldavia in 1711, and to Wallachia in 1716 after Stefan Cantacuzino. In the same years, in the face of the 18th-century Russian danger and the Austrian threat, Wallachia had begun to be regarded as the principal line of defence; Khotyn too was detached from the voivodeship in 1713 and made directly an Ottoman sanjak. Thus the logic of the system was one: internal administration to a trustworthy official, the frontier fortresses to the state.\n② WHO WERE THE PHANARIOTS — Fener (Phanar) is a district of Istanbul on the southern shore of the Golden Horn; when the seat of the Orthodox patriarchate settled there at the end of the 16th century, the district acquired its distinctive character, and wealthy Greek aristocratic families settled around the patriarchate. These families, called Fanariot in Western sources, sent their children to study in Italy; these “Phanariot gentlemen”, who spoke many European languages besides Turkish and Arabic and followed international politics, were, on returning to Istanbul, employed as dragomans of the Imperial Council and of the Imperial Fleet. The families listed by TDV are: Ghika, Callimachi, Caradja, Soutzo, Ypsilanti, Mourouzis, Ralli, Handjeri and Rosetti. These princes, called “hospodar” (gospodar) in Western sources, each became an official of the sultan and were appointed for three years at most. Nor was their influence confined to the two countries: until the Tanzimat, especially during the wars with Austria and Russia, they were active in the politics of the capital.\n   THE EXAMPLE OF ONE LIFE — Alexandre Handjéri (Hançerli). He was born in Istanbul; the family's nickname comes from a dagger (hançer) given as a reward for their service as the palace's private physicians in the time of Mehmed IV. In Ottoman sources his name appears as “Hançerî Bey, one of the beys of Bucharest”. Besides Western languages, he learned Turkish, Arabic and Persian; between August 1806 and February 1807 he was chief dragoman of the Sublime Porte, and between 7 March and 24 July 1807 voivode of Moldavia. After the deposition of Selim III he was forced to return to Istanbul; although he sought the voivodeship of Wallachia in 1815-1816 and in 1818, a new regulation made in 1819 deprived the Handjéri family of this right — the reason given being that they had established secret relations with the Filiki Eteria and helped Alexander Ypsilanti. When the revolt broke out in 1821 he fled to Odessa with his family, worked in Russia as a translator of Oriental languages, settled in Moscow and died there on 12 June 1854; he is known for his French-Arabic-Persian-Turkish dictionary (Moscow 1840-1841). 📌 The DATE in this biography is important: the Sublime Porte's distrust of a Phanariot family was set down in a DOCUMENT in 1819 — that is, two years before the revolt.\n③ HOW THE ERA PASSED — Two TDV articles describe the same era in different tones, and both are worth reading. The article on Moldavia is measured: in the time of these voivodes the moral condition of the Principalities was shaken and bribery and intrigue increased; nevertheless serfdom (bondage to the soil) was abolished, some personal liberties were granted to the peasants, and hospitals and schools were opened in many places. The article on Wallachia is much harsher: the era of the Phanariot Greek beys, which lasted more than 100 years, “is a period still remembered with hatred in Wallachia”; the beys supported by the patriarchate exploited the local people together with the relatives and Greek business partners they brought with them. The same article also adds a political dimension: after 1750 these beys, in cooperation with the patriarchate of Istanbul, sought a way to revive the old Byzantine State and spread Byzantine nationalism; Wallachia became the economic source feeding this nationalism and its training ground. TDV nevertheless records the positive side: in the Phanariot era Wallachia opened up to the West, established schools and hospitals, and even took forward-looking steps such as the abolition of slavery — but the people lived amid great hardship.\n④ THE END — 1821, THE PHANARIOTS THEMSELVES ENDED THE SYSTEM. According to TDV's article on Wallachia, Tudor Vladimirescu prepared a revolt in 1821 with the aim of getting rid of the Phanariot Greeks who were grievously exploiting the people; this coincided in date with Alexander Ypsilanti's Eteria movement. The two movements DID NOT UNITE: to Ypsilanti's call to fight together against the Turks, Vladimirescu replied, “I rose up not against the Turks but against the Greeks”; his soldiers clashed with Ypsilanti's soldiers near Bucharest, and the Wallachian commander was betrayed and killed by Ypsilanti. TDV also notes that Russian historians wished to present these two movements as a single movement directed against the Turks in the Balkans. On the Moldavian side, by contrast, the voivode himself was part of the revolt: the movement organised by Ypsilanti was supported by the voivode of Moldavia, Michael Soutzo (Mihail Suça), who was of Greek origin. Thereupon Mahmud II abandoned the appointment of Phanariot beys; in 1822 he appointed to Moldavia one of the native princes, Ion Sandu Sturdza, and in Wallachia too he set about bringing the native families to power as before. The 110-year system was closed not by a reform of the Sublime Porte but by the joining of the revolt by the system's own personnel.\n   ⇒ WHAT APPEARS ON THE MAP — Throughout these 110 years Wallachia and Moldavia remain on the map in the VASSAL colour; what changes is not the owner of the land but who the voivode is. The shell around them, however, is dark, and that shell was built up stage by stage: Kiliia (Kili) and Akkerman have been direct Ottoman sanjaks since 1484, Bender-Budjak since 1538, and Khotyn since 1713. In other words, this card appears NOT as a change of colour on the map but in the chronology — the administrative personnel changed in 1821 did not move the borders.", "8a50d36c"]},
"isyan-sirp-milli-anlati":{"baslik": ["The Serbian uprisings in the Serbian narrative: not a “revolt” but the “Serbian Revolution”", "dbc95e39"], "metin": ["① The contemporary narrative of the other side — According to Dušan T. Bataković, the movement that began in 1804 was at first a peasant uprising against the local Janissaries, and after 1805 it gradually acquired a national character. The rebels donned the coat of arms of the medieval Nemanjić dynasty; the Governing Council (Praviteljstvujušči sovjet), established in 1805, held its sessions in Smederevo beneath a portrait of Emperor Stefan Dušan. The letter of authority that Karađorđe gave in 1806 to the delegation that was to go to the Habsburg and Russian emperors was written with the phrase “on behalf of the whole Serbian nation” and with the signature “Karađorđe Petrović, commander-in-chief in Serbia”. The report of an Ottoman official held captive in Serbia in 1806 lays bare the connection in the rebels' minds: they always keep with them Jovan Rajić's history book telling of Knez Lazar, and it is this that drove them to revolt. In other words, on the Serbian side 1804 was constructed as the continuation of Kosovo in 1389 (for the Kosovo epic itself: karsi-kosova-1389). Intellectual support came from the other side of the border: Dositej Obradović's ode beginning “Arise, Serbia” became the rebels' anthem, and in 1805 Count Sava Tekelija sent the first 500 copies of the map of Serbia, Bosnia, Dubrovnik and Montenegro that he had printed in Vienna directly to the rebel leadership. An interesting detail: the first historian of the Serbian revolution was a Greek — Triantafillos Doukas printed his History of the Slavo-Serbs in Budapest as early as 1807. The first great narrative in Serbian is Vuk Stefanović Karadžić's book Miloš Obrenović, knjaz Serbii, ili gradja za srpsku istoriju našega vremena, printed in Buda in 1828; in today's academic studies the scenes of 1815 are still based on it page by page.\n② TODAY'S MEMORY AND HISTORIOGRAPHY — The one who gave it its name was the German historian Leopold von Ranke: because of the long-term effect it left on the political and social order of the region, he likened the uprising of 1804-1813 to the French example and described it as the “Serbian Revolution” (History of Servia and the Servian Revolution, London 1848). Today's Serbian historiography has broadened this name: Srpska revolucija 1804-1835 is divided in two — the period of war (1804-1815) and the period of peace (1815-1835); the milestone separating the two is the Second Serbian Uprising (Radoš Ljušić's periodisation). That is why, in Serbian memory, 1815 is the date not of a firman but OF AN ASSEMBLY: on 23 April 1815, on Palm Sunday (Cveti), the people's assembly that gathered beside the church in Takovo elected Miloš Obrenović (1783-1860), obor-knez of the nahiyes of Kragujevac, Rudnik and Čačak, as vožd (leader) of the uprising. In Serbian sources the end of the uprising too is not a surrender but an agreement: the VERBAL agreement reached in Belgrade on 6 November 1815 between Maraşlı Ali Pasha and knez Miloš. With it, dual administration (dvovlašće) was established in the pashalik: alongside the Ottoman administration there were Serbian organs — the Narodna kancelarija, the highest judicial and administrative body, the obor-knezes of the nahiyes and the village kmets — and the Serbs collected the tax. The verbal agreement was given legal form by eight firmans issued by the Sublime Porte in the winter of 1815/16; the jizya was fixed at the lowest bracket, 3 kuruş, and in the pashalik of Belgrade it was never raised until 1833. And within the same narrative stands a FOUNDING CRIME: on 25 July 1817, on the knez's orders, Đorđe Petrović Karađorđe, vožd of the First Serbian Uprising, was killed; two days later his severed head was delivered to the pasha of Belgrade, and the pasha sent it on to Istanbul at the beginning of August. Miloš, in his own words, never felt any remorse for this murder; on the contrary, he used the situation to strengthen his position. On 18 November of the same year the Narodna skupština that met in Belgrade proclaimed him — at his own wish — HEREDITARY knez. Nor was he the only rival to die: in 1817 knez Sima Marković and captain Dragić Gorunović, who opposed Miloš, were handed over to the pasha and beheaded on 21 March, and the knez also had their “accomplices”, voyvoda Pavle Cukić and pop Sava, killed. The Obrenović dynasty, that is, the Obrenović-Karađorđević feud that would divide Serbian politics until 1903, was built upon these purges.\n③ THE OTTOMAN NARRATIVE — According to TDV's article on Serbia, the Janissaries, who increased their influence in the sanjak of Smederevo after 1739, came to be the masters of Belgrade, levied heavy taxes on the people and forced the Serbian peasant into corvée; “the ever-increasing oppression by the Janissaries called dayıs and yamaks” brought about the revolt, which broke out in 1804 under the leadership of Karađorđe. In this narrative TDV regards the national character as acquired later, and also writes that the Serbian units that took Belgrade carried out a great massacre of the Muslims in the city. The Treaty of Bucharest of 1812 brought partial autonomy; in 1813 the revolt was completely suppressed and Karađorđe fled to Austria. For 1815, TDV's sentence is short and sets up an entirely different framework: “The second Serbian revolt broke out in 1815 under the leadership of a Serbian knez named Miloš Obrenović. Although the revolt was suppressed in the same year, from this date onwards the privileges granted to the Serbs began to be extended. Miloš Obrenović was appointed chief knez.” The real story behind the Ottoman documents lies between the two: when news of the revolt reached Istanbul, Mahmud II issued a firman on 25 May 1815 ordering the governor of Bosnia, Hurşid Ahmed Pasha, and the governor of Rumelia, Maraşlı Ali Pasha, to “root out the rebels and force them into obedience” (in Serbian historiography it is referred to as the “death firman”). The assembling of the army took a long time, and the units reached the border only in July; in the meantime Waterloo took place on 18 June, the international situation changed, and an order went to the governors to pacify the rebels by peaceful means. Maraşlı made better use of this interval than Hurşid Ahmed Pasha: he did not impose on the Serbs the condition of laying down their arms, contented himself with “obedience to the sultan”, in return carried the rebels' complaint against Süleyman Pasha Skopljak to Istanbul, and by a firman of 22 November 1815 himself took over the post of guardian (muhafız) of Belgrade.\n④ WHERE THEY DIVERGE — The name: on one side a “revolt” (and two separate revolts), on the other a single “revolution” (and a single process spanning 1804-1835). The ending: TDV says “the revolt was suppressed in the same year, Miloš WAS APPOINTED chief knez”; the Serbian academic narrative says “the uprising ended with the verbal agreement of Belgrade on 6 November 1815 and dual administration was established” — the two are describing the same months. The hero: Serbian memory has two founders, and each undermines the other; the heroic rebel Karađorđe and the pragmatist Miloš who had him killed. In the Ottoman source, by contrast, there is only “an appointed chief knez”, not the founder of a dynasty. What is not in dispute? The NUMBERS of the agreement: a jizya of 3 kuruş, the Narodna kancelarija, the collection of the tax by the Serbs — these rest on Ottoman firmans and are recorded in the same way on both sides. What they differ over is not the facts but the story into which the facts are placed. (For the language of Serbian textbooks today and the formula of the “five-hundred-year yoke”: bakis-sirp. For the question of the link between the Serbian revolt and the French Revolution: the relevant further-reading card.)", "c96163aa"], "kisa": ["In the Ottoman source, 1804 and 1815 are two separate revolts: the first was suppressed, and after the second Miloš Obrenović was appointed chief knez. In Serbian historiography, however, the two are two phases of a single event, and its name is the “Serbian Revolution” (1804-1835) — the name Ranke gave it by likening it to the French Revolution. This is why it is regarded as foundational: the modern Serbian state was born of this revolution. But the same narrative also carries a founding crime — Miloš's having Karađorđe killed.", "0127f90a"]},
"isyan-ypsilanti":{"baslik": ["Alexander Ypsilanti: the tsar's aide-de-camp, head of the Eteria, the man who crossed the Pruth", "86d9f4c5"], "metin": ["① Do not confuse the three Alexanders — The Ypsilanti family is one of the Phanariot families listed by TDV and produced three Alexanders in three generations. The grandfather, Alexandros Ypsilanti, was voivode in Wallachia and Moldavia at the end of the 18th century; TDV's article on Moldavia writes that in his time some obligations previously imposed on the people were lifted by Abdülhamid I (1774) — that is, half a century before the revolt the family belonged to the personnel the state trusted. The father, Constantine Ypsilanti, was voivode of Wallachia, and in 1804, immediately after Karađorđe's revolt, encouraged by the Russian foreign minister Count Czartoryski, he developed the idea of founding a great Christian state in the Balkans and sent the Serbian rebels arms, provisions and a small military unit. The son, Alexander Ypsilanti (1792-1828), is the man of 1821. This card is about him.\n② BIOGRAPHY — Alexander Ypsilanti held the rank of major general in the Russian army and was aide-de-camp to Tsar Alexander I. The Filiki Eteria (Society of Friends) was a secret organisation founded in 1814 by Greek merchants and politicians in Odessa, an important Russian port city on the Black Sea. The organisation first offered the leadership to Ioannis Kapodistrias; Kapodistrias refused and warned that an uprising would be folly. Thereupon the leadership was given to Ypsilanti in April 1820.\n③ THE SERBIAN CONNECTION — The Eteria's representative in Serbia was Georgios Olympios, who had taken part in the First Serbian Uprising and was known among the Serbs as “Kapetan Jorgaç”. In June 1817 the exiled Karađorđe was made to swear the oath of the Eteria in a garden at Galata near Jassy — at Constantine Ypsilanti's house; he was then taken to the Serbian border and was killed a few days after entering Serbia (on Miloš's orders; see isyan-sirp-milli-anlati). In January 1821 Alexander Ypsilanti sent knez Miloš a draft alliance of ten articles; the Eterist carrying the draft was caught at Ada Kaleh, taken to Istanbul and executed. Miloš did not rise up during the whole of the Greek war.\n④ THE PRUTH — In February 1821 Ypsilanti crossed the river Pruth from Russian soil, in the uniform of the Russian army, entered Moldavia and reached Jassy. His proclamation called for a fight for “faith and fatherland”, stressed the natural right to liberty and invited the Greeks to emulate the peoples of Europe who were rising up for “liberty and happiness”. The Russian support he expected DID NOT COME: Tsar Alexander I ordered him to lay down his arms and expelled him from the Russian army; the Congress of Laibach (Ljubljana), meeting at the time, condemned the uprising; in Istanbul the patriarch excommunicated him. Ottoman forces suppressed the movement in June. THE SOURCES CONTRADICT EACH OTHER AT THIS POINT, and the card gives both: TDV's article on Wallachia describes Ypsilanti as having “gone into action in 1821 in the Crimea with the support of the Russian tsar”; the academic source consulted (Heraclides-Dialla), however, says that the tsar rejected him. TDV's article on Greece, too, gives Odessa, not the Crimea, as the place where the organisation was founded. The text of the card took the academic side as its basis and did not hide TDV's wording.\n⑤ VLADIMIRESCU — When Ypsilanti came down into Wallachia, there was already another uprising there: that of Tudor Vladimirescu. According to TDV, Vladimirescu's revolt was against Phanariot Greek rule; to Ypsilanti's call to fight together against the Turks he replied, “I rose up not against the Turks but against the Greeks.” The two forces clashed near Bucharest, and the Wallachian commander was betrayed and killed by Ypsilanti. This is the critical sentence for Ottoman chronology: in the Wallachia of 1821 there was no SINGLE front against the Ottomans; there were TWO separate movements, one against Phanariot Greek rule and one against the Ottomans, and they destroyed each other.\n⑥ HIS END — The course Ypsilanti took in the Principalities is severely criticised in academic assessment: Heraclides and Dialla describe his conduct as a mixture of arrogance, cruelty and incompetence. The campaign ended in rout; Ypsilanti fled to the Austrian Empire, and the remaining Eterists (Iordache Olimpiotul, Iane Farmache and others) resisted the Ottoman army for some time longer, using churches and monasteries as their headquarters. Alexander Ypsilanti died in 1828. The real consequence of the movement, however, lies elsewhere: in the words of TDV's article on Moldavia, “the movement produced results in Greece before Wallachia” — the revolt defeated in Wallachia and Moldavia turned into a war of independence that was won in the Morea (see the entry of 25 March 1821 and, for the end of the Phanariot system: isyan-fenerli-voyvodalar).", "b26a94d0"], "kisa": ["The son of a Phanariot voivodal family, a major general in the Russian army and aide-de-camp to Tsar Alexander I. In April 1820 he took the head of the Filiki Eteria, in February 1821 he crossed the Pruth in Russian uniform, entered Jassy and started the Greek War of Independence. The tsar ordered him to lay down his arms and expelled him from the army, the patriarch excommunicated him, and Vladimirescu said, “I rose up not against the Turks but against the Greeks”; by June it was all over. He himself fled to Austria and died in 1828.", "753dbcc5"]},
"kabakci-isyani-niye-cikti":{"sebep.b": ["The incitement claiming that the yamaks (auxiliary garrison troops) in the Bosphorus forts were to be brought into the Nizam-i Cedid (New Order) system and made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms; the killing of Mahmud Raif Efendi, Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and Halil Haseki", "cf38e5a2"], "sonuc.b": ["Selim III's abolition of the Nizam-i Cedid with all its institutions and practices, his deposition and the accession of Mustafa IV", "4e31054c"], "bag": ["The visible face of the revolt was Kabakçı Mustafa, a Janissary from Kastamonu; the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam writes that the “real architects behind the scenes” of the uprising were Köse Musa Pasha, the deputy grand vizier (kaymakam), and the Shaykh al-Islam Topal Ataullah Mehmed Efendi.", "8f1c1945"], "metin": ["The setting. The Nizam-i Cedid had been launched after the Treaty of Jassy of 1792 and had carried out important reforms, but from 1805 onward it began to be shaken by reactions and acts of protest. The Janissaries and members of the ulema who were dissatisfied with its measures found an opportunity to widen the opposition front by taking a common stand. The attempt to apply the Nizam-i Cedid in Rumelia as well came to nothing because of the resistance of the ayan (provincial notables) and armed clashes (the Second Edirne Incident, 1806) — the TDV article `selim-iii` regards this retreat as the turning point of Selim III's reign. The closure of the pilgrimage routes owing to the Wahhabi revolts and the arrival of the British fleet off Istanbul in February 1807 dealt a heavy blow to the sultan's authority; the fleet's blockade caused scarcity and high prices in the city, bringing the accumulated anger to the point of explosion.\n\nTHE SPARK. Because of the war with Russia, the army had left Istanbul on 12 April 1807. When the forts on the Bosphorus had to be reinforced against the Russian threat, the intention of bringing the yamaks there into the Nizam-i Cedid system became an opportunity for Köse Musa Pasha's covert agitation. The incitement to the effect that the yamaks would be made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms, and that Mahmud Raif Efendi, Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and Halil Haseki, commander of the Hungarian battery, had been assigned to this task, resulted in the killing of these two men — this is the starting point of the revolt (25 May 1807).\n\nWHY it could not be suppressed. When the uprising became known in Istanbul, Köse Musa Pasha presented it as a mere outburst of indignation; the possibility of intervention by the drilled troops at Levent Farm and the initiative of the chief of the artillery were likewise eliminated by his schemes. Selim III was being given misleading information. The figure the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam underlines is this: the sultan, who failed to show the foresight to use the Nizam-i Cedid soldiers in Istanbul, who numbered more than 10,000, first sacrificed the officials demanded of him and then agreed to the abolition of the Nizam-i Cedid. Even this did not end the revolt; he himself was deposed, and on 21 Rabi al-Awwal 1222 / 29 May 1807 Mustafa IV was placed on the throne.\n\nTHE AFTERMATH. On 31 May 1807 a “hüccet-i şer'iyye” (a sharia court deed) was drawn up: in return for the soldiers' undertaking not to interfere in affairs of state from then on, they would not be called to account for the revolt. The deed did not hold. After the revolt Kabakçı Mustafa obtained the post of Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and fifteen days before Mahmud II was placed on the throne he was killed by Ali Agha, the ayan of Pınarhisar, sent by Alemdar Mustafa Pasha. The verdict of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam is severe: with this revolt the era of renewal was closed until 1826, and the state's “great opportunity to reform itself in a way that would keep pace with the developments of the age” was thwarted.", "58445285"]},
"kanuni-olumu-gizlendi":{"soru": ["How was the death of Suleiman the Lawgiver concealed?", "82d5042d"], "baslik": ["The coffin beneath the throne", "6dee61ec"], "metin": ["Suleiman the Lawgiver died on 7 September 1566 while the siege of Szigetvár was still going on, but his death was concealed with great skill. According to Feridun Bey, one of the few people who knew the secret, the sultan's internal organs were removed and his body, prepared with ambergris and musk, was placed in a coffin and temporarily buried beneath the throne. Forty-two days later the corpse was secretly placed in a carriage, and all along the road people behaved as if the sultan were still alive; the death was officially proclaimed only when Selim II arrived in Belgrade. The funeral ceremony was held for the third time on 23 November in Istanbul, at the Süleymaniye Mosque, and the sultan was buried in the tomb beside the mosque.", "dd52257b"], "not": ["The details of the concealment rest on the account of Feridun Bey, who was involved in the event.", "a9c307d9"]},
"kapitulasyon-diplomatik-araçtan-mali-bagimliliga":{"oneri": ["It began 562 years earlier as the gift of an alliance and ended by taking away control over customs.", "31efcffe"], "sebep.b": ["The first commercial privilege that Orhan Gazi signed with the Genoese — a unilateral and revocable grant, designed as a diplomatic and military instrument of alliance of the period", "b804b273"], "sonuc.b": ["The unilateral abolition of the capitulations by the Ottoman government on the eve of the First World War — the end of a regime of privileges lasting four centuries, which by then had turned into a web of financial dependence structurally limiting sovereignty over customs and justice", "0a38b258"], "bag": ["The chronology's entry for 18 February 1536 sums up this transformation in a single sentence: 'the capitulations, a diplomatic instrument that day, would over the centuries turn into the gateway to financial dependence.' TDV's article on the ahidname confirms the intermediate link: after France (1536), England (1580) and the Netherlands (1612) received similar privileges; in the eighteenth century several European states attained 'most-favored-nation' status, and after the British first succeeded in lowering the customs duty to 3%, others who paid 5% also began to trade under the British flag — in other words, there was a competitive downward dynamic among the capitulations. The entry for 30 May 1740 marks the turning point: the privileges ceased to be limited to a single sultan and were tied to a permanent treaty, and 'the regime of capitulations, which became impossible to terminate unilaterally, could be abolished only at Lausanne' — even the abolition of 1914 was a unilateral declaration made under wartime conditions, and it became legally permanent only at Lausanne in 1923.", "f7594cf7"], "metin": ["At first, capitulation meant 'privilege': it was a grant that a Muslim state made unilaterally to Christian states so that they could trade in safety — the sultan could revoke it if he wished, and the foreign merchant was subject not to the court of the Ottoman qadi but to his own consul (judicial capitulation). Over time it changed along three axes: ① Duration — from a grant that in 1352 could be considered void on the sultan's death, it turned after 1740 into a permanent treaty; ② Scope — from only customs and judicial privileges at first, it expanded with Baltalimanı in 1838 into the legal basis for opening the whole Ottoman market (including domestic trade) to free competition; ③ Number — limited to one or two states in the sixteenth century, in the nineteenth it spread automatically, through the 'most-favored-nation' clause, to almost all the great European states. When these three axes came together, the capitulation turned from the 'diplomatic gesture' of the beginning into a structural constraint under which the Ottoman government could not determine its own customs tariff or its jurisdiction over foreigners on its own territory. Even its abolition shows this: the government could do it only by a unilateral declaration, at a moment when Europe was occupied with the First World War.", "9a4f8e0e"]},
"karsi-caldiran-1514":{"baslik": ["The Battle of Chaldiran: from the point of view of Safavid and Iranian historiography", "b6a043af"], "metin": ["① The other side's contemporary account — On the Safavid side the most important source for the campaign is the anonymous history of Shah Ismail; Khwandamir's Habib al-siyar and the Ahsan al-tawarikh of Hasan-i Rumlu, of the following generation, also narrate the event. According to the account based on Safavid sources, two commanders who knew Ottoman tactics well, Muhammad Khan Ustajlu and Nur Ali Khalifa, advised the shah to attack the enemy immediately. The courtier Durmish Khan Shamlu and the shah himself rejected this advice; they considered it cowardice to attack an unprepared enemy. This delay gave the Ottoman army the opportunity to take up its positions. Writing about a hundred years later, Iskandar Beg Munshi explained the defeat by God's wisdom: in his view, had the shah won this battle too, the devotion of the Kizilbash to him would have gone to excess and their faith would have strayed from the right path. Present-day researchers do not find this explanation convincing. According to the Venetian Caterino Zeno, writing in the same period, had Ismail won he would have become the absolute master of the East.\n② MEMORY TODAY — In Iranian historiography Chaldiran is regarded as a turning point in the life of Shah Ismail. According to the Iranian historian Nasrollah Falsafi, the defeat turned the shah's pride into despair: the shah put on black clothing and a black turban, had his standards dyed black and never again went to war at the head of his army. The Kizilbash saw the shah as a semi-sacred spiritual guide and an invincible leader; according to researchers, this bond was shaken beyond repair. The capture of the shah's wife, Tajlu Khanum, also turned into a story told in differing versions in later sources.\n③ THE OTTOMAN ACCOUNT — Ottoman historiography narrates the battle as a necessary campaign against Safavid propaganda and the rebellions in Anatolia. The battle appears in the works of authors such as İdris-i Bitlisi, Celalzade and Kemalpaşazade and in the proclamations of victory (fetihname). In this account the keys to victory are the generalship of Sultan Selim the Grim and the artillery; the restoration of Anatolian unity and the acquisition of Southeastern Anatolia are counted as the most important results. According to the Ottoman account, a close associate of the shah, introducing himself as the shah in his place, gave himself up, and this enabled the shah to escape.\n④ WHERE THEY DIVERGE — Size of the armies: Turkish research describes the Safavid army as 'at least as large as the Ottoman army' and fresh; Encyclopaedia Iranica, on the other hand, says that the Ottoman army was at least twice as large and presents as the most reliable figures those given by İdris-i Bitlisi: 100,000 Ottoman and 40,000 Safavid soldiers. Cause of the defeat: in the Ottoman account, cannon, muskets and good command; in the Safavid account, a mistaken decision that delayed the attack; Iranian research adds to this the Ottomans' numerical superiority and firearms. Date: both sides place the battle on 2 Rajab 920, that is, 23 August 1514; for the entry into Tabriz, however, the dates 5, 6 and 7 September are found. Result: for the Ottomans a decisive victory that secured Anatolia; according to Iranica, however, a partial success for the Ottomans and a catastrophe for the Safavids and especially for Shah Ismail — because of supply difficulties the Ottoman army could not winter in Tabriz. (For the course of the battle: savas-caldiran-1514.)", "4efe02cc"], "kisa": ["Safavid historiography attributed the defeat at Chaldiran to a bad decision and, in the following century, to God's wisdom. Present-day researchers agree that the defeat shattered Shah Ismail's image as an 'invincible spiritual guide'. Ottoman and Iranian sources differ most over the size of the two armies.", "aee0eb29"]},
"karsi-inebahti-1571":{"baslik": ["The Naval Battle of Lepanto: from the point of view of the Holy League", "36b86ddb"], "metin": ["① The other side's contemporary account — As the news spread across Europe, celebrations began. According to a letter from a Mantuan, the news was received with great joy in Brussels on 30 October; a few days later processions were held and prayers offered in Madrid. All over Italy the people openly showed their joy. The joy was felt most of all in Venice: the celebrations went on for weeks, and the victory was engraved in memory with music, poetry and ceremonies. For Venice this was no ordinary victory; it seemed to be the first military success in decades of which it could be proud. On 26 December 1571 King Philip II sent letters to the archbishops, religious orders, governors and city councils in Central and South America; he asked that festivities, masses and processions be held and that those who had died in the battle be remembered as Catholics who had been martyred 'in holy service'. Those who took part in the battle also placed the victory at the centre of their own life stories: Cervantes, who would later write Don Quixote, was among those wounded that day.\n② MEMORY TODAY — The battle left a lasting mark on the Catholic calendar. According to the Catholic Encyclopedia of 1913, Pope Pius V declared 7 October the feast of 'Our Lady of Victory' in memory of the victory; in 1573 Pope Gregory XIII turned this commemoration into the feast of the 'Holy Rosary', celebrated on the first Sunday of October. According to the same encyclopedia, the processions held by the rosary confraternities in Rome on the day of the victory were associated with the victory 'in a miraculous way'. It was Pope Clement XI who extended the feast to the whole Catholic world; this decision came after the victory won against the Ottomans at Petrovaradin in 1716. Some popular books describe Lepanto as 'the victory of the West' or 'the victory of Christianity over Islam'; the historian Stefan Hanß criticizes this account as a 'mythical frame'. In his view Lepanto was not an event belonging to Europe alone; news and stories of it spread from the Americas to Iran and as far as Japan.\n③ THE OTTOMAN ACCOUNT — In Ottoman sources the battle is also referred to as 'Sıngın Donanma' (the Broken Fleet) and is regarded as the first great battle in which the fleet was lost. At the centre of the account are a weary fleet and a mistaken decision: after a campaign lasting six months, the ships were short of oarsmen and soldiers; Uluç Ali Pasha proposed waiting in the sheltered gulf, but the Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Müezzinzade Ali Pasha insisted on battle, citing the order he had received. The reaction in Istanbul was harsh: the Shaykh al-Islam Ebussuud Efendi wrote in a fatwa that those who drowned while fleeing from the battle had incurred the wrath of God; Pertev Pasha, commander (serdar) of the fleet, was retired. The name of Uluç Ali, who had saved his fleet, was changed to Kılıç, and he was given the office of Kapudan Pasha. While the celebrations were going on in Italy, the grand vizier Sokollu Mehmed Pasha started the construction of a new fleet; the fleet put to sea again on 13 June 1572. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam regards the battle as the last great Crusade of the Catholic world and 'a temporary victory without lasting results': Cyprus could not be recovered, in 1573 Venice agreed to pay an indemnity, and Tunis was conquered in 1574. The same assessment also acknowledges that the battle destroyed the belief in Europe that 'the Turks are invincible'. (For the 'beard and arm' saying attributed to Sokollu: vecize-sokullu-sakal-kol.)\n④ WHERE THEY DIVERGE — Meaning: contemporaries in Europe saw the victory as a turning point in the fate of Christianity, even as a miracle; the Ottoman assessment, by contrast, stresses that it had no lasting result — the main aim of the Holy League, saving Cyprus, was not achieved. What the two sides have in common is that the battle shook the belief that 'the Turks are invincible'. Heroes and culprits: the Christian account highlights the heroism of Don John and his soldiers, and the religious account also the power of the rosary prayer; the Ottoman account seeks the cause of the defeat at home (the weariness of the fleet, the Kapudan Pasha's insistence) and presents as heroes Kılıç Ali Pasha, who saved his fleet, and Sokollu, who rebuilt the navy. Numbers: according to the summary in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the Ottoman fleet had about 230 ships and 25 thousand fighting men, and the allied fleet 243 ships and 37 thousand fighting men; the Ottomans suffered about 20 thousand dead, and 190 of their ships were sunk or captured. Another article of the same encyclopaedia gives the Ottoman loss in ships as 200-250 — that is, the sources use different figures even among themselves. (For the course of the battle: savas-inebahti-1571.)", "dfa96451"], "kisa": ["In the world of the Holy League, Lepanto was celebrated as a miraculous victory that changed the fate of Christianity, and it became a church feast. In Ottoman sources the same day is a heavy defeat referred to as 'Sıngın Donanma' (the Broken Fleet), but it is assessed as a temporary victory with no lasting result. Present-day historians question the account of 'the victory of Christianity over Islam' as a mythical frame.", "71b18f0b"]},
"karsi-istanbul-1453":{"baslik": ["The Conquest of Istanbul: from the Byzantine/Greek point of view", "fbd1aa48"], "metin": ["① The other side's contemporary account — Four historians wrote about the event from the Greek side: Sphrantzes, Doukas, Kritoboulos and Chalkokondyles. Sphrantzes (George Sphrantzes) was a close associate of the emperor. In his account the fall is a punishment: he writes that despite the emperor's prayers and donations, God disregarded these efforts, without anyone knowing 'why or for what sins'. He calls Constantine a 'martyr'. He says that the city fell on Tuesday, 29 May, that the emperor was killed at that time, and that he himself was not at his side at that moment. Doukas was not in the city during the siege. In his account the city falls when a group of fifty men, entering through a gate that had been forgotten open, climb onto the walls. Doukas also reports that among the people opposed to the union of the churches there circulated a saying that preferred the Turkish turban to the Latin headgear. Kritoboulos, for his part, was a Greek who entered the Sultan's service after the conquest and wrote the life of Mehmed II. Thus one of the defeated told the story of the same catastrophe as the historian of the victorious ruler. The Venetian Nicolò Barbaro, who was present at the siege, writes in his day-by-day record that it could not be learned whether the emperor had died, and that reports came that he had strangled himself.\n② MEMORY TODAY — Because it is not known for certain how the emperor died, his death turned into legend. According to Greek popular belief, Constantine did not die: he was turned into marble, and one day he will wake and take back the city. This belief is known as the 'Marble Emperor'; the historian Donald Nicol's book on Constantine deals with both his life and this legend. In Russia, meanwhile, in the 16th century the monk Filofei of Pskov wrote that Moscow was the 'Third Rome': the first Rome had fallen through heresy and the second, Constantinople, through sin, while the third still stood. According to the historian David Goldfrank, Filofei's letters were written between 1523 and 1526. Goldfrank stresses that this idea never played any role in Moscow's foreign policy and that there is no basis for regarding it as a fundamental element of Russian consciousness since the 1500s.\n③ THE OTTOMAN ACCOUNT — On the Ottoman side the event is narrated by Tursun Bey, who witnessed the siege, and by Aşıkpaşazade, Oruç Bey and Kemalpaşazade. In this account the conquest is an outcome of the ghaza tradition and is remembered together with the Prophet's saying foretelling the conquest of Constantinople. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, when Mehmed II entered the city the army was brought under discipline, the plundering was stopped, Hagia Sophia was converted into a mosque and the people were promised safety. According to Tursun Bey, while the emperor was trying to flee toward the seaward side, his horse stumbled and fell, and he was killed by a wounded azeb (light infantryman). After the conquest the Conqueror regarded himself as an heir of the Roman imperial tradition as well.\n④ WHERE THEY DIVERGE — Numbers: Sphrantzes gives the Ottoman army as 200,000 men; as for the defenders, on the basis of a census taken on the emperor's orders he counts 4,773 Greeks and about 200 foreigners. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the figures given in the sources for the Ottoman army range from 100,000 to 300,000; the actual number was probably around 100,000. Cause: the Greek writers attribute the fall to sin, to the internal division arising from the effort to unite the churches and to the help that did not come from the West. The Ottoman account rests on the ghaza, the cannon and the sultan's determination. Hero: the hero of Greek memory is the 'martyr' emperor who vanished on the walls, while the hero of the Ottoman account is the sultan who won the title 'the Conqueror'. For the emperor's death there are mutually inconsistent accounts, such as death by the sword, a rumour of suicide and being crushed in the crowd. The same event is for one side the end of an age and for the other the beginning of a capital. (For the period when Byzantium paid tribute to the Ottomans: statu-bizans-haracguzar.)", "e012b137"], "kisa": ["For Ottoman sources, 29 May 1453 is a conquest and the birth of a new capital. For the Greek writers who lived through the siege, it is a catastrophe: they ask 'for what sins' they lost the city and regard the last emperor as a martyr. In later centuries this loss turned into the legend of the undying emperor and into Moscow's claim to be the 'Third Rome'.", "7a15742e"]},
"karsi-karansebes-1788":{"baslik": ["Karánsebes (Şebeş) 1788: the truth behind the story of 'the army that defeated itself'", "044cbdd4"], "metin": ["① What happened — the documented part. In the war of 1787-1792, on the night of 20/21 September 1788, Joseph II's army evacuated its camp at Ilova and was withdrawing to Karánsebes (Şebeş in Ottoman sources). According to the army's own campaign journal, at the bridge over the Temes at Slatina a Wallachian peasant, in spite of the ban, was selling brandy from his cart; the hussars stopped to drink, and when the Wallachian volunteer infantry coming up behind demanded a share, a quarrel broke out; the soldiers who had been driven off fired their muskets a little farther on and shouted 'Turci! Turci!' (Turks! Turks!). In the darkness panic spread, units fired on one another and the baggage column was overturned. Two contemporary documents relate this: the campaign journal in the army's own war archive, and the letter the Emperor wrote to his brother Leopold on 26 September 1788. In his letter Joseph writes that his soldiers fired on one another and that they were lucky the Turks were not at their heels. In other words, the claim that 'there is no contemporary record' is not true.\n② WHAT THE FIGURES SAY. The list of losses in the campaign journal is as follows: three guns, 14 ammunition wagons, 20 air rifles, about 1,000 knapsacks, 600 coats, tents, cauldrons and horses; on the human side 1 officer, 24 Jäger (light infantry) and 538 private soldiers are entered as 'missing', but the record itself says that most of these came back. As for those killed by their own side's fire, the record says only that 'some officers and soldiers died, others were wounded' — it gives no number. A doctoral thesis written at the University of Vienna (Ernst Gramm, 2008) also gives, from the archive, the monetary value of the loss: 136,717 gulden, including the pay chest. So where do the '10,000 dead' come from? They are not in the academic literature. The oldest traceable source with a name attached is Paul Bernard's book Joseph II of 1968, and no source is cited there; the figure was popularised by a popular book on military blunders (Geoffrey Regan, 2000). Gramm calls this figure 'grossly exaggerated'. The often repeated explanation that 'the figure came from Gross-Hoffinger's history of Joseph II in the 19th century' was also tested: the full text of the 1847 edition of that book contains no casualty figure at all; the author passes over the event as 'an insignificant incident'.\n③ WHAT THE OTTOMAN SOURCES SAY. On the Ottoman side there is no trace of the panic story. According to the TDV, the Serasker (commander-in-chief) of Vidin, Kethüdâ Hasan Pasha, 'defeated the Austrian armies one after another at Mehadia and Şebeş', and Joseph II barely escaped with his life; the battles are dated to August-September 1788, with no day given. But the official chronicler Sâdullah Enverî, who was with the army that night, recorded the same hours: at the council that met in the tent of the serdârıekrem (grand vizier as commander-in-chief) on 21 September 1788, it was decided to attack the enemy headquarters; during the night, while preparations were under way, the Austrian soldiers were seen to be leaving their positions and withdrawing towards the town of Şebeş; the Ottoman units set off in pursuit and hemmed them in at the town; many soldiers died in the fire that broke out; the survivors took refuge in Lugoj (Logoş), seven hours away; and more than a thousand people were taken prisoner. In other words, the Ottoman record sees the movement, attributes its cause to its own pressure and writes the outcome as its own victory.\n④ WHERE THEY DIVERGE. The next day: the popular story says that the Ottomans arrived two days later and found the town empty; the Austrian campaign journal, however, writes that that same day sipahis and Janissaries attacked the rearguard, entered the town and burned most of it, and that the army withdrew to Lugoj on 24 September. On this point the two sides' records do not refute but complement each other: the Ottomans really did take Şebeş. Where they diverge is the cause of that night — the Austrian archive attributes the disintegration to its own panic, the Ottoman record to its own pursuit. What happened afterwards has also dropped out of the accounts: when news of the panic reached Lugoj, the town was plundered not by the enemy but by fleeing soldiers and the people of the surrounding area. The name 'battle' is also disputed: Austrian military historiography heads this event not as a battle but as a retreat. Finally, it has also been established that the event was understood the wrong way round in two major academic works: Stanford Shaw's book on Selim III and Isabel de Madariaga's book on Catherine II describe the same night as though it were an Ottoman surprise attack. For the end of the war: antlasma4-zistovi-1791.\n⑤ CLAIMS WITH NO SOURCE TO BE FOUND — HEARSAY. None of the following rests on any document: 10,000 dead · that the event took place on 17 September · that the Ottomans entered an empty town two days later · that Gypsies sold the brandy (the record says 'a Wallachian peasant') · that the Emperor fell from his horse and tumbled into a stream · that the command 'Halt!' was mistaken for a cry of 'Allah' (this detail is found only in the 19th-century account, not in the archival record).", "1f39f6f1"], "kisa": ["According to a story often told on the internet, in 1788 the Austrian army opened fire on itself at night, 10,000 soldiers died, and the Ottomans arrived two days later to find the town empty. The Austrian army's own campaign journal and Emperor Joseph II's letter confirm the panic; but the figure of 10,000 appears in no record, and the Ottomans arrived the next morning and took the town. The Ottoman sources describe the same night, make no mention of any panic and count the event as their own victory.", "9c46a948"]},
"karsi-karlofca-1699":{"baslik": ["The Treaty of Karlowitz: from the Habsburg, Venetian and Polish point of view", "b321e00e"], "metin": ["① The other side's contemporary account — The Habsburg court celebrated the victory with paintings and ceremonies; in 1712 the Battle of Zenta was put on canvas as part of a series of paintings commemorating the victories of Prince Eugene. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, Zenta (11 September 1697) aroused great excitement in the West and was regarded as an important success of the 'Holy League'. Yet the allies' own envoys did not leave the negotiating table satisfied. Marsigli, who took part in the talks on the Austrian side, wrote that the Christian delegates at times fell into a pitiable position vis-à-vis the Turks. The Venetian envoy Carlo Ruzzini was disappointed by the success of the defeated side at the table and put the blame on Austria, which had fallen out with France over the Spanish succession and for that reason made concessions. The terms of peace were not liked in Vienna, Warsaw and Venice; in Europe there even arose a rumour that the Christian delegates had been deceived.\n② MEMORY TODAY — In Austrian memory the Habsburg offensive after 1683 is narrated as an 'age of heroes' with Prince Eugene in the leading role; especially after the fall of the monarchy in 1918, this period was nostalgically turned into the 'great past'. Eugene was glorified as the 'vanquisher of the Turks' and the 'saviour of the West'; the song 'Prince Eugene, the noble knight' is still known. Present-day Austrian sources also add the bloody side to this picture: the capture of Buda in 1686 turned into a massacre, and thousands of Muslims and the city's Jewish community were killed. According to the same source, with Karlowitz the rule over Hungary and Transylvania that the Habsburgs had claimed since the 16th century became a reality. Britannica too describes the treaty as a peace that made Austria the dominant power of East-Central Europe; according to it, the Ottomans for the first time accepted negotiating with a European coalition, the mediation of neutral states and defeat.\n③ THE OTTOMAN ACCOUNT — The TDV regards Karlowitz as a turning point in Ottoman history, but its main emphasis is that the defeated delegation showed no weakness at the table. The chief plenipotentiary, the reisülküttab (chief secretary) Rami Mehmed Efendi, examined the texts of earlier treaties, obtained reports from experts and kept his composure in negotiations that ran to 36 sessions over 72 days; in this way he retained Temesvár and certain fortresses in Bosnia. Rami Efendi later described the negotiations in his work entitled 'Sulhname'; Silahdar Fındıklılı Mehmed Agha also recorded the treaty in detail in the 'Nusretname'. The second plenipotentiary, Alexander Mavrocordatos, dragoman (interpreter) of the Imperial Council, is on the other hand a controversial figure: Cantemir regards him as the architect of the peace; the emperor's conferring a decoration on him led to accusations that he had acted against Ottoman interests. The TDV says that this accusation should be treated with caution. (For the road to peace and its provisions: sebep-sonuc-karlofca-1699.)\n④ WHERE THEY DIVERGE — Result: the other side's summary is 'the Ottomans accepted defeat, and Austria became the dominant power'; the Ottoman account says that 'the side that lost the war held out at the table'. The contemporary witnesses of the two sides — Marsigli, Ruzzini and the discontent in Vienna, Warsaw and Venice — partly support this second interpretation. Numbers: the Ottoman soldiers killed at Zenta are put at around 30,000 in Austrian sources and at some 7-8,000 in certain Ottoman sources. Hero: on the other side, Prince Eugene and Emperor Leopold I; on the Ottoman side, Rami Mehmed Efendi and Amcazade Hüseyin Pasha; the suspect figure, for his part, is Mavrocordatos, the Ottoman side's own interpreter. The question of 'decline': older historiography regarded 1683-1699 as the beginning of the Ottoman 'decline'. Today many historians see these years as the start of a period in which the political structure underwent 'transformation rather than decline'; for the 17th century Suraiya Faroqhi uses the expression 'crisis and change'. This new approach is also being criticized: according to Olivier Bouquet, the 'transformation' approach has not entirely abandoned the habits of the older writing. (For the military dimension: avusturya-osmanli-askeri-geri-kalis-1683-1718.)", "ec93c6bf"], "kisa": ["On the Habsburg side, Karlowitz was remembered as the moment when Prince Eugene's victory at Zenta was crowned and the dynasty's two-century-old claim to Hungary became a reality. But Venice and Poland, which did not get what they expected at the table, were not satisfied with the treaty. Ottoman writing, for its part, tells how the defeated delegation resisted with dignity at the table. Modern scholarship, too, is increasingly abandoning the view of 1683-1699 as the beginning of a 'collapse'.", "7c27f5e5"]},
"karsi-kaynarca-1774":{"baslik": ["The Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca: through the eyes of Russian and Western historiography", "19949b15"], "metin": ["① The other side's contemporary account — In those years Russia was grappling with the Pugachev rebellion at home. According to the TDV's report, Catherine II expressed her satisfaction, saying that no such treaty had ever been concluded before, and found the terms better than could have been expected given Russia's straitened circumstances. As the date of the treaty, the Russian side took not the day of signature but 26 July, when the texts were exchanged. What really determined how the treaty was perceived was the official French translation that Russia had printed in 1775: in the Turkish text the church to be built in Istanbul was tied to the 'Russo-Greek' rite, whereas in the translation this church became 'a public church of the Greek rite'. For a long time Europe used this translation and the English version made from it.\n② LATER AND PRESENT-DAY MEMORY — In the 19th and 20th centuries many historians wrote that Articles 7 and 14 had given Russia the role of protector of the Ottoman Christians; some summed up the treaty with the phrase 'Russian skill and Turkish imbecility'. Russia's claim to protect the Ottoman Orthodox was among the causes of the Crimean War of 1853. In 1976 the historian Roderic Davison re-examined this interpretation against the documents and showed that the articles granted no such broad right. Victor Taki's study of 2015 likewise shows that the legal basis of the Russian 'protectorate' was very uneven: for a long time Russia's dealings were not with all the Orthodox but with particular groups in the restored territories (Wallachia and Moldavia, the Islands, Serbia). Today Russian historical institutions commemorate the treaty above all for Russia's access to the Black Sea and the separation of the Crimea from the Ottomans; the Crimea's independence, on the other hand, they describe as an independence 'on paper only'.\n③ THE OTTOMAN ACCOUNT — The treaty is related by the contemporary Ottoman historians Enverî, Vâsıf and Şem'dânîzâde, and by the chief plenipotentiary Ahmed Resmî Efendi. According to the first assessment, written in the besieged army camp at Shumen (Şumnu), the important articles were these: the cession of fortresses to Russia, the freedom of the Crimea, the free movement of ships and an indemnity of 15,000 purses. The church in Galata and the granting of the title 'padishah' to the Russian ruler were regarded as unimportant details. According to the TDV, while Article 3 made the Crimea independent, it also accepted the Tatars' religious allegiance to the caliph and the reading of the Friday sermon (hutbe) in the sultan's name. According to the TDV's article 'Hilâfet' (Caliphate), this treaty is the first official document in which a Western state recognised the Ottoman sultan as caliph of all Muslims; the same article stresses that it is wrong to read this as 'the first claim to the caliphate', because in the treaty he made in 1727 with the Afghan ruler Ashraf Khan, Ahmed III had already described himself as caliph of all Muslims. According to Halil İnalcık, however, this provision, which combined in a single article the Crimea's independence and its allegiance to the caliph, was contradictory from the outset and made the later annexation easier. (Stories told about the treaty: tartisma-kucuk-kaynarca-rivayetleri.)\n④ WHERE THEY DIVERGE — The church articles: the older Western and Russian reading finds a general right of protection; according to the TDV and Davison, Article 14 is limited to a single 'Russo-Greek' church to be built in Istanbul, and that church was never built. The Crimea: in the Russian account the Tatars were made 'free and independent'; according to İnalcık, since Kerch, Yenikale and Kinburn were ceded to Russia, this independence had no chance of surviving. The caliphate: in the West after the 19th century it was seen as an innovation and a claim; according to the TDV, what was new was not the title itself but its entry into an international treaty. The date: the Russian side gives 26 July, the Ottoman archival copies 16 July, Enverî and Vâsıf 17 July; the signature took place on 21 July 1774.", "a5919716"], "kisa": ["For Russia, Küçük Kaynarca was a peace won on better terms than expected at a time when the country itself was in difficulty. For a long time Western and Russian historiography read the treaty as a document giving Russia a right of protection over the Ottoman Christians. Present-day scholarship and the Ottoman side show that this reading arose from a later, expanded interpretation of the articles.", "fc8e463a"]},
"karsi-kirim-1783":{"baslik": ["Russia's annexation of the Crimea: through the eyes of the Russian Empire and the Crimean Tatars", "83f4c9b4"], "metin": ["① The other side's contemporary account (the Russian Empire) — In a letter to Catherine at the end of 1782, Potemkin said that the Crimea cut across Russia's borders, and argued that annexation would bring not wealth but tranquillity. In the manifesto dated 8 April 1783 (19 April in today's calendar), Catherine explained the following: Russia could have kept hold of the Crimea in the last war, but for the sake of peace with the Ottomans it had given up this right and made the Tatars a free and independent people. The manifesto promised the inhabitants of the peninsula that they would be treated as equals of Russian subjects, and that their lives, property, places of worship and faith would be protected. In a letter to the Austrian emperor Joseph II, Catherine linked her justification to the Ottomans' sending troops to Taman. The manifesto was kept secret for months; the oath of allegiance of the Crimean notables did not take place until the summer of 1783.\n② PRESENT-DAY MEMORY — In Russia, in 2018, the State Duma added 19 April to the official days of commemoration as 'the Day of the admission of the Crimea, Taman and the Kuban into the Russian Empire'; the explanatory text of the law states that this day will show the historical justness of the 2014 referendum. Russian historical institutions describe the lists of oaths collected by Potemkin as 'a kind of 18th-century referendum'. The Representative Office of the President of Ukraine in the Crimea, in a text it prepared together with the Ukrainian Institute of National Memory, draws the picture the other way round: the Crimea was an independent state and was annexed by force; this was part of the same imperial policy as the abolition of the Zaporozhian Sich in 1775. In Crimean Tatar memory, 1783 is the beginning of the 'Black Century' and of the great waves of migration. According to the historian Hakan Kırımlı, at least 1,800,000 Crimean Tatars migrated to Ottoman lands between 1783 and 1922. To the new administrative region it established in February 1784, Russia gave not the name 'Crimea' but the name 'Taurida'.\n③ THE OTTOMAN ACCOUNT — According to Halil İnalcık, the people of the Crimea, who rose against Şahin Giray, sent petitions to the sultan in 1782; the Russian army then occupied the peninsula and the Crimea became a province of the tsardom. The TDV's article on Abdülhamid I adds that after 1783 the caliph's name ceased to be recited in the Friday sermon in the mosques of the Crimea. According to Nihat Çelik's study, based on the Ottoman archives, Crimean delegations came to Istanbul in the summer of 1782 and asked for help; as late as mid-June 1783 the Sublime Porte still did not know of the annexation. In the consultative councils convened in November-December 1783, most statesmen chose to remain silent in the face of the heavy responsibility. Because the Ottoman government shrank from a two-front war against Austria and Russia, in January 1784 it signed the 'Crimea Deed' (Kırım Senedi) recognising the annexation; the Kuban river became the border. The government legitimised this decision by the principles of Islamic law concerning a peace made when there is an imbalance of power, and presented the situation as a temporary state to be corrected once strength had been regained. According to İnalcık, from the Ottoman point of view the aim of the new war that began in 1787 was to recover the Crimea. (The place of the Khanate in Tsarist and Soviet historiography: tartisma-kirimrus-carlik-sovyet; the Ukrainian, Crimean Tatar and Turkish perspectives: tartisma-kirimrus-ukrayna-kirimtatar-turk.)\n④ WHERE THEY DIVERGE — Consent or force: the Russian account has lists of oaths and a peaceful acceptance; the TDV, by contrast, has military occupation and a great slaughter. The TDV writes that Potemkin had 30,000 Crimeans killed, but it bases this figure on a single 19th-century English historian (Howorth), and the figure could not be confirmed by any other academic source. The cause: Catherine links the intervention to the Ottomans' sending troops to Taman; the TDV and Çelik, on the other hand, take as the basis the Russian military presence that had continued since Kaynarca and Şahin Giray, whom Russia supported. The name of the outcome: in Russia, 'admission' and 'union'; in Ukrainian and Crimean Tatar memory, 'annexation' and the beginning of the 'Black Century'; among the Ottomans, a loss that was recognised out of necessity but considered temporary. The date: at that time Russia used the old (Julian) calendar, which in the 18th century was 11 days behind today's. The date of the manifesto is 8 April 1783 Old Style, 19 April New Style. The day of the deed of recognition appears in Ottoman sources as 8 or 9 January 1784.", "58a5dd76"], "kisa": ["Catherine II's manifesto presented the annexation as a measure taken against the disruption, by Ottoman intervention, of the freedom granted to the Tatars. Today Russia counts 19 April as an official day of commemoration, while Ukrainian and Crimean Tatar memory recalls the same day as the beginning of a forcible annexation and of the great migrations. Because they could not risk war, the Ottomans recognised the annexation in January 1784 and saw this as a temporary necessity.", "02f717af"]},
"karsi-kosova-1389":{"baslik": ["The First Battle of Kosovo: from the Serbian point of view", "94fcdb11"], "metin": ["① The other side's contemporary account — The news that spread to the West immediately after the battle highlighted a single event: the death of the sultan. On 1 August 1389 King Tvrtko of Bosnia announced in a letter to the city of Trogir that he had defeated the infidel; news of a victory also reached Florence. The eulogistic texts written in church circles about Lazar presented his death as a spiritual victory won with the crown of martyrdom. Whether the expression 'glorious victory' in these texts describes a real military victory or only a spiritual one is disputed among Serbian historians. A Serbian chronicle written at Peć says that its author does not know whether Lazar was betrayed by one of his own men or whether God's judgment was fulfilled; this is the first trace of betrayal in the sources. A Dubrovnik chronicle written at the end of the 15th century records that the losses were very heavy and that neither of the two sides was able to win. Constantine the Philosopher, who wrote the biography of Lazar's son, openly acknowledges the defeat. The first author to name the man who killed the sultan is Konstantin Mihailović, writing around 1497.\n② MEMORY TODAY — In the folk epics collected by Vuk Karadžić in the 19th century, Lazar is offered a choice: an earthly kingdom or a heavenly kingdom. Lazar chooses the heavenly kingdom; thus a military defeat becomes a moral victory. According to the epic, Miloš Obilić, accused of being a traitor on the evening before the battle, kills the sultan to prove his loyalty; the traitor is Vuk Branković, who comes out of the battle alive. Vidovdan (St Vitus' Day), the anniversary of the battle, became linked with many turning points in Serbian history: the comrades of the man who carried out the Sarajevo assassination of 28 June 1914 saw him as 'the Miloš of our day'; on 28 June 1989 Slobodan Milošević addressed a huge crowd on the plain of Kosovo. Modern scholarship criticizes this myth. According to the historian Dejan Djokić, the identity of the sultan's killer cannot be established, and it is very unlikely that Branković was a traitor. In the 19th century Ilarion Ruvarac and Ljubomir Kovačević showed that there was no evidence of betrayal; this debate played a role in the birth of critical historiography in Serbia. According to the historian Thomas Emmert, the medieval Serbian state had already disintegrated long before the battle, and the idea of a 'great Serbian defeat' cannot be supported by contemporary sources; Kosovo is more important in legend than for its military outcome.\n③ THE OTTOMAN ACCOUNT — On the Ottoman side the first to narrate the battle are Ahmedi and Şükrullah; the detailed account is in Neşri. In this account Lazar is a vassal who defies his master, and the war is his punishment. According to Neşri, on the night before the battle Murad prays to be sacrificed for the faith; after the battle is won, while walking among the corpses, he is stabbed by Miloš, who approaches as if wounded, and dies a martyr. Bayezid ascends the throne in the camp. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, decisive military success belonged to the Ottomans; the TDV finds Neşri more useful than the Serbian sources, which are 'smothered in legends', but notes that he too confuses the course of the battle.\n④ WHERE THEY DIVERGE — Outcome: in Ottoman sources a clear victory; in some Serbian sources a victory, and in the more cautious ones a battle of uncertain outcome. Emmert points out that the early Ottoman sources do not magnify the victory either: because of the death of the leader and the army's immediate return, the victory is 'at best a Pyrrhic victory'; in his view the account of a 'clear Serbian defeat' passed to the West later, largely by way of Neşri. Murad's death: in the Ottoman account it is the ruse of a man who approaches as if wounded; in the Serbian and Western accounts it is a heroic attack planned in advance. The TDV finds this second account unconvincing. The figure of the traitor: the traitor of the Serbian epic is Branković; the TDV, relying on a Catalan source, finds this allegation meaningless. Numbers: according to the TDV, Ottoman sources greatly exaggerate the size of the army; the two armies were probably evenly matched, and each slightly exceeded 30,000. Date: the 15 June of the sources follows the old (Julian) calendar; today the Serbs commemorate the battle on 28 June, on Vidovdan. (For the course of the battle: savas-kosova-1389.)", "fefe5311"], "kisa": ["In the Ottoman chronicles, Kosovo is a victory in which a rebellious vassal was punished and the sultan died a martyr. In Serbian memory, it is a sacred defeat in which Knez Lazar chose the heavenly kingdom over the earthly one; Miloš is remembered as a hero and Vuk Branković as a traitor. Modern historians, for their part, stress that the contemporary sources do not say clearly who won.", "74082888"]},
"karsi-mohac-1526":{"baslik": ["The Battle of Mohács: from the Hungarian point of view", "753997f1"], "metin": ["① The other side's contemporary account — The most important Hungarian witness of the event is István Brodarics. He was the king's chancellor, took part in the battle and survived. His work describing the battle agrees in its main lines with the account from the Ottoman side; but Brodarics had a further aim. The Viennese humanist Cuspinianus, in order to support the Habsburg Ferdinand's claim to the throne, had accused the Hungarian nobles of failing to defend the country and even of leaving the king in the lurch; Brodarics defended the Hungarian nobles and Szapolyai against these accusations. A scene he reports shows that memory was being shaped even before the battle: at the council of war the young bishop Ferenc Perényi had said in advance that the day of the battle would be dedicated to 'twenty thousand Hungarian martyrs who died for Christianity'. Chroniclers such as György Szerémi, on the other hand, regarded the catastrophe as a punishment from God; according to them, the blame lay with the great men who ignored the Turkish danger, robbed the church and spent the money on amusements. All the sources blame the commander-in-chief, Tomori, to some extent. Because of the contest for the throne that broke out between two kings after the battle, the narrative was intertwined with politics from the very beginning: the claim that Szapolyai had deliberately arrived late for the battle was either repeated or rejected according to which king the writer sided with.\n② MEMORY TODAY — Mohács is one of the most powerful sites of memory in Hungarian history and is popularly known as 'mohácsi vész' (the disaster of Mohács). In the 18th century laments were sung under the name 'Song of Mohács'; in 1830 the reforming statesman István Széchenyi mocked those who only looked to the past by referring to this song. The saying 'Több is veszett Mohácsnál' (More than this was lost at Mohács) became widespread through the refrain of a poem by János Arany: in the poem a horse's shoe has fallen off, and the refrain makes light of this small loss by comparing it with Mohács. On the 400th anniversary of the battle, in 1926, 29 August was celebrated at Mohács as a national day of remembrance, and Mohács was read side by side with the wound opened by the Treaty of Trianon, which ended the First World War. In the same period the legend of 'the West's betrayal' grew stronger: according to this legend, it was the French king's appeal for help that brought the Turks to Hungary. Modern Hungarian historiography rejects this legend almost unanimously. The cliché that the Jagiellonian kings who came after King Matthias drove the country to collapse is also being criticized: historians associated with the Hungarian Academy of Sciences have shown that this cliché was born of a pattern of 'decline' that 16th-century humanist historians took from Rome. In an interview in 2026 the historian Géza Pálffy said that there was no need to look for culprits: in his view, a Christian army of 26-27 thousand had no chance of lasting resistance against an Ottoman army of 75-80 thousand. Pálffy also recalls that the account of the king drowning in the Csele stream rests on a single reliable witness.\n③ THE OTTOMAN ACCOUNT — On the Ottoman side the campaign is a story of victory. The campaign diary (ruzname) and the proclamation of victory (fetihname) record the events day by day; Kemalpaşazade's Mohaçname, Matrakçı Nasuh, Celalzade and Lutfi Pasha narrate the battle in detail. At the centre of this account is a plan prepared in advance: the troops of Rumelia open out to both sides, the Hungarian heavy cavalry is drawn in front of the chained cannon and the Janissary musketeers, and the akıncı raiders envelop them from behind. Ottoman sources also acknowledge that had the Hungarian army met the Ottoman army in the marshes of the Drava, it could have turned the situation to its own advantage.\n④ WHERE THEY DIVERGE — Numbers: according to the modern summary in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, at the time of the battle the Hungarian army numbered 40-50 thousand and the Ottoman army about 80 thousand; Pálffy gives the Hungarian army as 26-27 thousand. Brodarics reports that Tomori put the Ottoman army at 300 thousand in his report; at the end of the 16th century the Hungarian historian Istvánffy lowered this figure to 150 thousand. Losses: according to the Ottoman campaign diary, 20 thousand infantry and 4 thousand cavalry corpses were counted during burial; Pálffy gives the army's losses as about 16 thousand. Cause: according to the TDV, Hungarian historiography attributes the defeat to the fact that the left wing, which had broken through the Ottoman lines, turned to plundering, and takes no account of the plan the Ottomans had made in advance; present-day Hungarian scholarship, by contrast, emphasizes the imbalance between the two powers. Hungarian historians also criticize the exaggerated figures in Turkish historiography: Uzunçarşılı puts the Ottoman army at 300 thousand and the Hungarian army at more than 150 thousand. Result: in Hungarian memory Mohács is the end of the kingdom; assessments on the Ottoman side, however, stress that Mohács meant not the immediate seizure of Hungary but the preparation of its collapse. The Ottoman period in Buda did not begin until 1541. (For the course of the battle: savas-mohac-1526; for the durations of conquest and loss: tartisma-macaristan-fetih-kayip-sureleri.)", "bc765439"], "kisa": ["For the Ottomans, Mohács is a brilliant victory won in two hours. For the Hungarians, it is a national catastrophe whose 'culprit' they have been seeking for five hundred years. Present-day Hungarian scholarship criticizes this very search for a culprit.", "f504476b"]},
"karsi-nigbolu-1396":{"baslik": ["The Battle of Nicopolis: from the point of view of the crusaders and the Hungarians", "dbc9f0a8"], "metin": ["① The other side's contemporary account — In the West many works were written immediately after the rout. In 1397 Philippe de Mézières composed for the duke of Burgundy a 'letter of lamentation' devoted entirely to this defeat. In his view the real cause of the rout was the collapse of order, discipline, obedience and justice in the crusader army; although he used contemptuous words about the Turks, he held up their military order and discipline as an example. Michel Pintoin, the monk of Saint-Denis, wrote that he would have liked to pass over the event in silence, and then offered a moralizing interpretation that attributed the defeat to the army's sins, such as adultery, debauchery and blasphemy. At the centre of Froissart's account is the recklessness of the young French-Burgundian knights who did not heed the Hungarian king's wise advice about how to fight. The anonymous author who wrote the biography of Marshal Boucicaut, by contrast, defended his hero against these accusations. Johann Schiltberger, a sixteen-year-old Bavarian taken prisoner in the battle, gives other details: the son of the duke of Burgundy had refused to leave the honour of the first charge to the Hungarians; King Sigismund fled when his banner fell and escaped on a ship; on the day after the battle many prisoners were killed, while those under twenty years of age were spared. Schiltberger puts the Ottoman army at 200,000 and Sigismund's army at 16,000 men.\n② MEMORY TODAY — At the Burgundian court Nicopolis was remembered throughout the 15th century as an important political event. Jean de Nevers, who had been taken prisoner and freed by ransom, later became duke of Burgundy under the name John the Fearless; for his son Philip the Good, avenging his father's captivity was part of the rhetoric of the court. The defeat turned into a literary legend that fed the image of the 'invincible and barbarous Turk' in Europe; the story of a Flemish knight who returned from captivity to find his wife married to another even found its way into a court collection of tales. Present-day researchers (Kaçar and Dumolyn) point out that these accounts give very little information about the Turks: these texts were written mainly to criticize French chivalry and to legitimize Burgundy's crusading claim.\n③ THE OTTOMAN ACCOUNT — In Ottoman historiography Nicopolis occupies little space. The earliest Ottoman sources give no details about the phases of the battle; some place the event at another date or confuse it with another Hungarian campaign. The only witness who saw the battle and whose writing has survived is Ibn al-Jazari: he estimates that although the number of crusaders was said to be 200-400 thousand, it was in reality around 30,000. Oruç Bey, Aşıkpaşazade, Neşri and Hoca Sadeddin narrate the battle within an opposition between 'infidels' and the 'army of Islam', often in a single paragraph; Aşıkpaşazade does not mention the Western European contingents at all. Present-day Turkish research puts the Ottoman army at between 60 and 80 thousand and stresses that the victory won Bayezid great fame in the Islamic world and consolidated Ottoman rule along the Danube.\n④ WHERE THEY DIVERGE — Importance: in the West a catastrophe and a long-lasting trauma; in the Ottoman chronicles a minor victory beside the great upheaval experienced with Timur. Numbers: Western writers put the Ottoman army at 200-400 thousand; Turkish research does not accept these figures and estimates the army at under 80 thousand; Ibn al-Jazari, for his part, reduces the number of crusaders. The killing of the prisoners: Western chroniclers describe it with horror; some accounts explain it as retaliation for the killings the crusaders had committed along their route; the classical Ottoman chronicles do not mention this event. Hero and culprit: in the West the culprits are the arrogant French knights, while Froissart's hero is Enguerrand de Coucy; on the Ottoman side what stands out is the courage of Bayezid I the Thunderbolt and his knowledge of the terrain. Date: by the Ottoman reckoning 21 Dhu al-Hijja 798, that is, 25 September 1396; the 19th-century English translation of Schiltberger, however, says 28 September. (For the course of the battle: savas-nigbolu-1396.)", "c6d96233"], "kisa": ["In France and Burgundy, Nicopolis was written about as a great catastrophe; the cause of the rout was sought in the arrogance, indiscipline and sins of the crusader knights. In the Ottoman chronicles, by contrast, the same battle was narrated as a brief victory overshadowed by the struggle with Timur.", "67a4a7eb"]},
"karsi-viyana-1683":{"baslik": ["The Second Siege of Vienna and the Kahlenberg: from the point of view of Austria, Poland and Western Europe", "998f2e7e"], "metin": ["① The other side's contemporary account — The day after the battle (13 September 1683), King John III Sobieski of Poland wrote a letter to Pope Innocent XI in the vizier's tents. The opening of the letter was adapted from Caesar's famous saying: \"We came, we saw, God conquered\" (Venimus, vidimus, Deus vicit). Sobieski ascribed the victory to God, but in the rest of the letter he put himself forward as commander-in-chief of the allied armies. Along with the letter he also sent to Rome the vizier's captured standard. The letter was translated into many languages and circulated. In Rome bells were rung and thanksgiving masses held; the victory was celebrated as the joint work of the pope's prayers and financial support and the king's sword. The Habsburg side's framing is the same: in 1684 the pope, the emperor, Poland and Venice united in the 'Holy League', and the Habsburg offensive began.\n② MEMORY TODAY — In Vienna the siege was for centuries commemorated as a victory won against the Turk, the 'Erbfeind' (hereditary enemy). At the 200th anniversary commemoration in 1883 the Habsburg monarchy was celebrated as the 'bulwark of the Christian West'; in the same year a bitter dispute broke out between the city administration and the Church over who could claim the victory. The 250th anniversary commemoration in 1933 was held together with the Catholic Congress; at these ceremonies Chancellor Dollfuß proclaimed the goal of an authoritarian, Christian and German Austrian state, and that year Sobieski and the Poles remained in the background. On the 300th anniversary in 1983 the emphasis shifted back to Sobieski: a plaque describing him as the 'saviour of Christendom' was placed in the church on the Kahlenberg, and Pope John Paul II visited Vienna. The historian Peter Rauscher thinks that this emphasis by the pope may also have carried a message directed against the Soviet bloc. Some intellectuals, for their part, criticized the ceremonies, saying that celebrating a war was incompatible with the values of peace. In recent times the memory of 1683 has also been used by anti-immigration far-right groups. Some stories connected with the siege are regarded as legends in academic research: the claim that Vienna's first coffee house was opened by the courier Kolschitzky was first published by a priest in 1783; the story that the crescent-shaped 'Kipferl' pastry was invented to mock the Turks appears in print only in the mid-19th century, whereas this kind of pastry had been known since the 13th century.\n③ THE OTTOMAN ACCOUNT — Ottoman sources single out the grand vizier Merzifonlu Kara Mustafa Pasha as the person responsible for the rout. The army's real objective was Yanıkkale (Győr); at the council in İstolni Belgrad (Székesfehérvár) it was decided to besiege Vienna, which would bring the pasha greater fame. The chronicler Silahdar Fındıklılı Mehmed Agha, who took part in the siege in person, writes that Mehmed IV was surprised at this decision and records that the pasha's rivals at the palace rejoiced at the news of the rout. The pasha was executed in Belgrade on 25 December 1683. The chronicler Raşid reports an opinion that so powerful a vizier should not have been put to death. Dimitrie Cantemir wrote that the pasha wanted to become German emperor; the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam finds this claim baseless. Before the rout, Vienna was in the Ottoman world a 'Red Apple' about which legends were told; after the defeat the discourse about this city lost its force and began to fade into oblivion.\n④ WHERE THEY DIVERGE — Numbers: a figure of 500,000 is reported for the Ottoman army, which the TDV regards as exaggerated; an Austrian source writes that there were 'at most 200,000' soldiers before the city. The relief army is also given differently: the TDV puts Sobieski's force at 100,000, while the Austrian source puts the entire relief army at 70,000 men. Hero: in Polish memory, Sobieski; in the Austria of 1883 and 1933, Starhemberg, the Duke of Lorraine, the Church and the 'German' defenders; in 1983, Sobieski again. In the Ottoman account, by contrast, there are culprits rather than a hero: Kara Mustafa, the Crimean khan Murad Giray, who remained indifferent, and İbrahim Pasha, governor of Buda, who was the first to give way. Framing: the other side narrates the event as the deliverance of Christendom with God's help, while the Ottoman side narrates it as an error of leadership and an internal feud. A shared trace: Vienna turned the memory of this victory into a 'Turkish heritage' extending to standards, pastries and even skulls; according to the TDV, in the occupied places Ottoman graves were opened and skulls carried off as trophies of victory. One of these was attributed to Kara Mustafa Pasha and kept in a Vienna museum; whose it was is disputed. (For the military causes of the rout: tartisma-2viyana-bozgun-sebepleri; for the sixteen-year course of the war: savas-viyana-karlofca-1683-1699.)", "93d7a5cd"], "kisa": ["In Austria and Poland, 12 September 1683 was narrated as the day on which Christian Europe was saved with God's help, and this narrative was reconstructed in the commemorations of 1883, 1933 and 1983 according to the politics of each period. On the Ottoman side, the rout was written as the story of a vizier's personal ambition and of palace intrigues. Vienna itself soon began to fade into oblivion in the Ottoman mind.", "9bd6c71d"]},
"kiklad-adalari-1566-1830":{"sebep.b": ["The abolition of the Duchy of Naxos and the incorporation of the Cyclades into Ottoman administration", "63cac9ea"], "sonuc.b": ["The recognition of Greek independence by the London Protocol and the passing of the islands out of Ottoman administration", "e4c60e4e"], "bag": ["Throughout the Ottoman period, the status of the islands was not so much direct provincial administration as a tax arrangement attached to the Province of the Kapudan Pasha, with broad internal freedom; the overwhelming majority of the population remained Greek Orthodox, and the island councils themselves ran local affairs. This continuity made it easier for the islands to enter quickly into the same political framework as the mainland once the independence movement began.", "ee56c5c3"], "metin": ["The Cycladic archipelago was held by the Duchy of Naxos, of Venetian origin, founded after the Fourth Crusade; with the abolition of the duchy, the islands passed to Ottoman administration. In the Ottoman system the islands were attached to the Province of the Kapudan Pasha, and in return for a lump-sum tax they were granted broad freedom in their internal affairs — apart from the jizya and the mukataa revenues, the centre intervened little in daily life. During the Greek independence movement, the protocols of Saint Petersburg (1826) and London (1827) first envisaged an autonomous Greek principality; the London Protocol of 1830 then recognised independence. The first core of the new state was the Morea and Central Greece, and the Cyclades were also included within this core; Thessaly, Epirus, Crete and the northern islands of the Aegean joined through later wars and treaties, in some cases a century later.", "7ae3a6d6"]},
"kimdir-abdulhamid-ii-sahsiyet":{"baslik": ["The personality of Abdülhamid II — a sultan who listened much and spoke little", "793012c4"], "metin": ["■ Appearance and manner\nHe had a large nose and bright, large eyes; he tended to lean slightly forward when walking and sitting. He had a trembling but deep voice; he listened much and spoke little. He inspired respect in those who spoke with him, treated everyone courteously, showed a smiling face even to people he disliked and did not let it be seen that he did not care for them. He was adept at sensing the feelings and thoughts of the person before him.\n\n■ MIND\nHe had an extraordinary intelligence and memory; he never forgot anyone whom he had seen once or whose voice he had heard. His will was strong, he was independent in his ideas and decisions and steadfast in the face of danger.\n\n■ WAY OF LIFE\nThe fact that his mother and father had died of tuberculosis had led him from a young age to live cautiously. He did not drink alcohol, kept away from every kind of dissipation and lived a simple life; until his death he made it a habit to shower with lukewarm water every morning. He was fond of gymnastics and skilled in the use of sword and pistol. He enjoyed Western music, opera and theatre. He liked to work and followed a regular programme; he held state affairs above everything and wished to be woken from his sleep when important news arrived.\n\n■ STYLE OF RULE\nHe knew how to make use of people of different characters in state affairs and gave them duties suited to their temperaments. Before deciding on important questions he took the views of statesmen of differing opinions, sometimes even had those of opposing views debate in his presence, and then gave his final decision. In decisions that carried responsibility he referred the matter to an assembly and saw to it that the decision came from there — in other words, he took care not to shoulder the responsibility alone.\n\n■ SUSPICIOUSNESS\nHe had a cool-headed but suspicious temperament, and this is the centre of the portrait. The fact that the two sultans before him had been deposed had turned into a FIXED IDEA in him the suspicion that he too would be deposed. The two separate attempts to storm the palace in 1878 increased this suspicion all the more. This is why he set up a powerful organisation of secret agents in order to be informed of everything that happened in the state. In his own words, informing was a shameful and evil thing, but it was also impossible to give it up.\n\n■ FINANCIAL ATTITUDE\nThroughout his reign he always behaved economically. Unlike his predecessor, he did not lay hands on the state treasury; on the contrary, he made sacrifices from his own purse and cut the palace's expenses to the utmost. He led a simple life, far removed from a palace life filled with concubines.\n\n■ NOTE TO THE READER\nThis portrait is the image, drawn from ONE source, of the most debated Ottoman sultan, and its positive side predominates. The opposing reading is given on a separate card; no judgement should be formed without reading the two together.", "188da91c"], "kisa": ["A man who listened much, spoke little and never forgot a face he had seen once — and the same man, a suspicious mind that turned the fear of being deposed into a fixed idea.", "4b5f7161"]},
"kimdir-ahmed-cevdet-pasa":{"oneri": ["the Mecelle (the Ottoman civil code, the first compilation of its kind among Islamic states)", "48d0252f"], "ad": ["Who was Ahmed Cevdet Pasha? — a statesman who both wrote history and made laws", "e36e88f1"], "metin": ["He was born in 1239 (1823) in Lofça (Lovech) in present-day Bulgaria and died in 1312 (1895). In Istanbul he received a rigorous madrasa and language education and studied with the leading scholars of the day. While holding posts such as judge, inspector, governor and minister of justice, he also maintained an intense scholarly output.\n\nHis best-known work is the Tarih-i Cevdet — a twelve-volume history narrating the events from the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca in 1774 to the abolition of the Janissary corps in 1826. Unlike classical Ottoman chronicle writing, it also treats European history extensively and uses an analytical method, influenced by Ibn Khaldun's philosophy of history, that examines institutional collapse and the causal links between events; for its time, it favours a plain style.\n\nBeyond historiography, his most lasting legacy lies in the field of law: he led the preparation of the Mecelle (the Ottoman civil code, the first compilation of its kind among Islamic states); opposing the wholesale adoption of French law, he argued for adapting the native body of Islamic jurisprudence to contemporary needs. He also founded modern judicial institutions such as the Divan-ı Ahkâm-ı Adliyye (court of cassation) and the Mekteb-i Hukuk (law school) — he radically reshaped the Ottoman legal infrastructure.\n\nAccording to the TDV, he built a bridge between classical and modern Ottoman historiography: he combined the traditional narrative form with the systematic evaluation of sources and comparative analysis; through educational reforms and linguistic studies he also influenced Turkish intellectual life.", "9d15cbf2"], "kisa": ["The man who came out of a small town in Bulgaria and wrote the Ottomans' first civil code was also the empire's last great chronicler.", "ed4aeef1"]},
"kimdir-ali-suavi-ve-ciragan-baskini":{"baslik": ["Who was Ali Suavi? The Çırağan Raid (1878)", "f561037e"], "metin": ["■ Who he was\nAli Suavi (1839-1878) was a journalist, a thinker and a member of the Young Ottomans Society; he is counted among the first Turkists. He championed the idea of consultation and a parliament in place of despotism, was devoted to Islamic unity, and wanted the Turkish language to be simplified. He wrote in Muhbir and published the newspaper Ulûm. In his own words, he had spoiled the language and brought freedom of the press into the country.\n\n■ 20 MAY 1878: ÇIRAĞAN\nWith about 250 people, most of them refugees from the Balkans, Ali Suavi attempted to storm the Çırağan Palace. The apparent aim was to make the deposed Murad V sultan again. The raid quickly broke up when an unprepared crowd came face to face with the palace guards.\n\n■ HOW HE DIED\nAli Suavi was killed when Hasan Agha, the officer of the Beşiktaş police station, struck him on the head with a club. In popular memory this man is remembered by the nickname “Yedi Sekiz Hasan Pasha” (Seven-Eight Hasan Pasha), and the story is customarily told under that nickname; however, no separate encyclopedia entry on a person bearing this nickname could be found. What is told in this card is the part written in the source: the man who killed him was the police station officer, and the killing was done with a club, during the raid.\n\n■ WHY IT MATTERS\nThe Çırağan Raid is one of the rare moments when the constitutionalist opposition spilled over from the realm of ideas into the realm of action, and its outcome was severe: in the atmosphere of 1878, when the parliament was suspended and the opposition dispersed, the incident strengthened the hand of the government and in practice closed off the possibility of restoring Murad V to the throne.", "032f7749"], "kisa": ["Journalist, teacher, exile, school principal, and the man who one morning stormed a palace with two hundred and fifty people. Ali Suavi's end was very far from where he had begun.", "ca989e94"]},
"kimdir-celali-reisleri":{"oneri": ["Celal (1519)", "f6855d6d"], "ad": ["Who were the great Celali chiefs, and when did they rise in revolt?", "ec99ec53"], "metin": ["• Sheikh Celal of Bozok (1519) — proclaimed himself the Mahdi around Tokat; his revolt was harshly suppressed, and his name was left to the whole movement.\n• Karayazıcı Abdülhalim (1599-1602) — from the Kılıçlı tribe in Urfa; he was called 'Karayazıcı' because he had served as a clerk (yazıcı) of the sekbans. In November 1599 he took the citadel of Urfa and sent out decrees bearing the tughra of 'Halim Shah'. When he was promised a sanjak, he handed over Hüseyin Pasha, who was with him (Hüseyin Pasha was executed in Istanbul on 7 February 1600). On 23 September 1600 he defeated government forces on the plain of Kayseri; on 12 August 1601 he was defeated by Sokulluzade Hasan Pasha at Sepetli near Elbistan; news of his death reached Istanbul in February-March 1602.\n• Deli Hasan (1602-1603) — Karayazıcı's brother. On 20 April 1602 he had Sokulluzade Hasan Pasha, the commander-in-chief against the Celalis, killed at Tokat; he overran Kütahya and marched on Afyonkarahisar; in March 1603 he asked for pardon and was made beylerbey of Bosnia.\n• Tavil Ahmed (1604-1607) — rose from the ranks of the sekbans; he defeated Nasuh Pasha, the commander-in-chief against the Celalis. Although he was given the beylerbeylik of Shahrizor in 1605, he continued his revolt and took Harput; his son Mehmed seized the governorship of Baghdad with a forged firman, and Baghdad was recovered only in 1607.\n• Canbolatoğlu Ali Pasha (1606-1607) — sanjak governor of Kilis. He rebelled after his uncle Hüseyin Pasha was executed by Cigalazade in 1604; he took Aleppo, had the Friday sermon read and coins struck in his own name, and made contact with Tuscany. On 24 October 1607 he was defeated by Kuyucu Murad Pasha on the plain of Oruç.\n• Kalenderoğlu Mehmed (1604-1608) — plundered the districts of Manisa, Aydın and Bursa; the townspeople did not let him into the sanjak of Ankara given to him in 1607. He was defeated on 9 August 1608 at the Göksun Pass (Alaçayır), fled to Iran and was never heard of again.\n• The later generation — Abaza Mehmed Pasha, beylerbey of Erzurum, who took up the cause of avenging the blood of Osman II; Varvar Ali Pasha, governor of Sivas in the reign of Sultan İbrahim; Kara Haydar, who held up the Isparta-Uluborlu road, and his son Haydaroğlu; Gürcü Abdünnebi and Katırcıoğlu Mehmed (dispersed at Üsküdar-Bulgurlu on 7 July 1649); and Abaza Hasan Pasha, whom Köprülü Mehmed Pasha suppressed with difficulty.", "50dbcca6"], "kisa": ["From a sheikh to a clerk of the sekbans, from a bey of Kilis to a muleteer: the rebels who filled a century, only as far as the sources date them.", "a2a71098"], "not": ["Most of the dates are at the level of the year or month; a day has been written only where the source gives one. The sources are divided between 23 and 24 October 1607 as the day of the battle on the plain of Oruç (the fighting began on the 23rd, and the main battle took place the next day). Kalenderoğlu's defeat appears in one TDV article only as 'August 1608' and in another as 9 August 1608.", "4fd12094"]},
"kimdir-cevdet-pasa-sahsiyet":{"baslik": ["Who was Ahmed Cevdet Pasha? The architect of the Mecelle", "496b00fa"], "metin": ["■ The shape of the mind\nCevdet Pasha was a figure who tried to reach a synthesis between the traditional Turkish-Islamic culture of the East and the innovative West. While still a young madrasa student, he attracted his teachers' attention with his extraordinary intelligence, diligence and accurate analyses, and from time to time engaged them in debates on scholarly questions. He came to Istanbul in the year the Tanzimat was proclaimed and soon became known in scholarly circles.\n\n■ THE BASIC DISTINCTION: METHOD YES, IMITATION NO\nThe axis of his thought was this: Ottoman institutions rest on Islamic foundations; the Ottoman state and the Western states were born of different religions and civilisations; therefore Westernisation in every respect is both wrong and impossible. On the other hand, he accepted the West's superiority in the positive sciences, technology and administration, and advocated reforming Ottoman institutions in these fields along Western lines. So his distinction was not \"East-West\" but \"method-content\".\n\n■ THE MECELLE IS THE PRODUCT OF THIS ATTITUDE\nWhile some statesmen advocated translating and adopting French laws, Cevdet Pasha opposed this and said that Islamic traditions should be preserved. His playing the most important role in preparing the Mecelle (the Ottoman civil code) was the direct result of this objection: he did not stop at objecting but produced an alternative.\n\n■ THE LANGUAGE QUESTION\nOne of his aims in education was to make Turkish a language of science. His writing of his twelve-volume history in a language that was plain for its time is a result of this attitude. He prepared Turkish textbooks according to modern methods for use in schools, and had a treatise printed in answer to those who claimed that Turkish could not be a language of scholarship. He contributed greatly to the establishment of the Encümen-i Dâniş (Academy of Sciences), the regulation of the teachers' training college was drawn up while he was its director, and the first idadi (secondary school) in Istanbul was opened while he was Minister of Education.\n\n■ HIS UNDERSTANDING OF NATION AND HOMELAND\nHis understanding of the nation rests on the basis of Ottomanism, which represents the political unity of the Muslim nations. For nationality he uses the term \"kavmiyet\", and says that after the French Revolution it spread through Europe like a contagious disease. He is also conservative on the idea of homeland: he argues that the concept of homeland cannot find the favour among the Muslim people that it has in Europe, and that religion would be more effective in its place.\n\n■ A MAN WHO WROTE HIS OWN WORK ACCORDING TO HIS AUDIENCE\nThere is a subtle detail that shows his personality: a part of his memoirs was written in accordance with the wishes of the sultan of the time and in a language suited to his temperament; it differs from his other work in that it even gives room to gossip here and there. In other words, the historian wrote knowing for whom he was writing — something to be taken into account when reading him.", "fae7d4dd"], "kisa": ["The man who took the West's science but not its law: he opposed adopting European laws in translation and wrote the alternative himself.", "973e27f5"]},
"kimdir-dorduncu-mustafa":{"baslik": ["Who Was Mustafa IV? A Reign That Lasted Fourteen Months", "b59dbb07"], "metin": ["Birth and years as a prince. He was born on 26 Sha'ban 1193 / 8 September 1779. His father was Abdülhamid I, his mother Ayşe Sineperver Sultan. He was brought up in the palace with a classical education; together with his brother Mahmud he accompanied his father on his tours of inspection in the city. Because his cousin Selim III was childless, he received affection from him and led a comfortable life as a prince.\n\nACCESSION. When Selim III, having put an end to the Nizam-i Cedid, was deposed at the close of the Kabakçı Mustafa revolt, he became sultan on 21 Rabi al-Awwal 1222 / 29 May 1807. He ratified the “hüccet-i şer'iyye” (sharia court deed) drawn up on 31 May 1807, stating that the rebels would not be called to account, and gave an assurance under oath. The document was of no use: those who had brought about the change of reign could not be prevented from interfering in affairs of state and forcing through whatever they wanted.\n\nTHE REIGN. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam describes these fourteen months as “such great turmoil, disorder and disintegration of the structure of the state that it would soon lead to regret over Selim's deposition”. The war with Russia that had begun in 1806 was continuing; the events in Istanbul spread to the front as well, the Janissaries revolted, the grand vizier was chased away, and the agha of the Janissaries, Pehlivan Agha, was killed by his own comrades. On 24 August 1807 a one-year armistice was signed at Slobozia, but this period was spent not in preparing for war but in settling internal scores. When, upon the resignation of the deputy grand vizier Köse Musa Pasha, Tayyar Mahmud Pasha, a mortal enemy of Selim III, was appointed in his place (November 1807), the Rusçuk Committee (Rusçuk yaranı), who wanted to put Selim back on the throne, gathered around Alemdar Mustafa Pasha.\n\nTHE FALL. Alemdar came to Istanbul with an army on 25 Jumada al-Ula 1223 / 19 July 1808; order was restored in the city, Kabakçı Mustafa was killed on his orders, and the Shaykh al-Islam Ataullah Mehmed Efendi was dismissed. When Alemdar stormed the Sublime Porte and headed for the palace to put Selim on the throne, Mustafa IV had the palace gates shut and ordered that his cousin Selim and his brother Mahmud be killed. Selim III was murdered; Mahmud escaped wounded and, as the last surviving member of the dynasty, was placed on the throne on 4 Jumada al-Akhira 1223 / 28 July 1808. It is related that Mustafa IV greeted the coup with the words “I have not stepped down from the throne; who has enthroned Mahmud?”, and that the sultan's imam Ahmed Kamil Efendi calmed him and saved him from Alemdar's wrath.\n\nDEATH. After being confined to his apartment in the palace, he did not refrain from sending word outside so that he might be restored to the throne. When about three months later Alemdar was attacked and killed and the rebels wished to march on the palace, he was strangled with the sash from his own waist on the order of his brother Mahmud II (28 Ramadan 1223 / 17 November 1808). His body was buried at Bahçekapı in the mausoleum of his father Abdülhamid I. Rumors spread among the rebels that the coffin was empty.\n\nFor the point on which historians differ regarding his personality, see the card `dorduncu-mustafa-sevilen-mi`.", "d41175c7"]},
"kimdir-gazi-ahmed-muhtar-pasa":{"baslik": ["Who was Gazi Ahmed Muhtar Pasha? The eastern front of the War of '93", "f8a8094d"], "metin": ["■ Before the war\nHe was an experienced soldier who had become a marshal at the age of thirty-two, had served as governor and commander in Yemen, and had held the governorships of Crete and Erzurum. On 8 February 1877 he was appointed commander-in-chief of the Anatolian War Army with the rank of marshal of the Fourth Army, and on 7 April 1877 he reached Erzurum and tried to put the army in order.\n\n■ COURSE OF EVENTS — FOUR VICTORIES\nWith the Russian attack on the frontier on the night of 24 April 1877, the war began in the east as well. The Russians took Ardahan on 17 May. Muhtar Pasha posted reserves at Soğanlı, Eleşkirt and Erzurum and concentrated his army around Köprüköy and Deveboynu. When the Russians occupied Doğubayazıt and began to besiege Kars, he went over to the counteroffensive on 21 June: he defeated the Russians at Deli Baba (Halyas) and, on 25-27 June, at Zivin; the Russian forces withdrew as far as the outskirts of Gyumri (Gümrü). On 25 August he won the victory of Gedikler (Kızıltepe) with a sudden attack. When the reinforced Russian army launched an offensive with 70,000 soldiers on 2 October, he defeated them again with his force of 34,000 in the three-day Battle of Yahniler — an engagement regarded as an operation that entered the history of warfare as both a battle of fortifications and a pitched battle.\n\n■ TURNING POINT: ALACADAĞ AND DEVEBOYNU\nOn 9 October he was forced to withdraw to Alacadağ; the Russians won the Battle of Alacadağ on 15 October and took some of the Turkish soldiers prisoner. Muhtar Pasha withdrew to Kars and made preparations for its defence, then left the command of the city and went to Köprüköy with a small detachment. On the line he had set up to defend Erzurum, he was defeated at Deveboynu on 4 November, withdrew to Erzurum with the few soldiers he had left and posted them in the Aziziye redoubts.\n\n■ AZIZIYE\nOn 8 November the Russians attacked the Aziziye redoubts. In the fierce fighting that followed, Muhtar Pasha, TOGETHER WITH THE PEOPLE OF ERZURUM, routed them and forced them to retreat as far as Deveboynu. With this victory the advance of the Russian army in Eastern Anatolia came to a halt. However, when Kars fell on 18 November, the pasha was obliged to take new defensive measures at Erzurum.\n\n■ THE TITLE AND AFTERWARDS\nHis outstanding military qualities won him the title of ghazi, which in his time was deemed fitting for only three people. His post as commander-in-chief ended when he was summoned to Istanbul on 9 January 1878; although various rumours circulated against him, on 17 January 1878 he was appointed commander-in-chief of the Çatalca fortifications.\n\n■ WHY IT MATTERS\nThe eastern front is seldom narrated because it remained in the shadow of the Balkans. Yet the story here sums up the general lesson of the war by itself: an army may win tactically time after time and still be forced to retreat in the face of superiority in numbers, supplies and reserves.", "1bf87192"], "kisa": ["On the eastern front he won four victories in a row, then retreated after two defeats, and before Erzurum he won one last victory together with the people.", "79939cdc"]},
"kimdir-gazi-osman-pasa":{"baslik": ["Who was Gazi Osman Pasha? The hero of Plevna", "39b377c3"], "metin": ["■ Before the war\nOsman Pasha's name was known even before 1877: when the Serbian Prince Milan declared war on 2 July 1876, it was he who commanded the force that routed the Serbian army led by Russian generals. When Russia declared war in April 1877, he was in Vidin as commander of the forces of the Western Army.\n\n■ THE TITLE\nFor his resistance at Plevna he was granted the title of GHAZI. In that period this title was deemed fitting for only three people; that is, it was a rare distinction, and one won on the battlefield.\n\n■ SURRENDER AND CAPTIVITY\nAfter being wounded and surrendering on 10 December 1877, he spent some time in captivity at Bugot, Bucharest, Kharkov and in Russia. While he was a captive, the Russian tsar gave him the order of the double eagle in appreciation of his heroism. A commander receiving a decoration from the ruler of the state that had taken him prisoner says something about the ethics of war in that period: defeat and disgrace were not considered one and the same.\n\n■ AFTERWARDS\nAfter returning home he became one of the figures the palace trusted most. As the sultan of the time, who did not trust the Sublime Porte, gradually concentrated the administration of the state in his own palace, he did so with the support of certain statesmen whom he considered conservative and honest; Gazi Osman Pasha is counted first among those names.\n\n■ WHY HE IS REMEMBERED\nPlevna was the only great source of pride to emerge from a lost war, and for this reason Osman Pasha's name became the name not of a defensive tactic but of an attitude. The heavier the weight of defeat, the greater the symbolic value of that attitude became.", "156fb6ea"], "kisa": ["The enemy tsar decorated a commander who had surrendered — and his own state gave him a title that until then had been granted to only three people.", "0c766d60"]},
"kimdir-hurrem-sultan":{"oneri": ["Selim (later Selim II)", "1ea175ea"], "ad": ["Hürrem Sultan", "091becb1"], "metin": ["While a concubine of Sultan Suleiman the Lawgiver, she was formally married to him around 1533-34, creating a break with the past not seen under any earlier sultan and in effect inaugurating the institution of “Haseki Sultan”. She pursued a long-term policy in the palace to bring her son Prince Selim (later Selim II) to the throne: TDV reports that she had a hand in the fall of Grand Vizier İbrahim Pasha in 1536 and in the execution of the rival heir, Prince Mustafa, in 1553 — the full extent of this latter claim is disputed among historians. By marrying her daughter Mihrimah to Rüstem Pasha (1539), who thus became an imperial son-in-law, she built a broad alliance in the palace; this alliance also had a share in Rüstem's return to the grand vizierate for a second time in 1555. Besides the Haseki Complex in Istanbul, she is also known for her charitable work through the foundations she established in Mecca, Medina and Jerusalem. She died on 15 April 1558 and is buried in the Süleymaniye Complex.", "b6e2678f"], "kisa": ["From concubine to wedded Haseki Sultan: Suleiman the Lawgiver's first and only formally married wife, she became the most powerful woman in the palace for the sake of her son's throne.", "6dc1f90c"], "not": ["The extent of her part in the execution of Prince Mustafa is disputed; the dates of her marriage, foundations and death are certain.", "8b616b6d"]},
"kimdir-ibrahim-muteferrika":{"baslik": ["İbrahim Müteferrika (İbrahim Efendi the Printer)", "727c1ce6"], "metin": ["Origins. He is estimated to have been born between 1670 and 1674 in the Transylvanian city of Koloszvár (today Cluj, Romania); his original name and family are unknown. There is very little information about his life before he became a Muslim; TDV draws attention to the Transylvanian milieu, in which all the circles engaged in printing were Unitarian.\n\nIN Ottoman service. According to archival records, while he was in the sipahi division, on 18 April 1716, in return for his service in the Austrian campaign, he was promoted to the rank of müteferrika of the Sublime Court — this is the source of his epithet. In 1717 he became interpreter and mihmandar (official host) to Ferenc Rákóczi II, who had been invited to the Ottoman lands. In his own time he was also called İbrahim Efendi the Interpreter, but he became known above all as 'İbrahim Efendi the Printer'.\n\nTHE PRINTING PRESS. In 1726 he wrote Vesiletü't-tıbaa, explaining the need for a printing press. At the beginning of July 1727 he and Yirmisekizçelebizade Mehmed Said Efendi were granted permission by decree and fatwa. The first book of the press set up in his house in Yavuzselim was completed at the beginning of 1729. The books he printed are in the fields of history, geography, language and military affairs; to most of them he added explanations, notes and maps. While the first books were printed in 1000-1200 copies, the difficulty of selling them brought later ones down to 500.\n\nAFTER the Tulip Era. In 1731 he presented to Mahmud I the Usulü'l-hikem, on the order of the state and military affairs. He set about establishing a paper mill at Yalova. His year of death is accepted as 1745 (the 1747 on his tombstone is taken to be the year the tomb was made).\n\nSee also: muteferrika-matbaasi-kurulusu (society).", "cd22c9c3"], "kisa": ["A translator born in the Transylvanian city of Kolozsvár who became a Muslim and entered Ottoman service; he set up the first Turkish printing press in his house and himself chose the books he would print.", "3a4b011c"], "bag": ["Significance: the real lasting effect of the press — that the choice of which books to print belonged to a publisher rather than a statesman — can be seen in the notes and maps that Müteferrika himself added.", "bdffffa5"]},
"kimdir-itri":{"oneri": ["Of Mehmed IV (reigned 1648-1687)", "316778d6"], "ad": ["Buhurizade Mustafa Itri Efendi", "9278f0c1"], "metin": ["He was born in Istanbul (in the Yayla quarter), probably around 1630-1640, and his given name was Mustafa; he is said to have taken the pen name 'Itri' from his father's trade as a seller of incense ('ıtır', fragrance). He served as a musician and singer in the privy chamber of Mehmed IV (reigned 1648-1687) and later, at his own request, rose to the office of sword-bearer (silahdar). No definite chain of teachers is known for his musical training; according to Rauf Yekta Bey, he may have frequented the Yenikapı Mevlevi lodge and taken lessons in ney playing from the dervishes, and he is also associated with such names as Derviş Ömer and Koca Osman. The site of his grave is not certain — one account points to the vicinity of the Yenikapı Mevlevi lodge, another to a place outside the Edirne Gate.\n\nItri composed in almost all the forms of Turkish music. His best-known work is the takbir in the segah mode: this composition in free rhythm, which achieves great expressive power within a narrow range, won acceptance and is still recited today before the festival prayers in many parts of the Islamic world — a continuity of more than three hundred years. From his secular repertoire, the neva kâr is set to a Persian poem beginning with Hafez of Shiraz's line 'Gülbün-i iyş mîdemed sâkī-i gül'izâr kû?', and because it opens directly with the sung text, without an instrumental introduction, it is counted among the masterpieces of its genre. The Segah Salat-i Ummiyye, the Rast Na't and a Mevlevi rite (ayin) in segah are also among his important works. Although Mehmed Esad Efendi speaks of a thousand works by him, modern researchers count between forty-two and forty-nine of his compositions as having survived. He died in 1123 (1711), according to some sources in 1124 (1712).", "63d78231"], "kisa": ["A thousand works are spoken of, some forty survive — but one of them is still recited every festival morning, as it has been for centuries.", "a2097b52"]},
"kimdir-kanuni-tahta-cikis":{"oneri": ["Suleiman's Accession to the Throne", "c82a04d6"], "ad": ["Suleiman I (the Lawgiver) — accession to the throne", "90e2583e"], "metin": ["He was born on 6 November 1494 in Trebizond, where his father, Prince Selim, was sanjak governor; his mother was Hafsa Sultan. He spent his childhood in the palace at Trebizond; according to Evliya Çelebi, it was here that he learned the goldsmith's craft from a Greek master, together with his foster brother Yahyâ. Because of Bayezid II's hesitations, he received a sanjak late and was sent to Caffa in 1509; from there he followed and supported his father's struggle for the throne. After Selim took the throne and eliminated his brothers who claimed it, Suleiman, as sole heir, was sent to Manisa in 1513 and stayed there for about seven years. The news of his father's death on 21-22 September 1520 arrived with a messenger from Grand Vizier Pîrî Mehmed Pasha; travelling overland, he reached Üsküdar on 30 September, crossed to the palace and ascended the throne, and the next day he received his father's body at Edirnekapı. His first acts were read as signs proclaiming the style of the new reign: he allowed some 600-800 artisans and notables whom Selim had deported from Tabriz and Cairo to return to their homelands, lifted the ban on the silk trade with Iran and compensated the merchants whose goods had been seized, punished officials and soldiers who had oppressed the people, and consented to the return to Egypt of the last Abbasid caliph, who had been brought to Istanbul. Before the second month of his reign was out, Janbirdi al-Ghazali, the beylerbey of Damascus, rebelled; the revolt was crushed in January 1521 and turned the new sultan toward a great campaign, against Belgrade. The Venetian envoy who saw him in 1520 described him as a tall, slender but hardy and amiable young man whose beard and moustache were barely visible.", "7446ca21"], "kisa": ["A prince who came to the throne without a rival: his father had done away with Suleiman's uncles for the sake of the throne, while Suleiman's first act was to send home those his father had exiled.", "1318839e"], "not": ["The title \"Kanûnî\" (the Lawgiver) is not contemporary with him: according to TDV, it first appears in the eighteenth century in Dimitrie Cantemir's Ottoman history and became widespread in the nineteenth century; Westerners of his time called him \"the Magnificent\" or \"the Great Turk\". The sources give differing dates for his birth (Mecdî says April-May 1496).", "5e07e638"]},
"kimdir-katib-celebi":{"oneri": ["Keşfü'z-Zünun (bibliography, completed in 20 years)", "e4fceb67"], "ad": ["Katib Çelebi", "30b5e0e1"], "metin": ["He was born in Istanbul in February 1609; his given name was Mustafa, and he is also known as Hacı Halife in the East and as Hajji Khalfa in the West. He received private education until the age of fourteen, and in 1623 entered the Anatolian Accounting Office. He took part as a clerk in Murad IV's eastern campaigns (1624-1640); after returning to Istanbul in 1635 he devoted himself entirely to reading and writing — for ten years he took lessons, night and day, from teachers such as Kadızade Mehmed Efendi and A'rec Mustafa Efendi.\n\nHis personality is imprinted on his works: TDV describes him as a measured scholar who avoided satire, had refined pursuits such as growing flowers, opposed all sectarian fanaticism, yet remained devout in the Hanafi line. While taking a critical approach to the religious practices he considered superstitious, he also looked critically at scholastic (madrasa) thought — this twofold stance makes him an exceptional representative of rational thought in his time.\n\nHis three great works cover different fields: Keşfü'z-Zünun (bibliography, completed in 20 years), Cihannüma (geography, written twice and left unfinished twice) and Fezleke (an Ottoman history in Turkish covering 1592-1654). He also has another work, Mizanü'l-Hak, which touches on the intellectual debates of the period. Franz Babinger likened him to Jalal al-Din al-Suyuti, and his works came to rank among the basic reference sources for Islamic studies in the West. He died in Istanbul on 6 October 1657; the second version of the Cihannüma was left unfinished, having reached Van.", "4c1e8e28"], "kisa": ["Before reaching the age of forty, a finance clerk shut himself away entirely to read — and created the most productive one-man encyclopaedia project of the Ottoman Empire.", "e2651976"]},
"kimdir-kosem-sultan":{"oneri": ["Kosem Sultan", "1409ad69"], "ad": ["Kösem Sultan", "afe2d0c1"], "metin": ["She was the haseki of Ahmed I, the mother of Murad IV and Sultan Ibrahim, and the grandmother of Mehmed IV. When Murad IV came to the throne at the age of eleven or twelve (1623), it was she who in effect conducted state affairs. Because of Ibrahim's mental health problems she maintained her influence in his reign too (1640-48). After the accession of Mehmed IV at the age of seven in 1648, she acted as regent with the title “Grand Valide”, but this time she entered into a power struggle with the young sultan's own mother, Turhan Hatice Sultan. This rivalry ended on 2 September 1651, when a group of aghas siding with Turhan killed Kösem inside the harem — it is the only instance in Ottoman history of a valide sultan being killed in this way.", "8fde078f"], "kisa": ["The only valide sultan who in effect ruled the state during the reigns of two sons and a grandson and was finally strangled to death in the harem.", "1abfcddf"]},
"kimdir-kuyucu-murad-pasa":{"oneri": ["Kuyucu Murad Pasha", "ea29cc25"], "ad": ["Kuyucu Murad Pasha — how did he suppress the Celalis?", "a6f77930"], "metin": ["He was a devshirme (child-levy recruit) of Croatian origin; he is estimated to have been born around 1522. He was dismissed from the beylerbeylik of Yemen on rumours of ill-gotten wealth and shut up in Yedikule, and was later pardoned. While retreating from a night engagement during the Tabriz campaign of 1585, he fell with his horse into a deep well, was taken prisoner by the Safavids and was held at Alamut until the peace of 1590. He distinguished himself at Haçova, conducted the peace of Zsitvatorok through his son-in-law Kadızade Ali Pasha and became grand vizier on 11 December 1606.\n\nHis campaign consisted of three moves. The first move (1607): When he set out from Üsküdar at the beginning of summer, he gave the governorship of the sanjak of Ankara to Kalenderoğlu, who was threatening Bursa — his aim was to keep him occupied and secure his own rear. At Konya and Adana he eliminated minor chiefs (Saraçoğlu, Cemşid). When Canbolatoğlu laid an ambush at the Bakras Pass, he took a long way round and came down by way of Belen to the plain of Oruç. Canbolatoğlu, who had more than thirty thousand musket-bearing soldiers, at first had the upper hand; through Tiryaki Hasan Pasha's tactic of hiding the guns on the slopes of the pass and drawing the enemy within cannon range, the Celali army broke up (24 October 1607). He spent the winter in Aleppo.\n\nThe second move (1608): He forestalled an alliance by giving the sanjak of İçel to Muslu Çavuş, a possible ally of Kalenderoğlu, who had begun plundering again after being refused entry to Ankara, and defeated Kalenderoğlu in August 1608 on the Göksun uplands (Alaçayır). With Janissaries carrying a week's provisions, he pursued Tavil Halil, who was fleeing toward Iran, and his brother Meymun for six days and six nights and caught them at Karahisarışarkî. On 18 December 1608 he returned to Istanbul with some 400 Celali banners.\n\nThe third move (1609): Concealing it even from the sultan, he spread the rumour that a campaign against Iran would be opened, assembled the army at Üsküdar and, when the rebel chiefs he had summoned to join the army arrived — for example Yusuf Pasha, who had rebelled around Aydın — he eliminated them (the 'Üsküdar campaign'). That same winter he issued a justice decree (adaletname) forbidding officials to oppress the people and tried to secure the return of those who had abandoned their homes.\n\nThe price: According to the TDV, Celali pressure in Anatolia was broken above all by his harsh and bloody measures; in his inquiries thousands of people were killed on the grounds that they were Celalis. In 1610 he marched on Iran, spent the winter in Diyarbekir and, while preparing a new campaign, died at Cülek on 5 August 1611; his body was transferred to his tomb at Vezneciler in Istanbul.", "1b7ea3fd"], "kisa": ["A grand vizier past eighty broke up the great Celali armies of Anatolia in two years: with bargaining, trickery and blood.", "d72133a7"], "not": ["Tradition: According to an account going back to Peçuylu İbrahim, he killed some 60-70,000 Celalis and filled the wells he had dug at his halting places with their corpses. According to another tradition, his rival Nasuh Pasha had him poisoned at a banquet. Neither is a fact confirmed by contemporary documents. The starting day of the campaign differs in the sources (15 June, 2 July or only 'July 1607').", "1b9cb7d0"]},
"kimdir-midhat-pasa-sahsiyet":{"baslik": ["Who was Midhat Pasha? A reformer in the provinces, a short-lived grand vizier at the centre", "ee03f7b5"], "metin": ["■ His personality in one sentence\nA source of the period describes Midhat Pasha as follows: in his view nothing was impossible, everything could be done; in all the undertakings he embarked on he did not think ahead or behind, and in particular did not take into account the demands of the time and the requirements of the surroundings, and he wanted to do, and have done, whatever he wished. This single sentence explains both his success and his downfall.\n\n■ TWO FACES OF THE SAME TRAIT\nIn the provinces this trait is a strength: building roads, bridges, schools, an industrial school and an agricultural credit fund in a province with limited resources requires precisely not saying \"it can't be done\". At the centre, however, the same trait does harm. This side of him made it difficult for him to work harmoniously both with the palace and with his colleagues, and led to his generally being described as an administrator who was obstinate in his own ways, proud and haughty. This is one of the reasons why he could not repeat in the ministries and the grand vizierate the success he had shown in his provincial posts.\n\n■ FORTY-NINE DAYS\nOn 30 January 1877 he submitted to the palace a memorandum (tezkire) written in harsh and severe language in a manner unbecoming his position, whose contents were leaked to the press; then he withdrew to his mansion and left the sultan's summonses unanswered. On 5 February 1877, the forty-ninth day of his grand vizierate, he was dismissed and sent into exile abroad. The tone that the man who drafted the constitution used toward the sultan who proclaimed it cost him his office within a month and a half.\n\n■ THE MAIN CRITICISM DIRECTED AT HIM\nIt was not only about tone. One of the serious criticisms directed at him is that the constitutional regime, whose proclamation he had secured with great hopes and persistence, instead of calming the crisis atmosphere of the 1870s, served the separatist movements in the Balkans and their important supporter, Russia. Two things strengthened this view: the war of 1877-1878, in whose preparatory phase the pasha also bore significant responsibility, ended in a heavy defeat; and in the following period the line shifted from strict Ottomanism to one based on the priority of Muslims.\n\n■ DESPITE THIS\nThe same source concludes its judgement as follows: for all that, Midhat Pasha is regarded in Turkish political life as a HISTORIC SYMBOL of the constitutional and parliamentary regime. His personality is disputed; his symbolic value is undisputed.", "fa1a13f2"], "kisa": ["A genius in the provinces, a disaster at the centre: the same trait — not saying \"it can't be done\" — made him both a great reforming governor and a grand vizier of forty-nine days.", "d725525d"]},
"kimdir-mihrimah-sultan":{"oneri": ["Mihrimah Sultan", "f7be1265"], "ad": ["Mihrimah Sultan", "f7be1265"], "metin": ["She was the daughter of Sultan Suleiman the Lawgiver and Hürrem Sultan, and the sultan's only surviving daughter. In 1539 she was married to Rüstem Pasha; it is reported that, together with her mother, she was instrumental in her husband's twice being appointed grand vizier, in 1544 and in 1555 — at first she supported her brother Prince Bayezid, but after the struggle for the succession intensified she supported Prince Selim. She was a figure whom her father consulted on important matters in his old age and who preserved her influence in the palace until her death. She had a complex built in Üsküdar and another, designed by Mimar Sinan, at Edirnekapı, and made a large donation for the repair of the water channels in Mecca (the “Mihrimah Water”). She died on 25 January 1578.", "7b625637"], "kisa": ["Suleiman the Lawgiver's only surviving daughter; the woman her father consulted on state affairs in his old age.", "994041b8"]},
"kimdir-naima":{"oneri": ["Ravzatü'l-Hüseyn (popularly known as ‘Naîmâ's History’)", "300193ed"], "ad": ["Who was Naîmâ? — the empire's first OFFICIAL historian", "f3517f4d"], "metin": ["His original name was Mustafa; he was born in Aleppo around 1065 (1655), d. 1128/1716. In the Ottoman bureaucracy he began as a palace official and rose to be a clerk of the divan; ‘Naîmâ’ was the pen name he took in state service. He advanced in the administration under the patronage first of Kalaylıkoz Ahmed Pasha and then of Amcazade Hüseyin Pasha.\n\nAround 1114 (1702) he received the title of the empire's FIRST official historian (vakanüvis); his task was to collect and arrange historical records for Hüseyin Pasha. Alongside this task he also held administrative posts such as provincial accountancy and the office of defterdar (treasurer) of the treasury; he died in August 1716.\n\nHis principal work, Ravzatü'l-Hüseyn (popularly known as ‘Naîmâ's History’), is based to a large extent on the notes of an earlier author (Ahmed Efendi); in its final form it covers roughly the years 1000-1070 (1592-1660), and after his death, in 1147 (1734), it was printed in two volumes by İbrahim Müteferrika — it is one of the first major historical works printed in Turkish on a printing press. According to the TDV, with its vivid narrative, its meticulous attention to the background of events and its skilful combination of numerous sources, his work became an influential model for later Ottoman historians and modern researchers.", "31b97037"], "kisa": ["A palace clerk born in Aleppo received, at about thirty years of age, the title of the state's first ‘vakanüvis’ (official historian).", "3e6c6bec"]},
"kimdir-nesri":{"oneri": ["Nesri", "ee89d49c"], "ad": ["Who was Neşrî? — the first ‘critical’ Ottoman historian to write in Turkish", "fd2a6c72"], "metin": ["His real name is not certain in the sources; it is conjectured to have been ‘Hüseyin b. Eyne Bey’. He was of Karamanid origin, was in the service of Selim I, completed his education in Bursa and taught at the Sultaniye Madrasa in the reign of Bayezid II (d. 926/1520).\n\nHis most important work is the Kitab-ı Cihannüma — a world history which, by his own account, he wrote because ‘while there were many works in other branches of learning, history remained scattered, and there was no history written in Turkish’. The work originally consisted of six parts, from the Creation to his own time; only the section on pre-Islamic Turkish history and Ottoman history has survived to the present. The copies we have extend to 1485, 1512 or 1516; the Ottoman portion runs from the foundation to the middle years of the reign of Bayezid II.\n\nThe TDV regards him as ‘the first Ottoman historian who tried to seek the truth’: he systematically compared his sources (especially Aşıkpaşazade) and, instead of merely listing events, tried to explain them in terms of cause and effect — this is regarded as the first sign of the critical method in Ottoman historiography.", "3bd743fb"], "kisa": ["A Bursa madrasa professor whose very name is not known for certain, annoyed that ‘history has not been written in Turkish’, wrote a world history in six volumes.", "406a176f"]},
"kimdir-nurbanu-sultan":{"oneri": ["the Atik Valide Complex in Üsküdar (mosque, madrasa, soup kitchen, library)", "36dacb68"], "ad": ["Nurbanu Sultan", "a8d4b91a"], "metin": ["She was the haseki of Selim II and the mother of Murad III. With her son's accession in 1574 she became the first person to bear the title “Valide Sultan” (queen mother) officially, and she was influential in state affairs and appointments. According to TDV, the close diplomatic contact she maintained with Venice contributed to easing the Ottoman-Venetian tensions of the period; it is also reported that she corresponded with Queen Catherine de' Medici of France and played a role in the development of trade relations. She built the Atik Valide Complex in Üsküdar (mosque, madrasa, soup kitchen, library) — the library in the complex is cited as the first library founded with a woman as its patron. She died on 7 December 1583, and her son Murad III attended her funeral in person.", "8b346af3"], "kisa": ["The first official “Valide Sultan” in 1574: the woman who in effect began the institution.", "21402780"]},
"kimdir-orhan-gazi":{"oneri": ["Bursa (1326)", "c250031d"], "ad": ["Orhan Gazi", "de9c91f8"], "metin": ["He was the son of Osman Gazi; the year of his birth is not certain. From the fact that he was referred to as a \"yiğit\" (valiant young man) when he married Nilüfer Hatun in 1299, the TDV infers that he was about eighteen at the time. According to tradition, he was married to Nilüfer, the daughter of the tekfur of Yarhisar; Süleyman and Murad were born of this marriage. During his father's lifetime he was sent on campaigns alongside experienced commanders such as Akça Koca, Konuralp and Köse Mihal; since he had been at the head of the army since 1305, the beylik passed to him without any struggle for the throne. Bursa (1326), Iznik (1331) and Izmit (1337) were taken in his time, and the beylik of Karesi was added to his realm. Ibn Battuta, who saw him, writes that he was the most powerful of the Turkmen sultans in wealth, territory and soldiers, and that he constantly made the round of his hundred or so fortresses and stayed no more than a month in any city. In an inscription of 1337 he is referred to by the title \"sultan al-ghuzat\" (sultan of the ghazis). His eldest son Süleyman Pasha, who carried out the conquests in Rumelia, died before him; Edirne was taken in 1361 by Prince Murad and his tutor (lala) Şahin. Shortly after the capture of Edirne, when he was already well advanced in years, he lost his life in March 1362 (Jumada al-Awwal 763) in a plague epidemic in Bursa; it is known that as early as 1354 he was suffering from a liver ailment and was treated by a Greek physician named Taronites. His tomb is beside his father's. When he died, only Murad, İbrahim and Halil of his six sons were still alive; he was succeeded by Murad I.", "d88b6b11"], "kisa": ["He took over a frontier beylik from his father and left his son a state that had established a bridgehead in Rumelia; a plague epidemic in Bursa ended his life.", "047f2d2d"], "not": ["For the information that plague was the cause of death, the TDV relies on the edition of the Byzantine short chronicles (Schreiner).", "c8a165e1"]},
"kimdir-osman-dikne":{"ad": ["Osman Digna: the Red Sea coast's own man", "49efd47b"], "metin": ["The center of the Mahdist movement was in the west of the Sudan: Kordofan, the island of Aba, then Khartoum. Despite this, already in the summer of 1883 there was a force fighting in the Mahdi's name on the Red Sea coast, that is, at the other end of the country. Between them lay hundreds of kilometers of land still under Egyptian administration. This force did not march there; it was already there.\n\n■ WHO\nThe best known of the Mahdist emirs in the Eastern Sudan is Osman Digna. The sources differ about his origins: one account says that he was born in Sawakin in 1836; another claims that he was of French origin and bore the name Georges Nisbet. Whichever is true, the Mahdi appointed him emir over the Beja tribes in the Eastern Sudan; that is, instead of sending an army from outside, he put one of the region's own people at the head of the region.\n\n■ WHAT HE DID: the timeline given by the source\nOn 5 August 1883 he attacked the Anglo-Egyptian force with five to six hundred of his men. On 11 September he clashed with an Egyptian unit at Kubab. On 15 October at Ebint he put Egyptian soldiers and civilians to the sword. On 5 November he destroyed an Egyptian force on the Red Sea coast. On 2 December at Temeynab he defeated an Egyptian unit of 1100 men. The following year, on 4 February, he made his second attack on the coast; on 8 February he won a success in the Ukak raid; on 29 February he inflicted heavy casualties on a large British unit under the command of General Graham; and on 12 March 1884 at Tamai (Teemmay) he carried out his last major attack.\n\n■ WHY IT MATTERS\nThis timeline shows that the uprising in the east was not the work of an army coming from the west. In 1884 the Mahdist state was not a contiguous country: Kordofan in the west and the Red Sea coast in the east were acting in the Mahdi's name, while the Nile line in between (Berber, Khartoum, Dongola, Sennar) was still in Egyptian hands. The strip in between closed only with the fall of Berber in May 1884 and of Khartoum in January 1885. In other words, that strange gap on the map is not a drawing error but the reality of that year.\n\nOsman Digna resisted all the regimes that came after and died in Wadi Halfa in 1926.", "203ee153"]},
"kimdir-p75b-ahmed-fevzi-pasa":{"ad": ["Ahmed Fevzi Pasha (Firârî, the Fugitive)", "c1bca1e6"], "metin": ["No separate article on the life of Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Ahmed Fevzi Pasha was found in the TDV; these sources give no dates of birth and death. What we know about his personality has been compiled from the testimony about his conduct in Dönmez's article and from sentences scattered through other TDV articles.\n\n① Political line. The first principles of the Treaty of Hünkâr İskelesi (8 July 1833) were determined by Ahmed Fevzi Pasha together with Orlov; Hüsrev Pasha and Âkif Efendi later joined the negotiations (TDV Hünkâr İskelesi). Dönmez writes that these three received medals from Tsar Nicholas I and that from that date Britain regarded them as pro-Russian. At the beginning of 1834 Fevzi Pasha was sent to Russia as special envoy; the British ambassador, Lord Ponsonby, repeatedly reported to London that he was passing information to the Russian ambassador. In the Churchill Affair of 1836, the ambassador's real aim was to have Fevzi Pasha and Âkif Pasha dismissed; he did not succeed, and in November 1836 Fevzi Pasha was appointed Grand Admiral in full. Dönmez adds that Fevzi Pasha 'was sometimes close to the Russians and sometimes drew away from them'.\n\n② Rivalry. A few months after Fevzi Pasha became Grand Admiral, Hüsrev Pasha was removed from the office of serasker, and the rift between the two pashas gradually widened (Dönmez). Dönmez, citing Yüksel Çelik, traces the origins of the enmity to the contest to become a son-in-law (damad) of the palace and to Hüsrev Pasha's humiliating Fevzi Pasha in front of the Prussian officer Moltke. In the same footnote it is reported, with reference to Mustafa Nuri Pasha, that Mahmud II suspected a plan to place Abdülmecid on the throne and charged Fevzi Pasha with killing him in the event of such an attempt (a report from a single source; it could not be confirmed by another source). Regarding Hüsrev Pasha as an adversary forms the background to Fevzi Pasha's fears in 1839.\n\n③ Relations with Egypt. According to one of Dönmez's footnotes, in 1836 Mehmed Ali Pasha asked that, if a representative was to be sent to resolve the disputes, it be Fevzi Pasha; this request was not deemed appropriate, and İbrahim Sarim Efendi was sent.\n\n④ July 1839. In Avidick's report, Fevzi Pasha appears as an indecisive, weeping, anxious man: he informs the commanders, who weep at the sultan's death, of the situation; he waits for news from the İzmir steamer; he asks Avidick whether he would be killed if he returned to Istanbul; he says 'let us put out to sea and see what happens'; Osman Bey sharply opposes this and, according to Dönmez, in the end persuades Fevzi Pasha to go to Alexandria. In Alexandria, according to the report of the Austrian consul, he presents the image of 'a poor, lost man'; Mehmed Ali does not trust him and does not share important information, and Fevzi Pasha leaves the house assigned to him and begins to live aboard ship. Avidick's report also relates that one evening, while sitting with Osman Bey and two of his men, he drank and wept, saying 'kill me'. From the visit paid by Kamil Pasha in early 1840 it is understood that he did not openly state his remorse but that his conscience was troubled (Dönmez). Damat Said Mehmed Pasha became Grand Admiral in his place.\n\n⑤ Interpretation (this is an assessment drawn from reading the sources side by side, not a definitive verdict): when these testimonies are read together, the portrait that emerges is not that of a determined traitor but of a commander steered by his own fear and by the agitators around him. But the main document on which this portrait rests is Avidick's report, and at that time Avidick was in the service of Hüsrev Pasha, having received from him a promise of twice his salary and a house; the French ambassador Pontois also said that he was not reliable (whereas the Austrian ambassador trusted him). In other words, Fevzi Pasha's 'weakness' is partly a testimony that passed into the hands of his enemies.", "6bb77126"], "kisa": ["The Grand Admiral whom Mahmud II trusted, a target of the British ambassador as pro-Russian, an enemy of Koca Hüsrev Pasha, and the man who took the fleet to Egypt in 1839: the person described by the sources is indecisive, susceptible to fear and under the influence of those around him — but the chief source of this description is a report written against him.", "7a021e0c"], "not": ["The TDV article on Hüsrev Pasha calls Fevzi Pasha 'Firârî, Hâin' (the Fugitive, the Traitor); Dönmez, on the other hand, interprets the event as the result of factional conflict. The descriptions of his personality (indecision, fear) come from a single report; no second independent witness was found.", "09f4f6ee"]},
"kimdir-p75b-florence-nightingale":{"ad": ["Florence Nightingale (the Lady with the Lamp)", "9fa53b77"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article on the Selimiye Barracks, during the Crimean War the barracks was given to British soldiers, accommodation was provided for the soldiers who came with their families, and it was used as a hospital during the war; Florence Nightingale, who came to care for sick and wounded British soldiers, stayed in the tower of the barracks on the side of the Selimiye Mosque. The TDV finds it 'interesting' that this tower, which had been reserved for the sultan, was allotted to her; the building is today preserved as the Florence Nightingale Museum, and it attracted great attention in the Crimean War and became a subject for European newspapers.\n\nAccording to Hatipoğlu's (2011) summary, based on S. M. Goldie's book 'Letters from the Crimea', which examines Nightingale's letters: Nightingale reached Üsküdar (Scutari) on 4 November 1854 and wrote more than 300 letters until she left the East in July 1856. The Times correspondent William Howard Russell alerted his readers on 9 October 1854; on 14-15 October Nightingale wrote to her friend Elizabeth Herbert and to Elizabeth's husband Sidney Herbert, Secretary at War, to say that she wished to go to Üsküdar, and with Herbert's letter she was sent together with her team. The hospital at Üsküdar served as a general hospital between November 1854 and 1856, the Haydarpaşa hospital operated as one of its components, and the Koulali, Üsküdar and Kadıköy hospitals were her responsibility. According to the same source, the doctors at the hospital found her cold and belittled her, and Dr. John Hall, head of the army medical department in the war, did not want her there; Dr. Menzies, the medical director at Üsküdar, however, accepted all her requests. A team of 45 headed by Mary Stanley and a group headed by the Jamaican Mary Seacole had also come to Üsküdar; those around Nightingale were made tense by this rivalry.\n\nScope of this page: frequently repeated figures (what percentage, how many nurses), such as those saying that Nightingale reduced death rates and transformed British hospitals through hygiene reforms, are found in the historiography of nursing, but in this round the same figure could not be obtained from more than one place in a peer-reviewed source; therefore no figure is given here. As to the share of the Ottoman army and Ottoman health institutions in these hospitals, Hatipoğlu writes that Nightingale's letters mention the extraordinary contribution of the Turkish army and that the Florence Nightingale Museum conveys this to the world; no details are given.", "fbb50359"], "kisa": ["The nurse who came to the Selimiye Barracks at Üsküdar to care for British soldiers in the Crimean War; she stayed in the barracks tower reserved for the sultan, and her letters are among the most detailed testimonies of the war.", "7711843f"], "not": ["No separate 'Nightingale' article was found in the TDV; the information was taken from the article on the Selimiye Barracks and from Hatipoğlu's summary of the letters. The letters themselves were not read (second-hand summary).", "25e40538"]},
"kimdir-padisah-lakaplari":{"baslik": ["The sultans' epithets — where do Selim the Grim, the Lawgiver and the Conqueror come from?", "354a1313"], "metin": ["The epithets of the Ottoman sultans did not all arise in the same way; some took hold among their contemporaries, others were produced centuries later by the pens of historians.\n\n**Bayezid I the Thunderbolt** — he received this epithet because of the courage and dash he showed at the Battle of Frenk Yazısı in 1386; what TDV emphasises is not speed but fearlessness on the battlefield.\n\n**Yavuz, 'the Grim' (Selim I)** — TDV writes plainly that in his own time he was known by this epithet because of his stern temperament, courage and boldness; a common assumption of the kind 'it was given by the people after his death' is, according to TDV, wrong. In Ottoman documents his actual name is 'Selim Shah'.\n\n**Kanuni, 'the Lawgiver' (Suleiman I)** — here there is a great surprise: this name was not used in his own time. In TDV's own words, the epithet first appeared in the 18th century in Dimitrie Cantemir's Ottoman history, and in the 19th century was adopted by Ottoman historians and spread — that is, 'Kanuni' is a name that stuck more than two centuries later. Western writers of his day called him 'the Magnificent' (Magnificent/Magnifique) or 'the Great Turk' (Grand Turc) — this is the origin of the name 'Suleiman the Magnificent'.\n\n**Mad İbrahim (İbrahim I)** — TDV writes of this epithet in language that questions it, almost rejects it: it states that the nickname 'Mad' was put forward especially by some historians at the beginning of the 20th century and spread afterwards; it stresses that İbrahim, unlike his uncle Mustafa I, suffered psychological disturbances not continuously but from time to time (see the 'Mad sultans' comparison card).\n\n**Mehmed the Hunter (Mehmed IV)** — the reason TDV gives is simple and clear: his passion for hunting. In time this passion grew so great that he made the hunting grounds coincide with the army's campaign routes and devoted most of his working time to it — this neglect is regarded as part of the road that led to his deposition in 1687.\n\n**Two epithets common in popular speech but not found in TDV:** 'Sarı Selim' (Selim the Blond) and 'Sarhoş Selim' (Selim the Sot), used for Selim II, do not occur at all in TDV's `selim-ii` entry — this is a naming that lives not in academic language but only in popular historical narrative.", "18e209a6"], "kisa": ["Selim's name 'Yavuz' took hold in his own lifetime, Suleiman's 'Kanuni' was born 250 years later from a historian's pen, and 'Mad İbrahim' is a 20th-century invention that TDV itself rejects.", "880ed80c"]},
"kimdir-padisah-sanat-zanaat":{"baslik": ["The sultans' arts and crafts", "d978322f"], "metin": ["Most of the Ottoman sultans were, in TDV's own words, also poets — in the tradition of divan literature, using a mahlas (poetic pen name) was almost a custom at the palace:\n\n**Sultan Mehmed the Conqueror** — wrote poems under the pen name 'Avni'; he also took an interest in the sciences of geography, mathematics and astronomy and received daily lessons from private tutors.\n\n**Bayezid II** — used the pen name 'Adli'; a small divan of some 125 ghazals of his has been printed (Istanbul 1308/1890). He was a middling poet but highly skilled in calligraphy; it is also recorded that he learned to read the Uyghur script and knew a little Italian. Later, because of his piety, he was also referred to as 'Bayezid-i Veli' (Bayezid the Saint).\n\n**Sultan Suleiman the Lawgiver** — wrote a divan under the pen name 'Muhibbi', and also used the pen names 'Muhib' and 'Meftuni'. With about 3000 poems he was the most prolific poet among the Ottoman sultans.\n\n**Selim II** — composed poems under the pen names 'Selimi' and 'Talibi' and was considered a good poet.\n\n**Ahmed III** — became a good calligrapher by studying the thuluth and naskh scripts with Hafız Osman, the famous calligrapher of the age, and the ta'liq script with Veliyyüddin Efendi; he was a patron of the calligraphy masters of his time. He himself also wrote poetry under the pen name 'Necib'. In addition, he was at the centre of the passion for gardening and flower-growing that symbolises the Tulip Era, and was a good marksman/archer.\n\n**Mahmud II** — took an interest in music and calligraphy; a panel written in his own hand hangs in the tomb of his father, Abdülhamid I. TDV's own text says that he too was given the pen name 'Adli' at his birth.\n\nThis last piece of information calls for attention: the giving of the same pen name 'Adli' to both Bayezid II AND Mahmud II occurs verbatim in two separate TDV entries, but it is unusual for the same pen name to be given to two different sultans three centuries apart — this project records it as a fact, but cross-checking with a second academic source is recommended; it is not a contradiction but a repetition that needs confirmation.", "b60341ce"], "kisa": ["Suleiman the Lawgiver wrote three thousand poems and was the most prolific poet among the sultans; Ahmed III was a master calligrapher; two different sultans bore the same pen name, 'Adli'.", "9e874f05"]},
"kimdir-patrona-halil":{"baslik": ["Patrona Halil", "6cdba988"], "metin": ["(For his grievances and demands see patrona-halil-derdi-neydi; for what the rebels did see patrona-isyancilarin-yaptiklari.)\n\nHIS NICKNAME. He belonged to the Janissary corps; because he served for a time as a levend (marine) on galleons, he was known by the nickname 'Patrona'.\n\nWHO HE WAS. TDV presents him as of Albanian origin, from Hurpeşte, and a member of the 17th orta (company) of the Janissary corps. He had earlier been involved in the uprisings at Niš and Vidin, had twice escaped the death penalty and was a persuasive speaker. In Istanbul he had worked as a street vendor and a bath attendant, and was known among the people as one who knew the unseen.\n\nTHOSE BEHIND HIM. According to TDV, the task of preparing the revolt was given to him by Kaymakam Mustafa Pasha, who coveted the grand vizierate; it was he who had earlier saved Halil from execution for a murder. İspirizade Ahmed Efendi, preacher of Hagia Sophia, and Zülali Hasan Efendi, the qadi of Istanbul, were also involved. At Halil's side were the greengrocer Muslu Beşe of Rusçuk and Emir Ali the coffee-house keeper.\n\nIN POWER. He answered Mahmud I's offer of 100,000 gold coins by saying that all the wealth of Istanbul already belonged to him and that he had no need of office or money. Nevertheless, within a few weeks he acquired a great fortune; the Valide Sultan (queen mother) addressed him as 'my second son'.\n\nHIS END. On 25 November 1730 he was summoned to the palace with the promise of the post of beylerbey of Rumelia and was killed in the Circumcision Room by Pehlivan Halil Agha.", "b023dab1"], "kisa": ["A Janissary called 'Patrona' because he had served as a marine on a galleon; while a bath attendant, he toppled a sultan from the throne and refused 100,000 gold coins, saying 'Istanbul is already mine'.", "930b8e4e"]},
"kimdir-safiye-sultan":{"oneri": ["Safiye Sultan", "cad11635"], "ad": ["Safiye Sultan", "cad11635"], "metin": ["She was the haseki of Murad III and the mother of Mehmed III. She became valide sultan on her son's accession in 1595; in TDV's words, she had a say in many appointments, from the grand vizier to the Shaykh al-Islam. She is the only Ottoman valide sultan to have corresponded directly and exchanged gifts with Queen Elizabeth I (in 1593 she sent embroidered fabrics in return for a jewelled portrait) — this correspondence was part of the Anglo-Ottoman commercial diplomacy of the period. In 1598 she began the construction of the New Mosque (Yeni Cami) at Eminönü, but when she was removed from the palace on her son's death in 1603, the construction was left unfinished; the building would be completed only more than sixty years later, in 1665, by another valide sultan, Turhan Hatice. She died in January 1619.", "15189a20"], "kisa": ["The only Ottoman valide sultan to correspond directly with Queen Elizabeth I of England.", "4fa8a360"]},
"kimdir-sammar-hail-residileri":{"ad": ["The Rashidis: the emirs of Ha'il who took Najd from the Saudis", "b911771d"], "metin": ["The masters of Central Arabia at the end of the nineteenth century were not the Saudis but a much less known dynasty: the Rashidis, based in Ha'il.\n\n■ WHO THEY ARE\nThey belong to the Abda branch of the Shammar tribe. In 1835 Abdullah bin Rashid seized the emirate of Ha'il, the center of the Jabal Shammar region, and founded the dynasty. The name of the region also comes from this: Jabal Shammar, that is, the Shammar mountains.\n\n■ RELATIONS WITH THE OTTOMANS\nThe Rashidis accepted Ottoman sovereignty and received support from the state in times of war. This is the typical form of Ottoman policy in Central Arabia: instead of establishing direct administration in the desert, recognizing the strong tribal structure there and exercising sovereignty through it.\n\n■ THE RECKONING WITH THE SAUDIS\nTwo dynasties fought for the mastery of Najd. At Mulayda in the Qasim region, the army of Muhammad bin Rashid inflicted a heavy defeat on the Saudi forces led by Abd al-Rahman bin Faisal; this was the de facto end of the Second Saudi State. The Saudi family went into exile in Kuwait, and the whole of Najd came under Rashidi rule.\n\nTHE SENSITIVITY OF THE DATE: The sources give the YEAR of this battle (1891) but not its DAY. The name Mulayda does not appear at all in the entry on the Rashidis; the entry on the Saudis says that the fighting went on until 1889 and that in 1891 the Saudi family was completely driven out of Riyadh. The day dates commonly cited are not supported by these sources.\n\n■ ITS END\nThe struggle between the Rashidis and the Saudis went on without interruption from 1902: that year Abdulaziz bin Abd al-Rahman returned to Riyadh with the men he had taken with him from Kuwait and entered the city. In 1921 the Rashidis were decisively defeated by the Saudis, and the emirate of Ha'il passed into history. That is, for thirty years in Central Arabia two dynasties laid claim to the same land; in the end the losing side was the one that today is mostly not remembered.", "e706dd90"]},
"kimdir-sehzade-halil-orhan-oglu":{"ad": ["Prince Halil — Orhan Gazi's son by a Byzantine princess, the child at the centre of a ransom negotiation", "a942b31e"], "metin": ["Halil was one of the six sons of Orhan Gazi. His mother Theodora was the daughter of John Kantakouzenos, who was fighting for the throne in Byzantium; in 1346 Kantakouzenos had given his daughter to Orhan as a wife, hoping through this marriage to obtain military help from Orhan. Halil's elder brothers, Süleyman Pasha and Murad, were by another mother, Nilüfer Hatun according to Ottoman tradition.\n\n■ THE ABDUCTION\nAccording to the TDV article on Orhan, in 758 (1357) Halil, then eleven years old, was taken captive by \"pirates\" in the Gulf of İzmit and carried off to Old Phocaea (Eski Foça). The lord of Phocaea was the Byzantine governor Leo Kalothetos; he was a man well known to the Byzantine court, whom Emperor Andronikos III had made governor of Chios in 1329. The TDV article writes that the course of events showed Halil's captivity to have actually been a plot by the Byzantine court to force Orhan to make peace.\n\n■ TWO YEARS OF CAPTIVITY\nThe aged and ailing Orhan appealed to Emperor John V Palaiologos to rescue his much-loved son by Theodora. In the spring of 1358 the emperor went to Phocaea with three galleys; Orhan's friend İlyas, the Bey of Saruhan, also besieged the town by land, but no result was obtained. Kalothetos was trying to extract a large sum for Halil. The same year the emperor went to Phocaea once more, and again failed.\n\n■ THE RELEASE\nIn the spring of 1359 Orhan came to Üsküdar and the emperor to the Maiden's Tower, and negotiations were conducted through envoys. Orhan paid a ransom of 30,000 Venetian gold coins and Halil was freed. Halil was brought to Istanbul, where he was betrothed to the emperor's young daughter Irene, and was then brought to İzmit by the emperor. According to the article on Murad, the handover took place in September-October 1359.\n\n■ THE PROMISE OF THE SUCCESSION AND AFTERWARDS\nThe emperor also obtained a promise that Halil would succeed to the throne after Orhan; Byzantium hoped through Halil to open a period of peace and balance with the Ottomans. However, as the TDV points out, among the Ottomans, who followed Turco-Mongol tradition, there was no law of succession, and this promise was forgotten. When Orhan died in March 1362, Halil was sixteen years old, and the men of Halil and İbrahim, who were in İznik and Eskişehir, moved with the support of Karaman and Eretna to seize the throne in Bursa; but Çandarlı Kara Halil, the qadi of Bursa, took control of the situation and Murad came to the throne. Halil's subsequent fate does not appear in the TDV articles consulted.", "c9c18a2c"]},
"kimdir-serif-huseyin":{"ad": ["Sharif Hussein — the Emir of Mecca brought up in Istanbul, leader of the revolt of 1916", "837d50d4"], "metin": ["Sharif Hussein belonged to the Dhawi Awn branch of the Hashemite family, descended from the Prophet. According to the TDV, he was born in Istanbul, completed his education in Mecca, came to Istanbul in 1880 and for a long time served as a member of the Council of State with the rank of vizier. In November 1908 he was appointed Emir of Mecca.\n\n■ HOW THE TWO HOLY CITIES WERE GOVERNED IN THE OTTOMAN PERIOD\nThe Hejaz passed to the Ottomans in 1517 without fighting. The local government of Mecca remained with the Hashemite emirs known as sharifs; however, the emirs were appointed by the sultan's diploma (berat) and had the Friday sermon read in the sultan's name. In the last period the Hejaz was a province (vilayet), and alongside the emir there were a governor and a commander appointed by Istanbul. For centuries this dual system secured the attachment of the two holy cities to the Ottomans; although the sharifs competed among themselves, no one could become emir without the approval of Istanbul. The flow of taxes worked in reverse: the Hejaz sent no tribute to Istanbul; on the contrary, money and provisions went regularly to the two holy cities from Istanbul and Egypt.\n\n■ TENSION\nAccording to the TDV, Hussein's emirate was marked from the outset by disputes with the central government. He calmed the Bedouin, who had lost income with the opening of the Hejaz Railway, and gained prestige by ensuring the safety of the pilgrims; but he clashed constantly with the Unionist governors. While the Ottoman administration wished to see the emir as an official whose authority was limited to Mecca, Hussein wanted to have a say over the whole region. Vehib Pasha, who became governor and commander of the Hejaz in January 1914, asked in July 1914 for his dismissal on the grounds that he was aiming to overthrow the Ottomans. In 1912 his son Abdullah had made his first contact in Cairo with the British representative, Lord Kitchener.\n\n■ THE WAR YEARS AND THE REVOLT\nWhen the war began, Hussein officially declared his loyalty; on the other hand, he did not support the proclamation of jihad and tried to prevent the extension of the Hejaz Railway to Mecca. Between 14 July 1915 and 30 January 1916 he corresponded with the British High Commissioner McMahon and, in return for rising against the Ottomans, asked for the recognition of an Arab kingdom with extensive borders. The TDV writes that his worry that he would be dismissed from the emirate played a part in this bargaining. He launched the revolt in the tense atmosphere created by Djemal Pasha's execution of a number of Arabs in Beirut and Damascus in 1915 and 1916. The first days of the revolt are given differently in two TDV articles: according to the article on Fahreddin Pasha, on 3 June 1916 the railway and telegraph lines around Medina were destroyed, and on 9 June a general attack was launched; the article on Sharif Hussein places the revolt in June 1916 and the proclamation justifying it on 27 June. Jeddah fell on 16 June, Mecca in July and Taif in September; Medina, thanks to Fahreddin Pasha's defence, remained Ottoman until January 1919.\n\n■ WHY\nThe TDV reads the revolt as a combination of several causes: a conflict over authority with the Unionist administration extending over years, the fear of being dismissed, the ambition to found his own kingdom, the British promise of money and arms, and Arab public opinion made tense by Djemal Pasha's executions. According to the TDV, in many parts of the Islamic world Hussein was accused of treason and cursed for collaborating with the British against a Muslim state.\n\n■ HIS END\nBritain had shared the same lands with France through the Sykes-Picot Agreement (May 1916), and in December 1915 had also made a separate agreement with Ibn Saud. According to the TDV, Hussein had said that he could gather a force of more than 100,000 for the revolt, but was able to field only an armed force of around 4-5,000, and most Arabs remained loyal to the Ottomans. When the caliphate was abolished in March 1924, he proclaimed himself caliph; this move was met with a hostile reaction in the Islamic world. When Ibn Saud took Mecca in October 1924, he went into exile in Cyprus; in 1930, having fallen ill, he went to join his son Abdullah, who was Emir of Jordan; he died a year later and was buried in Jerusalem.", "2b0de99f"]},
"kimdir-turhan-hatice-sultan":{"oneri": ["Turhan Hatice Sultan", "89c0cc74"], "ad": ["Turhan Hatice Sultan", "89c0cc74"], "metin": ["She was the mother of Mehmed IV. She became valide sultan when her son came to the throne at the age of seven in 1648, but for the first three years she remained in the shadow of her mother-in-law, Kösem Sultan. In 1651, when a group of aghas loyal to her killed Kösem, she came to power alone. In 1656, on the advice of her counsellor Kasım Agha, she brought to the grand vizierate Köprülü Mehmed Pasha, an elderly provincial pasha who laid down four conditions, and left the administration of the state to him; this appointment began the Köprülü era, which would last nearly half a century. In 1665 she had the New Mosque (Yeni Cami), which her mother Safiye Sultan had left unfinished, completed. She died in Edirne on 5 July 1683 and is buried in the New Mosque Complex in Istanbul.", "88180f76"], "kisa": ["The valide sultan who, having eliminated her rival Kösem and entrusted the state to Köprülü Mehmed Pasha, opened an era that would last nearly half a century.", "17493198"]},
"kimdir-yeni-osmanlilar-cemiyeti":{"baslik": ["Who were the New Ottomans?", "0f52ed45"], "metin": ["■ Who they were\nThe New Ottomans were the opposition group formed by Ottoman intellectuals against the administration between 1865 and 1876. The names most often mentioned are Namık Kemal, Ziya Bey (Pasha) and Ali Suavi. The source's own note of caution is important: among those alleged to have been members there was no single, unified idea or aim — that is, the movement was not a party but a loose circle.\n\n■ FOUNDATION: THE DATE TOO IS DISPUTED\nThe formation of the group is generally traced to 1865. By contrast, according to Kaya Bilgegil's findings, the society proper (Young Turkey) was founded on 30 August 1867 in Baden-Baden with a thirteen-article statute. The two dates point to two different things: the birth of the secret circle in Istanbul and the formal founding of the organisation in exile.\n\n■ WHAT THEY WANTED\nThe programme had been outlined in the open letter that Mustafa Fazıl Pasha wrote to Sultan Abdülaziz: constitutional government, an assembly representing the people (şura-yı ümmet, council of the community) and liberty. That is, what they wanted was not the abolition of the monarchy but its being bound to a text and an assembly.\n\n■ THEIR WEAPON: THE NEWSPAPER\nThe movement's real strength was the press. Muhbir began to appear in London on 31 August 1867; Hürriyet was published in London and Geneva between 1868 and 1870. These newspapers were the means by which the opposition in exile reached Istanbul.\n\n■ ITS END AND LEGACY\nThe group broke up relatively quickly. Even so, it had an influence on the Constitution (Kanun-ı Esasî) of 1876 and inspired the Young Turk movement that came after it. The naming also stems from this: the Young Ottomans and the New Ottomans refer to the same group, whereas the Young Turks is the name of the later generation that followed them.", "c27fd3a5"], "kisa": ["Imagine an opposition movement: who its members were, and even whether they had a common idea, is disputed — yet ten years later the constitution was written in their language.", "177e6a89"]},
"kimdir-yirmisekiz-celebi-mehmed-efendi":{"baslik": ["Yirmisekiz Çelebi Mehmed Efendi (c. 1670 – 1731)", "5e542f12"], "metin": ["Birth and name. He was born in Edirne; his father was Süleyman Agha, the Saksoncubaşı (chief keeper of the mastiffs). Since in 1720 he is estimated to have been about fifty, his birth is placed around 1670 (Saint-Simon, who met him in Paris, writes that he looked sixty). He was called 'Yirmisekiz Çelebi' (Twenty-eight Çelebi) because he belonged to the 28th orta (Janissary company) of the Janissary corps. His pen name in poetry was Faizi.\n\nRISE. In the corps he served as çorbacı (company commander), muhzırbaşı (chief of the Janissary agha's guard) and, with the rank of hacegân (senior chancery official), as efendi (chief clerk) of the Janissaries; he was then appointed superintendent of the Imperial Mint.\n\nPASSAROWITZ (1718). At the end of 1717 he was sent to the peace negotiations with the title of defterdar of the third division (şıkk-ı sâlis), together with the former sword-bearer (silahdar) İbrahim Efendi. The talks, which began on 5 June 1718, ended in a treaty on 21 July.\n\nPARIS embassy (1720-1721). When sending an extraordinary envoy to France came onto the agenda, he was appointed to the post with the support of Grand Vizier Nevşehirli Damad İbrahim Pasha. It is known that before leaving he read a treatise surviving from an embassy to Vienna and that he was given a letter of instructions. On 7 October 1720 he set out with his son Mehmed Said Efendi and a large retinue; on 21 March 1721 he was received by Louis XV; on 8 October 1721 he returned to Istanbul. For the importance of the embassy and what he saw in Paris, see the card yirmisekiz-elciligin-onemi and the curiosity cards 'From the Sefâretnâme'.\n\nAFTER the return. He became defter emini (keeper of the registers), in 1722 rûznâmçe-i evvel (chief keeper of the daily ledger) and in 1724 chief accountant. His effort to conclude an offensive-defensive treaty with France against Austria came to nothing. In 1730 he was sent to Egypt to seize the property of Çerkez Mehmed.\n\nEXILE and death. After the Patrona Uprising, because of his closeness to Ahmed III and İbrahim Pasha, he was exiled to Nicosia and died there. According to TDV, although his date of death is generally given as 1732, the tombstone inscription in the burial ground of the Sinan Pasha Mosque in Nicosia gives 14 Muharram 1144, that is, 19 July 1731.\n\nWORKS. Sefâretnâme (Embassy Account; also included in Râşid's History; French translations were made from 1721 onwards). He translated the fourth part of Shahrazuri's Resâʾilü'ş-Şecereti'l-ilâhiyye under the title Semeratü'ş-Şecere.\n\nHIS SON. Mehmed Said Efendi, who accompanied him, was, together with İbrahim Müteferrika, one of the founders of the first Turkish printing press (see lale-devri-yenilikler).", "46a29fdc"], "kisa": ["A financial official who rose from the Janissary corps; a plenipotentiary at Passarowitz, an envoy in Paris, a carrier of French taste in Istanbul, an exile in Cyprus.", "cb243a8c"], "not": ["The year of death is contradictory among the sources: while some records give 1732, according to TDV's reading of the tombstone inscription it is 1731.", "0448037c"], "bag": ["Significance: according to TDV, his courteous, intelligent and accommodating personality played a part in changing the image of the Turk in Paris; his friendly letter to Marshal Villeroi after returning is one example.", "b2ae131a"]},
"kinalizade-ahlak-i-alai":{"baslik": ["Kınalızâde Ali Çelebi and the Ahlâk-ı Alâî", "db54bc77"], "metin": ["Kınalızâde wrote the Ahlâk-ı Alâî in 1564, while he was qadi of Damascus, and presented it to the beylerbey (governor-general) of Damascus, Semiz Ali Pasha; the word \"Alâî\" in its title also refers to this dedication. The book consists of an introduction and three books: individual ethics (temperament, virtues and vices, the etiquette of speech), family ethics (the Turkish-Islamic family, the duties of the head of the household, the upbringing of children) and political ethics (the administration of the state, the qualities of the ruler, the central place of justice). According to TDV, the work is neither a translation nor a compilation of old Persian sources; it draws on transmission-based, philosophical and Sufi conceptions of ethics. It was read as a textbook in the madrasas, served as a source for later Turkish books on ethics, and there are more than seventy copies of it in the libraries of Istanbul.", "6bb3b8aa"], "sanatci.ad": ["Kınalızâde Ali Efendi (Ali Çelebi)", "bc5ad7ba"], "sanatci.hayat": ["Born in Isparta in 916 (1510); in Istanbul he studied under Kara Sâlih and Çivizâde Muhyiddin Mehmed Efendi. In a career that began in 950 (1543) as professor at the Hüsâmiye Madrasa in Edirne, he served as qadi of Damascus, Cairo, Aleppo, Bursa, Edirne and Istanbul; in Muharram 979 he became kazasker (chief military judge) of Anatolia. He fell ill at Edirne while in the retinue of Selim II and died on 6 Ramadan 979 (22 January 1572).", "0dedab6c"], "sanatci.onem": ["The author of the best-known Ottoman book on ethics written in Turkish; his work was long taught in the madrasas.", "874b4c3c"]},
"kirim-eminek-kefe-1475":{"oneri": ["What brought the Crimea to the Ottomans was not a pitched battle but a tribal bey whom the Genoese had had removed from office…", "d82dcdca"], "sebep.b": ["The struggle for the throne between Hacı Giray's sons Nur Devlet and Mengli Giray after his death (1466); the Genoese of Caffa stoking this struggle and having Eminek, the bey of the Shirin, removed from office", "9a2ffdd0"], "sonuc.b": ["The taking of Caffa and all the Genoese ports by the fleet of Gedik Ahmed Pasha, and Mengli Giray's acceptance of Ottoman protection", "6414d820"], "bag": ["According to the TDV article on the Crimea, the khanate's greatest weakness was that the real masters of the state were the hereditary tribal beys; every rivalry within the khan's family easily turned into a civil war. Toward 1475 Eminek, the tudun (governor) of Caffa and bey of the Shirin, was removed from his office at the insistence of the Genoese on the charge of having come to an understanding with the Ottomans. Eminek assembled the tribes, rose in revolt and forced Mengli Giray to flee. Mengli, who took refuge with the Genoese, was this time imprisoned by the Genoese, who had come to terms with Nur Devlet. Eminek, who also fell out with Nur Devlet, appealed to the sultan against the Genoese. According to the article on Caffa, in the talks between the Conqueror and Eminek it had been agreed that, if the operation succeeded, Caffa would be left to the Ottomans.", "c6daa065"], "metin": ["Seizing this opportunity, the Conqueror sent a fleet of 100 vessels to the Crimea under the command of Gedik Ahmed Pasha; together with Caffa the whole coastal strip was taken in Safar 880 / June 1475. The Pasha set the imprisoned Mengli Giray free; Mengli took the khanate back from Nur Devlet, a friend of the Genoese, and agreed to be a friend to the sultan's friend and an enemy to his enemy. A month and a half later he confirmed this position in a letter he wrote to the sultan. Here the TDV also corrects a widespread belief: contrary to what is supposed, there is no separate document of vassalage; the bond rests on the agreement made with Gedik Ahmed Pasha and on the khan's letter. On the coastal strip stretching from west to east, from Sarıkirman to Sarıgöl, the sanjak of Caffa was established, while the interior remained with the khanate. From then on Caffa became the main base of Ottoman policy in the north and was also used as a prince's sanjak: Mehmed, the son of Bayezid II, and Suleiman — the future Lawgiver — son of Selim the Grim, served there as sanjak governors.", "ed385b93"]},
"kitapcik-istanbul-fethi-1453":{"baslik": ["The Conquest of Istanbul — a booklet on the siege: walls, cannon, chain and ships", "9ba20f4d"], "kisa": ["Read the 54-day siege with the plans, weapons and positions of both sides together: walls approaching 22 km in length, the chain across the Golden Horn, an army pressing from four directions, ships brought down overland and the order of the final assault.", "dee23e5e"], "bolumler.0.baslik": ["The city and its walls", "abfc8a02"], "bolumler.0.metin": ["According to TDV, the total length of the land and sea walls approached 22 km. There were about 5.5 km of walls along the shore of the Golden Horn and about 8 km from Seraglio Point (Sarayburnu) along the Marmara shore to Yedikule; these walls on the seaward side were single, and in places rose straight out of the sea. Along the land walls ran moats filled with sea water. In some places the walls were double, but they had not been modernised to meet the conditions of the age; because of Byzantium's financial straits they had not been properly repaired for a long time. Even so, they are regarded as an obstacle that was hard to overcome. The great length of the walls was a disadvantage for the small number of defenders facing a large army, while their thickness and height made the defence easier.", "53c35915"], "bolumler.1.baslik": ["Preparations before the conquest: Boğazkesen and the Rumelian shore", "279c5be2"], "bolumler.1.metin": ["Mehmed II planned the siege in person; it is related that he read books on siege tactics and worked on plans. The military side of the preparations was carried out by the team of Şehâbeddin, Saruca and Zağanos Pashas. In April 1452 construction of a new fortress began on the Rumelian shore of the Bosphorus at its narrowest point; completed in August 1452, the Boğazkesen Fortress was to cut Istanbul off from the supply routes coming from the Black Sea, secure the passage of fleets between Anatolia and Rumelia, and serve when necessary as a base for the besieging army. Immediately after the fortress was finished, the sultan suddenly appeared before the walls with a force of 50,000 and carried out a reconnaissance lasting three days. The preparations stretched financial resources to the limit, and new taxes caused unrest in society.", "74deab46"], "bolumler.2.baslik": ["Strategy: not a long blockade but a swift capture", "a5c96931"], "bolumler.2.metin": ["According to TDV's reading, Mehmed II calculated that a prolonged blockade would give time to any help that might come from Europe, and he aimed to take the city as soon as possible. The fact that a city taken by the sword had a different status in Islamic tradition, giving the sultan full right of disposal over it, is also reckoned to have entered into the calculation. According to the same source, the conquest was also seen as a requirement of the tradition of holy war (gaza) and, because Istanbul lay in a position that prevented uninterrupted communication between Rumelia and Anatolia in the middle of the two, as essential for the future of the state. Two independent sources reporting the great council held in Edirne (Tâcîzâde Câfer Çelebi and Kritovoulos) also record the objection of the cautious viziers concerning the strength of the walls.", "bc81a3a9"], "bolumler.3.baslik": ["The Ottoman army: numbers and components", "392e7045"], "bolumler.3.metin": ["Contemporary sources put the Ottoman forces at between 100,000 and 300,000; TDV says that those who took part in the siege numbered around 100,000 or slightly more, and writes that it counts a quarter of these as Janissaries and standing army (kapıkulu) troops. The main strength consisted of the timar-holding soldiers of Anatolia and Rumelia; it assigns the most effective role in the fighting to the kapıkulu, who were infantry, and to the azebs who fought in the front line. Among those digging mines, it is stated, there were about 300 Serbian miners as well as German, Bohemian and Hungarian masters. The army also included volunteers, irregulars (başıbozuk), and sheikhs and dervishes of the Sufi lodges; scholars such as Akşemseddin, Molla Gürânî and Molla Hüsrev gave the sultan and the commanders moral support.", "c65c6497"], "bolumler.4.baslik": ["The defence: how many men, under whose command", "8656730f"], "bolumler.4.metin": ["According to contemporary Byzantine and Latin sources, the military force in the city was about 5,000, or 8-9,000 with the auxiliary units; the civilian population is estimated at about 30,000. Emperor Constantine XI set up his headquarters with his best units between Topkapı and Edirnekapı. The Genoese Giustiniani, who arrived in January 1453 with 700 men (400 of them Genoese), held the right wing and was later shifted to the centre. The Venetian bailo Girolamo Minotto guarded the sector of the Tekfur Palace, Teodor Caristino the line from Eğrikapı to the Xyloporta on the Golden Horn, and Filippo Contarini the stretch from the Marmara shore to the Golden Gate (Yaldızlıkapı); Alvisio Diedo took charge of the fleet, Loukas Notaras of the shore of the harbour on the Golden Horn, and Cardinal Isidore, with 200 men, of Seraglio Point. In the sector of the Tekfur Palace, the moat, which had filled up, was dug out again during preparations for the defence. Relying on Dolfin, TDV reports that although there were many Greeks in the city, those able to wield a sword were few and not skilled, whereas civilians played an important role in repairing the walls and supplying materials.", "5a765ccc"], "bolumler.5.baslik": ["The chain across the Golden Horn", "6c0024a7"], "bolumler.5.gun": ["1453-04-02", "ee0ef198"], "bolumler.5.metin": ["On 2 April the Venetian Bartolomeo Soligo stretched a chain across the Golden Horn and closed its entrance. The chain was drawn between the Tower of Saint Eugenius, near present-day Sirkeci, and the walls of Galata at Tophane (the Mumhâne point); behind the line Byzantine and Italian ships were ranged. The walls along the Golden Horn, however, are considered weaker than those on the Marmara and on the landward side; according to TDV, forcing the chain and entering the Golden Horn was one of the objectives of the Ottoman side.", "c2fdf153"], "bolumler.6.baslik": ["The Ottoman camp and the deployment of the corps", "ead2c086"], "bolumler.6.gun": ["1453-04-05", "f50efc9d"], "bolumler.6.metin": ["On 5 April Mehmed II pitched his imperial tent on the hill (Maltepe) to the left of the Lycus (Bayrampaşa) valley, and then moved the units a little forward to form a front. Zağanos Pasha, with the part of the army on the ridges of Beyoğlu and Kasımpaşa, kept the northern shores from Galata to Kâğıthane under pressure; it was decided that a bridge should be built between Hasköy and the land walls. Karaca Pasha, beylerbeyi of Rumelia, held the front along the walls from the Golden Horn to the area of the Tekfur Palace and Eğrikapı, while İshak Pasha, beylerbeyi of Anatolia, and Mahmud Pasha held the sector from Topkapı to the Golden Gate and the Marble Tower. In the centre, on the Edirnekapı-Topkapı line, were the sultan himself and the grand vizier; in this sector, considered the weakest part of the walls, the army's choicest soldiers and heavy cannon were concentrated. The fleet of Baltaoğlu Süleyman Bey prevented foreign ships from passing along the Marmara shores.", "9dd1d038"], "bolumler.7.baslik": ["Cannon and batteries", "d1ed0f69"], "bolumler.7.gun": ["1453-04-11", "751204b4"], "bolumler.7.metin": ["The most effective weapon of the Ottoman units before the walls was their cannon. TDV writes that the batteries were carefully sited and changed position as needed during the siege. According to Kritovoulos, the cannon stood in three emplacements in front of the walls at Edirnekapı, at the Tekfur Palace, between the Bayrampaşa stream and Topkapı, and at Eğrikapı; some Byzantine and Italian authors count fourteen batteries (according to some, nine), each with four large cannon. According to Barbaro, on 11 April the cannon were drawn up before the walls considered weak and placed at four positions. The breaches opened by the cannon were for the most part repaired immediately by the defenders. For the start of actual fighting TDV gives two dates: some researchers take 6 April as the basis, others 12 April (the firing of the great cannon).", "1d6f5163"], "bolumler.8.baslik": ["The first assault and the fleet put to the test", "8dce8f8e"], "bolumler.8.gun": ["1453-04-18", "6e11f9af"], "bolumler.8.metin": ["The fleet that assembled off Beşiktaş and Kabataş on 12 April is given in TDV's Istanbul article as 145 vessels (galleys, galleons and small craft); the article on Baltaoğlu Süleyman Bey, however, says about 400 ships, and the two articles diverge on this number. Between 12 and 18 April no serious assault was made apart from cannon fire. The first assault, launched on 18 April against the outer walls in the direction of the Bayrampaşa stream, came to nothing because of Giustiniani's effective defence; Barbaro puts the Turkish losses at 200.", "c45995c7"], "bolumler.9.baslik": ["The Genoese relief ships entered the Golden Horn", "4d59007c"], "bolumler.9.gun": ["1453-04-20", "e40a64ac"], "bolumler.9.metin": ["On 20 April three Genoese ships bringing provisions and aid appeared off Istanbul, together with a Byzantine transport that had joined them at the entrance to the Dardanelles. The Ottoman fleet met them off Yenikapı; a strong southwesterly wind prevented the oared Ottoman ships from manoeuvring, while the high-sided Genoese vessels easily broke through the blockade and entered the Golden Horn. This failure broke the army's morale and caused unrest among the ulema and the commanders: Akşemseddin openly reproached the sultan in a letter; while Çandarlı Halil Pasha favoured accepting the Byzantine peace offer, Akşemseddin, Zağanos Pasha and Molla Gürânî argued for continuing the siege. According to TDV, Mehmed II hesitated, but in the end decided to carry on the siege.", "ea3efc6a"], "bolumler.10.baslik": ["The ships brought down into the Golden Horn overland", "127c84e8"], "bolumler.10.gun": ["1453-04-21", "e50a663f"], "bolumler.10.metin": ["After the decision to continue the siege, the plan was put into effect to haul part of the fleet overland and launch it into the Golden Horn, in order to put pressure on the walls along the Golden Horn, which were weaker than the land walls. TDV supposes that this was not done all at once but required long preparation, and that the ships were drawn up with slipways and machines (‘cerr-i eskāl’ in Tursun Bey, the art of moving heavy weights), lined up on the ridges of Kasımpaşa and suddenly launched at night. On the night of 21 to 22 April some sixty to seventy ships, large and small, suddenly appeared in the Golden Horn; this both restored the soldiers' morale and caused great astonishment among the Byzantines. The route is disputed: some researchers argue that the ships were taken from Tophane by way of Humbaracıbaşı Yokuşu and Asmalı Mescid to Kasımpaşa, others that they were brought down from Dolmabahçe or Beşiktaş by way of Harbiye to Kasımpaşa or opposite Eyüp; the expression common to the Ottoman sources is ‘over / behind Galata’. The artillery of Zağanos Pasha on the ridges of Kasımpaşa and Okmeydanı fired so as to prevent the Byzantine and Italian ships in the Golden Horn from attacking; two ships of the Byzantine fleet were sunk by cannon fire. The bridge built close to the walls, which made it possible to cross from one side to the other, was also under the protection of this fleet.", "d283803c"], "bolumler.11.baslik": ["The May assaults, the tunnels, the siege tower", "71144ceb"], "bolumler.11.gun": ["1453-05-06", "83b0bcd5"], "bolumler.11.metin": ["On 6 May the siege intensified again with a general assault on the walls at the Bayrampaşa stream; on 12 May the attack was pressed between the Tekfur Palace and Edirnekapı, against the Edirnekapı-Eğrikapı walls that had been brought down by cannon fire, but no result was achieved. On 16 May the great tunnel that had been extended beneath the walls into the city was discovered by the Byzantines and collapsed with a countermine; later tunnels met the same fate. On 18 May an assault with a large siege tower failed, and the tower was burned and destroyed. According to Barbaro, a hide-covered passage had been made between the tower and the camp, and soldiers could advance to the walls without losses; those inside it had filled the moat in front of the walls and piled up the earth along the walls.", "6ee3e273"], "bolumler.12.baslik": ["The final offer of surrender and the decision for a general assault", "f7016b0f"], "bolumler.12.gun": ["1453-05-23", "0eab8778"], "bolumler.12.metin": ["In the fourth week of May news spread through the army that the Hungarians and a Crusader fleet had set out; the sultan's attempts to take the city peacefully were creating impatience and anxiety among the soldiers. On 23 May Mehmed II sent İsfendiyaroğlu İsmail Bey as envoy and announced that, if the city surrendered, the lives and property of its people would not be touched, the emperor would be given the Despotate of the Morea, and whoever wished could go wherever he liked; the reply was to the effect that, if the siege were lifted, there could be vassalage and tribute as before. In the council of war Zağanos Pasha opposed Çandarlı Halil Pasha's call for a compromise; the decision for a general attack was taken, and the choice of the day was left to Zağanos Pasha.", "72e97526"], "bolumler.13.baslik": ["The plan of the final assault and the crack in the defence", "f6764100"], "bolumler.13.metin": ["In the two or three days after 23 May great preparations were made for the final assault. The plan was this: Zağanos Pasha would attack the walls along the Golden Horn; Karaca Pasha, on his right, the area from the centre to the Golden Horn walls; İshak and Mahmud Pashas the sector extending from the centre to the Marmara shore; and the central forces, with which the sultan was, the walls above the Bayrampaşa stream; the centre of gravity would be between Topkapı and Edirnekapı. When the news reached the Byzantines, Giustiniani undertook to have the cannon under Notaras's command moved to the central sector; Notaras objected, thinking that the attack would come against the walls along the Golden Horn, but by the emperor's order the cannon were sent where Giustiniani wanted them. The population, Greek and Italian, Orthodox and Catholic, attended religious ceremonies together in Hagia Sophia; the emperor sent the commanders to their posts on the outer walls and had the gates of the inner walls closed behind them.", "329f7d7b"], "bolumler.14.baslik": ["The night of candles: 28/29 May", "b4189b68"], "bolumler.14.gun": ["1453-05-28", "09ab7f99"], "bolumler.14.metin": ["On the night of 28 to 29 May the Ottoman army held festivities and lit up the surroundings with candles; towards midnight the walls, which had been as bright as day, suddenly went dark, and the army completed its final preparations. TDV writes that these lights struck terror into the people of Byzantium.", "a3f4bfbf"], "bolumler.15.baslik": ["29 May: assault on all fronts", "ae9d0a52"], "bolumler.15.gun": ["1453-05-29", "08ab7e06"], "bolumler.15.metin": ["The final attack began on all fronts at once before dawn on Tuesday, 29 May. The fleet blockaded the Marmara walls as far as Samatya and in places landed azeb troops; between Edirnekapı and Topkapı, along the Bayrampaşa valley, the walls, which had been knocked down and in places were being repaired, were attacked in great strength. According to Barbaro, three successive assaults were made; an hour before daybreak the great cannon began firing at the repaired walls. 300 Turkish soldiers got over the second walls but, not receiving support in time, were driven back; immediately afterwards a large force got over the second walls (Barbaro writes 70,000; TDV says this figure is exaggerated). When Giustiniani was wounded he was carried to his ship, and the people, supposing that the city had fallen, rushed in panic to the harbour on the Golden Horn. The attacking columns at the breach at Topkapı and between Edirnekapı and the Canbazhâne Gate joined up and scattered the defenders at three points. According to Barbaro, the fleet under the command of Zağanos Pasha landed troops at Fener. Emperor Constantine XI, who had gone to fill the place left by Giustiniani, died fighting.", "4d4288ef"], "bolumler.16.baslik": ["The accounts diverge: who entered first, how the emperor died", "866bb41b"], "bolumler.16.gun": ["1453-05-29", "08ab7e06"], "bolumler.16.metin": ["TDV shows that the sources diverge on this point. Phrantzes (Sphrantzes) writes that a Janissary named Hasan climbed to the top at a ruined spot in the wall, got over the walls with some thirty Janissaries beside him and died from the blow of a stone, and that the emperor was wounded while fighting and died. Barbaro records that no information could be obtained about the emperor's death and that there were reports that he had killed himself by strangling. Doukas writes that a group of fifty entered through a gate that had been ‘left open’, while Zorzi Dolfin writes that the great cannon, moved opposite Topkapı, brought down the tower and filled the moat, and that the emperor was crushed to death. Among the Ottoman sources, Tursun Bey states that the emperor's horse stumbled and fell and that he was killed by an azeb who did not know who he was, while Kemalpaşazâde speaks of the entry through the breach at Topkapı and of the killing by an azeb of the emperor as he fled towards Yedikule, and notes that there are many accounts of this. On those who first mounted the walls the Ottoman sources generally give no information; only Bihiştî writes that the first to enter the city was his father, Süleyman Bey. Selâhattin Tansel argues that the first entry into the city may have been through the weak walls on the side of the Golden Horn.", "5ded8195"], "bolumler.17.baslik": ["What could not be found for this booklet", "bf830a8f"], "bolumler.17.metin": ["The following were searched for in TDV's Istanbul and Mehmed II articles and not found, and have therefore not been written: any statement that Greek fire was used in the siege; the name of the cannon founder Orban/Urban and the dimensions of the ‘Şâhî’ cannon; a day-by-day account of the remaining days of the siege (the days not included in the detailed chronology). The primary basis for the latter is the diary that Barbaro kept day by day (Diary of the Siege of Constantinople, Kostantıniyye Muhâsarası Ruznâmesi; TDV: barbaro-niccolo), and no online text of it was found. As for the length of the siege, TDV's Mehmed II article says fifty-four days, between 26 Rabi al-Awwal and 20 Jumada al-Awwal 857 (6 April - 29 May 1453).", "b3c1d646"]}
});
